      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>RANDOM READINGS.</head>
        <p>RANDOM READINGS.</p>
        <p>CONCERNING WATCHES,</p>
        <p>As early as Shakespeare's day, says Ilacaver Percival in the Country Home, watches were well known., and Queen Elizabeth possessed. many of them. Indeed, if one-quarter of those that are said to have belonged to her were really hers, she must have had enough to stock a fair-sized watchmaker's shop. Sixteenth-cenitury watches are often very com- plicated in their mechanism, and some curious specimens are found wirh their works entirely of iron. The cases were often made of the most singular shapes, such as skulls, vases, crucifixes, halls, animals, and so on. Instead,.of glass to protect the dial they had perforated covers through which the figures could be seen. Glasses, however, were introduced early in the seven- leetith century, at first merely pieces of thick flat sheet; then they went to the other extreme, and are often extremely rounded, like half a glass During the seventeenth and -eat-1y eighteenth vetkuries French watches were most ,</p>
        <p>EXQUISITELY AND DELICATELY PAINTED</p>
        <p>with enamel pictures, an art akin to china paint- ing, as the gold was first covered with opaque na me 1 , and the scenes or figures were painted in fusible Colours, which were then fired at a high teinperature in muffle furnaces.- In ;the eighteenth century chiselled gold oases rhased with scenes and groups of flowers were -fashionable, the gold being often of differently- tinted alloys, -which' caused the work to be de- wribed as a quatre couleurs. Diamonds often -surrounded' faces, and were set in the deli- zately pierced and worked fingers. In simpler forms the metal was repousse (that is, beaten up Ddt, of sheet metal), and sometimes cut steel was substituted for the diamonds, but, this is, more towards the end of ;the century, when 'money was, perhaps, getting scarcer. The minute-wheel and hands are.said to have been invented by a London watchmaker named Quare, ithe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>INVENTOR OF REPEATING WATCHES,</head>
        <p>INVENTOR OF REPEATING WATCHES,</p>
        <p>which had an ingenious device for making the nour strike when required, so that thq time :oald be ascertained at night or without remov- ing the watch from the pocket. The way of wearing watches has differed ac- cording to the fashions of the period. Queen Elizabeth received from The Earl of Leicester a present. of a watch set in a bracelet, Mt the more general plan was to wear them on .chain or -ribbon round the neck as medals or pendants were worn. However, they were also very gene- -rally carried in the .pocko, a practice which seems to have become the most usual about 1630. One of the earliest fob chains known is now in the British Museum; it is attached to the watch said to have belonged to Oliver Cromwell, who runs Queen Elizabeth hard in the number of watches reputed to lase .belonged to him. In the eighteenth century a very favourite way of carrying the watch was at the side on a chate- laine, where it shared the honours with other useful and ornamental trifles. Later on in the century it became the fashion to wear several watches set in pins, rings, and buttons. Sometimes one was worn at each side, or a real watch on one hip was balanced by an iinitation one on the other, which, though the back was similar to the watch, was in reality a com pass , pin-cushion, or other device.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WHAT IS GERMAN SILVER?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS GERMAN SILVER?</p>
        <p>German silver is a white alloy. Everybody knows that. But in answering the above ques- tion a writer in "Things Technical" tells us that it -consists of nickel, copper, and zinc, the best quality being made up of four parts zinc. This quality, however, is the most difficult to work. It. is known under probably a greater number of names than any other alloy. In England and the United States it is known as nickel silver, packfong, white copper, silveroid, Nevada silver, and electrum. It is very ductile, and can be hammered, rolled, stamped, and drawn. At the same time it possesscs the properties of being bard, tough, and not easily corroded. Iron, lead, and tin ,are frequently found as im- purities in German silver. • Iron alloys perfectly lirith it, increasing the strength, hardness, and elasticity of the alloy, at the same time making it whiter. Tin, on the other hand, does not enter into the alloy in the same way as iron, but forms a mixture which renders it brittle and unfit ,for rolling. Even when present in small quantities, it GIVES THE ALLOY A YELLOWISH COLOUR.</p>
        <p>Lead does not alloy with German silver, but separates out as metallic lead. When the alloy in to be cast and subsequently turned upon a lathe, from 2 to 3 per cent. of lead is added, as it enables the lathe to be worked at a higher speed and gives a better finish to the work. German silver is made by melting the metals in graphite crucibles. In order to produce a 2Tior e homogeneous alloy, and to lessen the oxida- tion of the zinc, the separate metals are not melted together; but alloys of copper and nickel, and copper and zinc, are used, and, shortly, be- fore pouring, a small quantity of zinc is added to overcome the effect of the volatilisation of the zinc and to ensure a thorough deoxidation of the alloy. The metal is cast in iron moulds previously heated and coated with oil or mixtures of char- coal and oil or rosin and oil. The temperature of casting is a very important point. If the tew-i- --perature is to high the metal will be full of blowholes, and ,</p>
        <p>WILL CRACK, IN ROLLING. / If, en the other hand, it is cast at too ow a tem- perature, it tends ,to solidify as it touches the mould, with the result that there is imperfect cohesion of the mettil. As the melting-point of German silver is higher than that of brass, it must be cast rapidly, or the crucible must be re- turned to the furnace and be reheated. :If the ..crtal is to be rolled into -sheets moulds from loin. to 18in. long, 4in. to sin. wide, and lin. to liin. thick arc used. When intended for wire the moulds are 4Lft. to sft. long, 34in. wide, and 14in. thick. German silver, besides being used for many ecientifie instcuments, is frequently used as a base in cheap grades of silver-plated ware. Ger- man silver, containing 60 per cent. copper, 14 per cent. nickel, 24 per cent. zinc, and 1 to 2 per cent. tungsten. is used, under the name of plati- mead, for electrical resistances. It has also been alloyed with silver for jewellery and subsidiary coinage. Aluminium and magnesium have also been alloyed with German silver; ,but, owing to the high cost, these alloys have never found extended uses. German silver can be readily soldered. The usual composition of German silver solder is: Copper 47 per cent., nickel 11 per cwt., and zinc 42 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DANG.c.iooS TREE</head>
        <p>A DANG.c.iooS TREE</p>
        <p>Everywhere the manchinecl bears the reputa- tion of being a most dangerous tree, in the shade whereof i is never safe to rest. This evil repu- tation has its origin in the poisonous qualities of the sap and fruit of a tree of this kind found in Africa—the arborescent ettphorbia. This tree, says an Ameriean paper, has a mag- nificent but most peculiar appearance, and the thickness of its foliage, which wholly excludes the sun, seems to invite the traveller to rest be- neath its branches. The negroes have a Way of taking advantage of the delightfully cool shade, and at the same time avoiding the danger from the poisonous droppings of the tree. They erect a thatched roof below the lowest branches, and then repose in peace and security. Tremaux, the French explorer, has perhaps given the best account of these arboreseent ieuphorbias. While taking a view of Cacane, he asked one of his negroes who stood near him to scat himself under a great enphorbia that stood in the foreground. At first the native hesi- tated; then after a while he decided to yield, btr. not without raising his eyes many times in ap- prehension towards the branches of the tree. The Frenchman was about to climb upon a -rock to break off a branch, but. the negro, seeing iim approach, fled 'in terror from the shade of the deadly! tree, gesticulating wildly and giving utterance to terms the foreigner could not under- stand. The man's gestures, however, and a few Arabic,words uttered by one of the bystanders,</p>
        <p>"DO 17017 MEAN TO DIE?" made the explorer understand that in touching the tree he was running a serious risk:</p>
        <p>But the thing was done, and the broken branch in the Frenchman's hand. Immediately a milky fluid flowed forth, in much greater quantity than he could have imagined from what he knew of these plants in other 'countries, covering his ,clothes and penetrating even to hi ,s skin. The features and ge,tures of the negroes ex- premed their fear. They made the traveller linderstand that if the white juice touched one ,of the numerous wounds which he at that time had on his body he would die, and that it was dangerous _even to let it touch the skin.</p>
        <p>It is with this juice that certain of the African tribes poison their weapons in order to make the wounds inflicted thereby mortal. , They first thicken it until it acquires the consistency of paste. Then they dip in it the .points or blades .of the weapons they wish to poison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>"COCAINE CREEPER."</p>
        <p>Victims of the cocaine habit suffer from various affections. One is the so-called "coke-bug," an imaginary insect under the skin of the hand, and there is a disease of the nostrils, commonly called "Cocaine Cree- per," which has puzzled maiiy doctors. Some have diagnosed it as a symptom of the specific complaint, but Dr. Bisserie, of Paris, at one time doctor-in-chief at the Rothschild Hospital, state's • that it is merely a tempo- rary affliction which ceases with the habit. Apparently the Cocaine powder prevents the tissues of the nostrils from performing their proper functions, and so causes . the matter to clog. Occasionally the, poison thus set in will eat through the nose. Poisoning is often set up by the habit cocaine fiends have of using knives, hairpins, &amp;c., to free their nostrils from the coagulated matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LIFE IN THE DEEP SEAS.</head>
        <p>LIFE IN THE DEEP SEAS.</p>
        <p>Sir John Murray, lecturing at the ,Royal Institution on. "Life in the Great Oceans," described the methods adopted to insure that the catches in the trawl were representa- tive of the different depths examined, and dhowed how marine plants and animals .wers Adapted to their special environment. Refer- rinrto the presence of bacteria,.:he showed that life could not exist in the sea unless the bacteria did their work on the floors of the ocean. In the war.mer waters the processes of life were hastened, so 'that while in the Arctic and' Antarctic many individuals were found but few species, in the Tropics many species were found but few'individuals. In the Saragossa Sea the fishes showed colour adaptation, and it was there that the youngest of the larvw of the eel were found. A com- mon method of protective coloration was for the backs of fish to be black and the sides silvered, this making them almost invisible at a considerable depth. When the limit of light was reached at about 500 metres the fish were red coloured, but as they reached the lower depths the colours became more sombre. There were various modifications in fishes to facilitate floating in the warmer and less vicious waters, and some fish which had developed air bladders had a tendency 'to fall upwards from the lowest depths. At the flaottom of the sea the vast majority of ani- mals were - td-eaters, and in fact all strati- fied rocks had passed through the intestines of animals.</p>
        <p>GETTING A LINE ON THE NEWCOMERS. "Recently," says a Washington man who had been spending a holiday at home in a suburb of the national capital, "I was afforded an amusing instance of the artlessness of childhood. "Louise, one little girlA on the next porch, was evidently engaged in. cultivating the acquaintance of another little girl, a newcomer in the neighbourhood. The second little girl was romping ou the porch of the third house from me. ! "'What's your name?' shouted Louise. "'Elizabeth.' was the answer shouted back. 'What's yours?' "'Louise. The name of the people that lived in that house before you was Berry.' "'Our name is Parker.' "'Ours is. Taylor. • You didn't know the Berrys, did you? "'They were something awful for borrowing. They used to be sending Over to our house all the time for everything you could imagine. Your folks don't do that, do 44:4 No./ "Whereupon Louise turned and shouted up to her mother, at the second-storey window: "'She says they don't, mamma.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ANIMALS THAT NEVER DIE.</head>
        <p>ANIMALS THAT NEVER DIE.</p>
        <p>Probably no field of scientific investiga- tion is more fascinating than that which deals with the length of life of unicellular forms of protoplasm. Weismann's theory that death did not necessarily follow life, but arose through the process of natural selec- tion, has in a measure been proved to be true, at least with regard to the very lowest forms of life. The paramoecium or "slipper animalcule," for instance, is able to live on indefinitely and is endowed with</p>
        <p>POTENTIAL IMMORTALITY.</p>
        <p>Why these minute animals are thus endowed iith a deathless existence while sentient man 3 doomed to face the terrors of the grave is ade clear by a report from the Maine Agri- cultural Experiment Station written by Pro- fessor M. D. Pearl. "The paramoecium is an organism which has long been used by biologists in a great variety of experiments, and is consequently very well known. A. ctilture of these animals kept' in a laboratory shows that the indivi- duals at intervals divide. Then there will come a period when many will unite with each other. Microscopic examination shows that in this conjugation there is an exchange of nuclear material so that each animal re- ceives into its body or cell a part of the ani- mal with Which it mated. Conjugation always seems to occur when the culture is in a run-down condition ; that is, when the en- vironment in which the paramoecia are living becomes more or less unsuitable to their existenee. Cannot paramoecia</p>
        <p>LIVE ON FOR EVER, then, merely dividing when the cell has at- tained its full size? In other words, are not the lowest forms of life potentially immortal, as Weismann claimed them to be? Professor L. L. Woodruff, of Yale University, has proved that Weismann was right in his con- tention. He :found that under favourable conditions--when the organisms were not too crowded, and- the culture medium was changed frequently and kept free from bac- teria—paramoecium would live apparently indefinitely. Five years ago he started em experiment with a single individual in an en- vironment best suited to produce healthy, vigorous aniinals. Since that time this single cell of protoplasm has passed through 3.500 generations without at any one time passing through the stage of conjugation,. And as the Animals in the line of descent be- come older they show</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NO DIMINISHING</head>
        <p>NO DIMINISHING</p>
        <p>of their vital powers. In fact. they are in a more vigorous condition to-day than they were five years ago. "No doubt all of us have a lurking desire to live as long as paramoecium, and wonder why it is that if such simple forms can appa- rently live on for ever death must come to higher animals. If we could take on the simple structure and vital economy of the unicellular organisms we would stand a chance for continuous life, but just because of our specialisation there cannot be a per- petual existence. When one part gives out. other parts must 'suffer, and finally the en.- aro system succumbs. . The penalty 'of our highly-developed organisation is death.".</p>
        <p>HORIZONTAL WIRELESS ANTENNZE. Kiebitz finds that wireless waves can lae re- ceived with surprisingly good results by using an antenna made 'up of wires stretched along at ,a•short distance from the. ground, mounting the receiving devices at .the centre of the antenna. For instance, upon a large fiat area near Delzig he stretched -several wires between pairs of posts at about 3ft. from the ground. However, a combination is made br stretching one antenna from north to south, then a. second from east to west so as to cross the first .one at right angles and in the middle. He also ran a third antenna across the middle point and directed N.E. and S.W., that is at angles of 45deg. This latter antenna was about I,oooft. long and lay in the direction of the Schonberg station (40 miles off), and also in• the direction of the Eiffel Tower, 500 miles away. _awl the German .post of Swinemunde (140 miles). In this way he as able to pick up these two German posts, as well as the Norddeich post, 250 miles distant. Signals 'could be heard very well from the Eiffel Tower, and he concludes that .an antenna of this length is equivalent to a vertical one of 401 t. height. Poldhu was also beard, and fie could receive from Clifden and Glace Bay by using a 4,000 ft. wire 31. from the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WISE AND OTHERWISE</head>
        <p>WISE AND OTHERWISE</p>
        <p>Genius will out—often at the elbows. Hard luck, says a wise wit, is often due to an effort to avoid hard work. Travel broadens some, but others can remain at home and get fat, says a. wag. . Money can never buy happiness, we are told. But think of the discomfort it can buy off. "You can't. stuff me," as the hunger-striking suffragette remarked to the prison authorities. He was a wise wit who wrote that many a bad end results from trying to he a "good fellow." The race is not always to the swift, but. a fast man can usually run most quickly„ through a fortune.</p>
        <p>He was an embittered cynic who wrote that ew people are disappointed in love until after</p>
        <p>ma rna.ge,</p>
        <p>Marrying for money reveah a poor spirit. said a speaker the other day. It certainly indicates a lack of " mettle."</p>
        <p>A barrister must have considerable standing at the Bar, writes a young lawyer, before he is in- vited to sit on the Bench. "I daresay," declares a woman writer, "a girt might to marry an economic man, but it's just awful being engaged to one." • "No. we don't call this the nursery " harassed parent to the visitor; speak of it as the ' bawl-room ' ! ' She: "I love Ireland. I think I was meant for an Irishwoman." Pat: "On the contrary, I think you were .meant for an Irishman." "Can't you give me an order?" pleaded the persistent commercial traveller. Certainly," replied busy Mr. Jones. "Get out of my house." It is better to charge an item twice than to forget to charge it at all, says a business journal. This expounds the real principle of double entry! Daughter (singing to her own accompani- ment): 't I'm afloat! I'm afloat ! " Father: " You'll jolly soon be capsized by .such a squall as that! "</p>
        <p>said the wo generally</p>
        <p>"Bliggins is constantly talking about his dis tinguished ancestors." "Yes," replied Miss Cayenne, "most of them are dead and can't re- sent the familiarity." He,: "A woman would always rather talk than listen." She: ',Not always." He: " When not?" She: "If the man she wanted was pop- pmg the question." "If you don't stop nagging me, Emily, I shall shoot myself this very minute." "Yes, that's just like you, when You know how nervous I am when I hear a shot.'" Maiden Fair: "Oh, it always inakeS my head swim to go on the water!" ',Facetious Sailor: "No danger of drowning, then, mom, if you should fall overboard." Joker: "If Mrs. .Bigger had a baby, who would be the bigger?" Smart: "Mrs. Bigger, of course." Joker: "No, the baby; because it would be a little bigger." - Magistrate: "The other witness says you were both playing a friendly game." Isaac Moses: "A freendly game, a freendly game. I don't tink so. I lost a sovereign." A stranger to the Law Courts, hearing a Judge addre....s. another Judge as "brother," expressed his surprise. "Oh! ' said one present, "they are brothers—brothers-in-law." A little girl, on hearing her mother speaking to her father for the first time on the telephone, sobbed; "Oh, mother, how are we going to get papa out of a hole like that?" Gushing Young Thing: "Oh, do- you know, I'm simply mad to go on the, stage!" -Distin- guished Actor: "Ye-es, I should think you would be, my dear young lady!" - TommY: "Look at the lost ball I found on the links, father!" Father: "Are you sure it was a lost ball?" Tommy: "Rather! I saw the man and his caddy looking' for it." "Did the Medicine I sent you do your rheu- matism any good?" queried the doctor. "Shure, an' it did, sorr," returned the patient. "I blame it for the good night's Test I had." A self-made man may be no particular help to his fellow-men, but, -says a cynic, a self-made woman is a. perfect mine of infermation about hair dyes, face creams, and nose powders! Jones: "Do you give your wife an allow- ance?" Smith: "Yes." Jones: "How much do you allow her?" .firnith: "Don't you think it' rather impertinent to ask what my salary is?" Haughty Lady- (who has purchased a stamp): "Must I put it on myself?" Clerk (very politely): "Not necessarily, ma'am. It will prob- ably aCcomplish more if you put it ‘on the letter." "John, dear, I wish you would taste this milk and see if it's perfectly sweet." "What for?" "Becaust if it's the least bit sour I don't want to give it to Fido. It isn't good. for him." "And what do you usually drink?" asked t},lie panel doctor of. the Irish labourer seeking his advice. "Shure, doctlior," - said the patient, brightening up, "whatever ye're takin' yourself, thank ye."</p>
        <p>"Come and dine with 'me to-night." said Bounderby. "Sorry," replied his friend, " but I'm going to see • " "That's all right, chap. The More the merrier. Bring him with you."</p>
        <p>"There is a visitor to see you," said the wife to her husband, who was ailing. "Oh," he growled, "I'm too sick to see anybody." "But it's our pastor." "Thunder! I'm sick enough to see him." She: "You puckered uP your lips so that I thought you were going to kiss me." He: "No; I got some grit in my mouth." She: "Well, for goodness' sake, swallow it. You need it in your system." "Say. !Jlobbie, your mother's calling you." "Oh, shci.: doesn't want me very badly." "But she has called you seven times." " Aw, that's all right. She hasn't started calling me 'Robert' yet." "Do not insist on being worshipped on a pedestal" is Ella Wheeler Wilcox's advice to women. "It tires a man's neck to be for ever gazing upward, and statues -are less agreeable companions than human beings." The "University Correspondent" quotes the following -getters from a 'Cardiff schoolmaster's letter-box: "Dear Sir,--.-Please do not let my son John. learn Welsh to-day: his throat is so bad he can hardly speak English." Kindly Visitor: "What's your name, my boy?" ,Hotel.. Page -Boy: "They • call me Bil- liard Cue. sir." Kindly Visitor: "Whatever for?" Hotel Page- Boy: "Because I work so much better with a good tip. sir." Jimmy: "Just fancy. I know a man who lived on water for twenty-five days." Timmy: "I knew a man who lived for, forty-five years on water." Jimmy: "Oh! who was he?" Tommy: "Why, he was an old sea captain." Net "You are quite comfortable, dear?" She: "Yes, love." He: "You are not affected by the draught?" She: "No. dearest." He: "Aro the cushions soft, my lamb?" She: "Yes, my ownest own." Hei." Then change, seats with me." "I made an awful mistake the other day," said the modern Bob Sawyer, sadly: "Operated on a - man for appendiCitis, and he didn't have what I thought-" "He hadn't appendicitis at all, then?" "Oh, yes, lie had. appendicitis all right, but he didn't have any money! ' Having saved, a nice little sum. Jones decided to spend the. holidays at Blackpool. One morn- ing he approached a boatman with the intention of having a sail. "You can't. go out to-day." said the old salt, "there's a swell on the sea." "Swell or no swell," Jones exclaimed, "I've as much right to be on as him." "Mrs. Hauskeep: "Yes, my new girl formerly worked' for. Mrs. De Style. She claims she left there of her own accord, but I think she was dis- charged." Mrs Kaul: "What makes you think so?" Mrs. Hanskeep: "I judge from certain things she let fall since she's keen here." Mrs. Kaul: "What were they?" -r Mrs. Hauskeep: "Dishes." It was the old story. Mrs. Newlywed had quarrelled for the first time with her hubby, and, naturally, she flew to mother for advice. " Oh, mother," s'le wailed. "I've just had a quarrel with- George." "Well, you must forget and forgive." " Oh. .I can never forgive: I was in the wrong, you see." " Well, then, there is nothing for it only to demand an apology." A woman, for whose husband the doctor had ordered a black draught., came to' the surgery in the middle of the night' wringing her hands. "Oh, doctor. George is much worse." she fal- tercd. "Did you give him 'that 'black draught I ordered?" "No, sir; I couldn't find a Hoek draught. but I gave him a double-six domino, and he's been going steadily downhill ever since." A leetti.er was annoyed by a Man in the audi- ence who insisted on rising and asking questions. "Sit down, you ass!" said a second man,, jump- ing, up.. "Sit down, you, .too," cried a third man; "you "are both asses." "There- seems to be plenty of asses about to-night," commented the lecturer; "but for heaven's sake let, us hear, one at a time." "Well, you go on, then," said the first man, resuming. his seat. "Tommy," exclaimed the mother, reproach- fully, -" you've • been - • fighting again!" "1 couldn't help it, mamma. That, Bobby Jones in- sulted me" replied the boy. 'That was no reason for fighting," said the mother. "You should have remembered that a soft answer turneth away wrath, and given him a soft an- swer." " I 'did," replied the boy; "I hit him with a chunk of mud."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>IN THE COURTS</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS</p>
        <p>' GOLF HOUSE ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>Haydcck Charge to be Investigated at the</p>
        <p>Sessions.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Wednesday' before Mr. William Fairclough, James Newa:l, 65, Renshaw-street, Patricroft, and Arthur Lowe, in, Moss-lane, Harpurhey, were charged with breaking and entering the Haydock Park Golf Club house, between the 16th and 17th July, and stealing 15s. in money, flve bottles of whisky and a clothes brush, of the total value of 375. Sergeant John Summerville, of Leigh, said that on Monday, July 21st, be was conveying the prisoner Lowe from Eccles to Leigh on another 0-arge. Lowe raid : "Which job are you taking me for ? " He (witness) cautioned prisoner and be said, " The one at Kenyon, or the one at Haydock ? " Witness replied "I he one at Kenyon." Prisoner said that Newell and he got four bottles of whisky from Haydock and carried them away in a bag. They drank the whisky with some "pals," and left the brush at Newell's house. Samuel Foden Moss, school teacher, 3C6, Earle-street, Earlestown, said he was in the golf house on the Wednesday night. He left everything secure. He was certain the brush (produced) belonged to the club. William Leigh Hall, groundsman, Woodlands Cottage, deposed to leaving the whisky safe in the lockers. The next morning the front door had been forced and the staple which the lock shot into was broken. On going ins,cle the pavilion he found everything in a disorderly state. He examined the locker and found that the lock had been broken and that the whisky awl money were missing. The brush had disappeared from the lavatory. Two other lockers had been forced open, but, so far as he knew, there was nothing else missing. P.C. Howarth, of Haydock,said that from information received he went to Patricroft and heard that Newell had been taken into custody by the Leigh police on July 17th. He found, from the description given him, that Newell was one of the men who had been seen near the Haydock Park Golf Club at 5 a.m. on the 17th inst. At 4 p.m. on tbe 18th inst., be visited Newell's house, in company with other officers, and found the brush (produced) on the sideboard in the kitchen. On July 22nd he received the prisoners into cestody from the Leigh police, and conveyed them to Haydock Police Station. Newell said be knew nothing at all abcut the affair. Lowe pleaded not guilty. Prisoners were committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>"MANY WEARY YEARS."</head>
        <p>"MANY WEARY YEARS."</p>
        <p>Prisoner's Extraordinary Story of Wounding</p>
        <p>Episode.</p>
        <p>A serious wounding case was beard at the Warrington Police Court on naturday, ',gore Mr. K. T. Fairclough (in the chair) and other magistrates. Henry Darlington was charged with unlawfully wounding Thomas Donnellan, a farm worker, by striking him on the bead with a pick shaft on July 6th. The Chief Constable raid that the case bad been adjourned twice in order that the prosecutor, who had been in the Workhouse Infirmary, might be able to attend. Prior to the assault, Donnellan resided at Cooper's farm, Culcheth. He was returning to Ireland and got lost on Central Station, Warrington, about 12 p.m. on July sth. He %talked along the line in the direction of Froghall Bridge and then sat down outside a cabin. Prisoner came from behind the cabin, and after some conversation, he asked Donnellan if he had any money, and upon the man replying in the affirmative he said, "Well, give us twopence." Prosecutor refused. The prisoner went behind the cabin and returned with a pick shaft in his hand. After walking up and down for nearly an hour prisoner 'admitted that he struck the matt over the bead and left him practically insensible to die, as he thought. Prisoner informed a man in Warrington of what he had done. P.C. Gibson went to the spot and found a great deal of blood near the cabin. P.C. Butterworth, of Padgete, afterwards encountered the prosecutor wandering about in a bleeding and dazed condition, not knowing what he was doing. Dr. Bennett put two ye stitches in the wounds and afterwards Donnellan was taken to the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary, where he had been for a fortnight. Prisoner had written a letter to his relatives, the following being an extract :</p>
        <p>Dear Relatives, I want you to try and bear this bad news as well as you can. I have committed a bad crime in Warrington. I quarrelled with a man on the fixtb. We bad a terrible fight and he came off second best. I have been remanded twice and there is not the slightest doubt but what I have almost killed the man. Another blow from the iron bar would have killed him. We got fighting and I know it meant life or death for either of us. I struck him six times across the head and I left him moaning and bleeding. I know if he dies that it means the rope for me, and I am quite ready for it if the worst happens. If he gets better, I am expecting three or four years at Dartmoor. So, dear relatives, goodbye for many weary years. I ask no mercy; I am quite ready when they give me the long drop. Dr. Alex Hodgkinson, medical officer at the Work- house, said that when he saw prosecutor he was suffer- ing from contused wounds on the scalp. The wounds were torn and lacerated and it must have required con- siderable violence to inflict them. He was also suffer- ing from slight concussion of the brain. After further evidence had been taken, prisoner stated that he did not ask the prosecutor for money. If he had wanted money it would have been quite easy for him to obtain some. It bad been a fair tight and if he had not "given " it prosecutor, Donnellan would have " given" it him. Prisoner was committed to take his trial at the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MOTORISTS FINED.</head>
        <p>MOTORISTS FINED.</p>
        <p>Police Court Sequel to Taxi-Cab Accident.</p>
        <p>A QUESTION OF SPEED.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. W. Roberts (presiding) and other magistrates, William Henry Robinson, The Mount, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, was summoned for having driven a motor car on the Knutsford-road at a speed dangerous to the public, having regard to all the circumstances, on July 7th.</p>
        <p>. P.C. Dixon said that at 9-5 p.m. the car was going in the direction of St. Mary-street at a speed of not less thin 25 miles per hour. He (the officer) raised his hand, but defendant did not stop. There were a great many people about, as it was the date of the King's visit. _ _</p>
        <p>Defendant said he was going at about 15 miles per hour. He was trying to pass a tramcar so that he would not be obstructing the traffic, and it might, therefore, appear that he was racing. lie did not see the officer. The CLief Constable said it was an exceedingly dangerous road. A line of 405., including costs, was imposed. THE KNUTSFORD-ROAD FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Walter Beresford, 67, Broad Green-road, Broad Green, Liverpool, manager for the Warrington Taxi-Cab Co., was summoned for driving a motor-car at a speed dangerous to the public in Knutsford-road on July 4th. The Chief Constable said that a number of witnesses would be called, and they would say that the speed was anything between 20 and 40 miles per hour. It would be recollected that there was an accident and a little girl was knocked down and subsequently died. William Brown stated that the speed was 35 to 40 miles per hour. In answer to Mr. Swallow (who represented the defendant), he said that the road was clear of traffic at the time. Joseph Briefly estimated the speed at 25 miles ner hour; and James Thomas Lee, 29, Thelwall-lane, said it was between 35 and 40 miles per hour. Harold Holt, 174, Knutsford-road, said the car flashed past his house and he sew it strike the child. Mr. Swallow mentioned that, after a full inquiry, a coroner's jury said the accident was unavoidable. The Chief Constable : Yes, but the Coroner had said that there might be a civil action. Mr. Swallow said the evidence as to speed was very contradictory, as it varied between 40 miles and 25 miles. The men who had given evidence had never driven a car, and could not give any definite expression of opinion as to the speed. The little girl ran out to pick up a ball, but instead of retracing her steps she unexpectedly ran in flout of the car. It was an unavoidable accident. Mr. Bereeford said he not exceed 15 miles per hour. It was a low geared taxi, and a noisy one. The car could not do more than 20 miles per hour at the most. He never saw Kautsford-road so clear in his life as on that occasion. At the time of the accident he was going at 12 miles per hour. Mr. Swallow called several witnesses, who stated that Beresford did all be could to avoid the accident and that he was not travelling at more than 15 miles per hour. Mr. Warburton (one of the occupants of the car) said that however slowly the car had been going the accident would have happened. Mr. Hickman, another occupant, said the car was going at from 16 to 20 miles per hour, and, in view of the fact that the road was clear, he did not think the speed was dangerous. Defendant was lined 40s. and costs. The Chairman said there ought to be a pronounce- ment that in a road like Knutsford-road great care should be takgn by drivers. The Chief Constable : Oaly last night, another accident happened.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. It. T. Fairclough (in the chair), T. Pemberton and A. .1. Jolley. A FASTIDIOUS PRISONEII.—Edith Holt, 174, Route- ford-road, was charged with stealing, as bailee, a pair of lady's boots, value 7s. 6d., from Messrs. Stubbs, Ltd., pawnbrokere, 62, Knutsford-road, on June 23rd.—The Chief Constable said that about 11 o'clock on the day in question prisoner went to Messrs. Stubbs' stores and was supplied with a pair of boots on approval. She failed either to return the boots or send the money. P.C. Skelton made inquiries, and found that prisoner pawned the hoots for 4e: at Mr. Morton's pawn- shop, linutsford-road.—Prisoner said that if she had had time and money she would have taken the boots back. She had nine children and could not make ends meet. Her husband was a man who would not do with- out his tobacco or his beer money.—The Chief Constable: The husband says he gives her Me. per week. She is telling you this tale of woe to mislead you. Whilst she hes been on remand she has told the doctor that new milk and that sort of thing were not good enough for her. She wanted something more tasty. About 68 pawntickets were found in the house. She has got other boots from shops in the town and straightway pledged them.—Prisoner I refuse to handle my husband's money any more. If he can do better than I have dons he can try. lam not afraid of work, and can earn my living honestly.—Prisoner was sent to prison for seven dsTe.</p>
        <p>CRUET, NEGLECT.—Benjamin and Mary Timmins, lodging in Dial-street, were charged with having, on various dates during the last six months, neglected their two children, and pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N'.S.P.C.C., and said the couple had been warned over and over again by tbe society's inspector. The man had been dismissed from his employment and had been continually in and out of the workhouse with his children. Their house was found to be in a dirty condition. If it had not been for kind neighbours no doubt the children would have starved.—Evidence was given by Inspector Wainwright. —Prisoners were sent to gaol for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), Mr. J. Hepherd, Mr. J. Rymer Young, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and Colonel Edelsten.</p>
        <p>CURIOUS SEPARATION CASE.—Selina Savage, 375, Winwick-road, summoned her husband, of s,Back Raven- street, Ravensthorpe, Dewsbury, for non-payment of arrears owing to her, amounting to £3 14s. 6c1., and he, on the other hand, applied for a discharge of the order on the ground of his wife's alleged misconduct. —Mr. Browne Appeared for the wife and Mr. Steel for the husband.—Mr. Steel asked for an annulment of the order, made three years ago, on the ground of adultery. He cited a number of instances to prove the case, and said that in consequence of the conduct of the mother five of the seven children had left home, and only two were now living with ber.—Mr. Browne: We deny all these charges absolutely.—Mr. Steel called witnesses, after which it was decided to adjourn both (Ages for a week, and in the case of the charges against the woman to allow other witnesses to be called.—ln the case of the arrears the defendent was ordered to pay 15s. into Court.</p>
        <p>TIWRSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. W. Roberts (in. the chair), A. J. Jolley</p>
        <p>find Dr.' Naden.</p>
        <p>AGENT'S WARM RECEPTION. • -Christopher Poole, a young married man, of 84, Winwick-road, pleaded guilty to assaulting Mr. H. Brandwood, of Percy-street. The Chief Constable said that Mr. Brandwood went to the house in connection with a distress warrant for non-payment of rent. He was left in charge of the house whilst Mr. S. Gough (the landlord) left the premises for a short time.—Mr.Brandwood said that on arriving at the house he saw two women standing out- side the door and he asked them "what the bother was." They said Mr. Poole had struck Mr. Gough. One woman had hold of Mr. Cough's coat. Poole made use of " violent " language to witness and threatened to put him out. Eventually he threw witness on the floor, punched him and caught hold of his legs, dragging him through the doorway.—Poole admitted striking complainant, but said he did it because his wife had been struck whilst holding a live- week-old baby. He never swore. He had only occupied the house a week and had refused to pay any rent until the premises bad been decorated.—Mrs. Poole alleged that complainant broke down a partition which they had erected in the shop. The rent was not due until Saturday.—Poole was fined 55., including costs.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED HOUSEDREAKING.—Robert Leech, of no fixed abode,was charged with breiking and entering the house 108, Knutsford-road, on Tuesday, and stealing a cap, a pair of hoots and a bicycle, value £1 59., the property of William Lagar.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner had practically admitted the offence, but there were circumstances which made an adjournment necessary. The bicycle had been sold for ss. 6d., the boots bad been pledged and the cap had been found in a lodging-house.—The case was adjourned for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before tbA Mayor (in the phair), AldermAn SmAthorst, and Bolton, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, S. Wright, G.Pilling</p>
        <p>and W. Furness. ALLEGED DESERTION.—WiIIiam Jump, 0, Ellen-street, was summoned by his wife, Jane, 35, Plumpton-street, for desertion.—Complainant said that they had been married six years and her husband left her a month ago. She had seen him inside a house with a woman whom she alleged was too friendly with him.—Defendant siid he had no intention of leaving his wife. He had been forced away from the house. He had offered to get a home together and take his wife from her mother's house.—Complainant said she could not live with her husband.—The magistrates adjourned the case for one month,in order that the Police Court Missionary might bring his good offices to bear. MAINTENANCE ORDER REDUCED.—Wm. Birchall, 110, Hulme-street, applied for the variation of an order made for 103. per week to his wife, Sarah, 80, Fog. street.—Mr. Steel appeared for the applicant, and said that the order was made in 1900, and his client's wages were then 30s. per week. His circumstances had now altered, and for some years his average wages had been 153. per week. His eister had helped him to keep up the payments, and had also kept him in food.—Mr. Browne appeared for the wife, who suddenly suggested that her husband ought to go home to her, as he had been away long enough. She would take him for better or worse.—Mr. Steel raid there were reasons, why that could not be done, and the magistrates reduced the order to one of 7s. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Things It is Not Essential to Know.</head>
        <p>Things It is Not Essential to Know.</p>
        <p>The preacher at the morning sei,vice on Sunday Lt the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, was Mr. A. Mounfield,. who gave an able sermon on Judas. —ln the evening the preacher was the Rev. E. Morgan, who chose the words found in Sr. Luke xiii. 23-24, "Lord, are there few that be saved ? " " Strive to enter in at the strait gate." Such questions as this one concerning the number of those who will meet in Heaven, were common subjects of debate in Rabinnical circles. The Jewish loaders revelled in such contro- versies, and indulged in most daring flights of specula- tion upon subjects about which the sacred revelations were silent. Searching the Scriptures for guidance on these questions was rewarded with nothing that satisfied. In the parable of the sower the bad soil preponderated over the good ; in the parable of the pounds the good was in the majority, while in the parable of the virgins, five were wise and five foolish, leaving us with an equality. "IDLE TO THEORISE AND WICKED TO DOGMATISE." It was not the main purport of the Bible to give any revelation on tbe point. Such questions were really above us and were far from practical. In all such cases it was idle to theorise and wicked to dogmatise. The Scriptures gave us a very definite answer to such attitudes of inquisitiveness. When Daniel in his perplexity asked, What shall the end of these things be ? " the reply which he obtained, "Go thou thy way to the end," was clear proof that it was not essential for him to know there and then. When Peter, in his impulsive anxiety to know, asked Christ about the rumour that John should not see death, the Master's reply was very definite. What is that to thee? Follow thou Me." And similarly here in the case of the inquiry of this unknown individual, concerning the number of those who shall be saved, no definite answer was forthcoming, merely a piece of practical personal advice, " Strive to enter in at the strait gate, for many will seek to enter in and shall not be able."</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ARCHITECTS.</p>
        <p>Should Competition be Restricted t'7.</p>
        <p>Local Firms ?</p>
      </div>
