      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN,</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN,</p>
        <p>HARVEST PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>The cutting of winter oats will begin this week in several counties. The crops, says the "Times," are seldom of average weight, being thin and short, but there is a fairly good head. When the small amount of sunshine experienced in the last few weeks is con- sidered, the colour is satisfactory. In the neighbour- hood of Cambridge a few fields were being cleared for the reaper last week, and patches of whitened oats i were also noticeable n Hertfordshire, Essex, and in the Thames Valley.. Ripening is proceeding irregu- larly even of the winter cereals, the colour as 'well as the yield varying in accordance with the natural drainage and texture of the land. The same variation of growth and maturity characterises the spring crops; and, from all appearances, the harvest, though beginning early, will drag on far through August, as much of the May corn is only now in the early stages of the filling process. This will not be a good corn year. Prolific crops are scattered about throughout the country —some,indeed, are suffering seriously in value from excessive growth —but fields that are under the average greatly pre- dominate. Wheat, which is easily the best of the cereals, is in many cases thin and short, and does not Present that level surface of uniformly filled ears that implies a liberal yield of corn. The difficulties of the farm6r truly are complex. Whereas the common cause of complaint is a deficiency of bulk, a large farmer in Kent states that his harvest has been marked by over-luxuriance of growth. His wheat, which up to a few weeks ago looked full of promise, has been flattened and broken in such a way as to depreciate greatly bcth the corn and the straw. He has never bad it in a worse condition, and he is at a lAs to know how he can harvest it. The soaking rains of last week have given renewed vigcur to the root crops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SALTING CHEESE.</head>
        <p>SALTING CHEESE.</p>
        <p>There are two methods of salting cheese—first, that of adding dry salt to the cheese, and, secondly, that of soaking the newly-finished cheese in brine. Hard- pressed cheeses, which have, in consequence of the method of manufacture and heavy pressure employed, a close texture, are usually dry-salted, while open textured cheese, such as Wensleydale and Caerphilly, are in many instances brined. This is done by soaking the cheeses, as soon as taken frim the press, in brine for two or three days in the case of Wensleydales, while direr varieties are only allowed to remain in the brine for about ten minutes daily for several days, Brining cheese is usually only resorted to in. private dairies, where a few cheese only are made daily owing to the extra labour, space, etc., entailed, as compared with dry-salting the curd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>TO PREVENT AND REMEDY ROPY MILK.</head>
        <p>TO PREVENT AND REMEDY ROPY MILK.</p>
        <p>The bacilli causing ropiness in milk are frequently found in water. Special care should, therefore, be taken in washing the milking pails, strainer cloth, etc., after each milking. When thoroughly clean these should be well scalded and should not again be rinsed with cold water. It is well to scald the pails, etc., shortly before milking. Great care should be taken that no water is splashed into the milk in the process of cooling, etc. Dust is also sometimes a carrier of the bacilli. The pails, etc., after washing should, therefore, be kept upside down, and the milk when it is in the pails should be kept covered as much as possible. The rooms where the milk is kept should be well cleaned. Wooden, cement, or stone floors may be cleaned with a mixture of five parts of commercial sulphuric acid to ninety-five parts of water,but care should be taken that:this does not get on the clothes of the person using it. Wooden vessels should not be used for milk, as they may persistently retain the ropy milk organisms. It is inadvisable to use wooden troughs, even for washing milking vessels, but when they are used special care should be taken to clean them each time after use. Dilute solutions of washing soda favour the gros th of the organisms, and is would be better to use sodium hypcchlorite or some other cleanser and disinfectant in cases of an outbreak of ropy milk. Straw, mouldy hay, and also Butterwort have been shown to hold ropy milk organisms, and the custom of wiping the udders of the cows with a wisp of straw before a ilking is a had one. After washing their hands for milking, milkers should not handle straw or fodder, nor should any such material be brought into the cow-byre just before or during milking. The organisms may be trans- ferred to cows from water in which they have been standing, or which has otherwise been splashed on their flanks or udders. The cows should, therefore, be kept clean ; and ponds should be fenced off so that the cows cannot wade in them. On the first indica- tion of an outbreak of ropy milk the cows should, if possible, at once be turned into another pasture.— Bcard of Agriculture Leaflet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RURAL LABOURERS' HOURS.</head>
        <p>RURAL LABOURERS' HOURS.</p>
        <p>How the Labour Party Propose to Regulate Them.</p>
        <p>A minimum wage for agricultural labJurers and the regularisation of the hours of labour are among the objects of a Labour Party Bill which has just been published. By this measure county agricultural labour boards are to be set up, to consist of elected representatives of the labourers and the employers in equal proportions, with an impartial chairman. Every county board shall,within six months of their establish- ment, determine for their area a maximum of hours per week, which must not exceed sixty, including meal times. Piece rates may be substituted, provided that they yield the labourer remuneration equal to or exceeding that fixed by the Board ; and provision is made for the exemption of the elderly or infirm who are incapable of earning the minimum. No agricultural labourer is to be employed as such after 1 p m. on Saturday, save by the permission of the Board of Agriculture, when another half-day must be substituted, and provision is also made for one day's rest in seven. The Board may, by special order for specified periods (not exceeding six weeks) and areas, permit the employment of agricultural labourers for a stated number of hours per week greater than that determined under the Act. An agricultural labourer under the Act includes any person employed in agri- culture, horticulture, forestry, and the use of land for any purpose of husbandry, but does not include any such person who is wholly or mainly employed in domestic work or any member of an employer's family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WART DISEASE IN POTATOES.</head>
        <p>WART DISEASE IN POTATOES.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries desire to inform potato-growers that cases of wart disease of potatoes (Synchitrium ciulobioticant, Percival) have occurred in Lancashire, and to remiad them that by Article 3 of the Wart Disease of Potatoes Order of 1912 they are required to report the presence of this disease on their premises to the police or other officers appointed by local authorities for the purpose, and that failure to report is punishable by a fine. Notifications may be sent to the Board, who will forward them to the proper quarter. A leaflet describing the disease and giving direc- tions for dealing with it can be obtained from the Secretary, Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall-place, London, S.W., gratia and post free. Letters so addressed need not be stamped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>AGRICULTURAL PRICES IN 1912.</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURAL PRICES IN 1912.</p>
        <p>An All-Round Advance.</p>
        <p>In his report to the Board of Agriculture of prices of corn, live stock and other agricultural produce during 1912 (Agricultural statistics, 1912, Part III.), Mr. Rew states that the average price of wheat in 1912, as calculated from the weekly returns received under the Corn Returns Act, was 345. 9d. per imperial quarter, being 3s. ld. higher than in 1911 and 1910. The average price of 139.riey was 30s. Bd. per quarter, being 3s. sd. higher than in 1911, and is. 7d. higher than in 1910. The average price returned for oats in 1912 was 21s. Gd. per quarter, being the highest since 1882. As compared with 1911, the price was 2s. Bd. higher. All kinds of live stock—for meat —made higher prices in 1912 than in the previous year, which was itself above the average of the five years 1906-10. Prices of store cattle were unusually high in 1912, especially in the ,3ase of older and stronger stores. The figures show considerable differences as between different breeds but on the whole there was an increase in 1912 of nearly 5 per cent, upon the prices</p>
        <p>of 1911. Owing to the outbreak of foot and mouth disease and the consequent interference with the Irish cattle supplies, there was a marked local shortage of stores in the winter feeding districts, and prices rose considerably, but at the same time it was, the report says, significant that in some of the chief cattle breed- ing districts store cattle were very little dearer than usual.</p>
        <p>The prices of store sheep were not so high as in 1908, but generally they were higher than in the three years 1909-11. The feature in the trade of dairy cows in 1912 was the high prices realised in the closing months of the year. The pric3 for good shorthorns in milk, which averaged £2O 16s. per head in May, being rather low as grass was scarce, was, in December, £24 2s. The price for good Ayrshires in milk rose from £l6 its. in March to £23 Os. in December. In spite of these high prices the average price for Shorthorn milking cows showed practically no change in the year. Ayrsbires, however, advanced noticeably, first quality COYV Bin milk averaging £l9 10s. per bead, as com- pared with milk £lB ss. in 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</p>
        <p>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SHOW,</p>
        <p>A meeting of the council of the 'Loyal Lancashire Agricultural Society was held on Monday on the show- ground at Burnley. Mr. Windham E. Hale presided. At a subsequent luncheon, presided over by Mr. H. A. Tatton, The Secretary (Mr. Edward Bohane) said that the arrangements for the show were well advanced. Local enthusiasm was an important factor, and in this respect Burnley had not been surpassed by any other town visited. The Mayor (Mr. Alderman E. Keighley) and the President of the Society (Sir John 0. S. Thursby) had worked indefatigably, with the result that the local fund amounted to £2,000. The total entries in all classes numbered 4,459, as compared with an average of 4,351. Mr. Bohane mentioned that 153 schoolgirls had decided to send a loaf of bread for competition. In the horse and cattle classes the entries were of a high order. There was a great improvement in the hackney section, and he believed that the show of shorthorn dairy cattle would be the finest ever seen in Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DISEASES OF RASPBERRY AND LOGANBERRY. —Hender- sonia rubi, Westendorp, is responsible for most of the injury caused to raspberries and loganberries. The canes or stems are the parts attacked, infection taking place during the summer, when the young growth is tender. The first indication of the presence of the parasita varies to some extent with the particu- lar variety attacked, but as a rule lurid red or purplish patches of variable size appear on the stem. Such patches continue for some time to increase in size, and during the winter change to a pale grey or dingy white colour, due to the bark having been killed. At this stage the fruit of the fungus, in the form of minute black dots, is thickly scattered over the dead, white patches of bark. Microscopic examination shows that tle minute black fruits contain myriads of coloured, four-celled spores. When mature these spores escape into the air, and those that happen to alight on young shoots germinate and enter the tissues, thus ensuring the continuance of the disease in the following season. When several diseased patches are present the canes are killed outright (hiring the winter, whereas when only one or two diseased areas are present, the cane may survive and produce a certain amount of fruit. As the spores of the fungus, however, will be present in such cases, it is sound policy, even at the sacrifice of a certain amount of fruit, to remove and burn all canes, how- ever slightly attacked, as it is only by such means that the disease can be eradicated. Infection mostly takes place at the period of the year when fruit is present, hence spraying cannot be resorted to unless the fruit is sacrificed, when Bordeaux mixture should be used. Even in these circumstances every diseased cane should be cut down the moment it is observed. The practice, common in many places, of allowing the dead canes to remain standing throughout the winter, is to be cundernned.—Bcard of Agriculture Leaflet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBERALS' RALLY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERALS' RALLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. F. L. Brunner and the Tory Party's Bankruptcy.</p>
        <p>A Liberal fête, which proved a great success,was held in the spacious Plantation Pleasure Grounds, Runcorn. on Saturday week. Mr. J. F. Norman, J.P., presi- dent of the Liberal Association, presided. On the plat- form there were f4l r. J. F. L. Brunner, M.P. for the Division, and Mrs. Brunner, Mr. Hay Morgan, M.P. for the Truro Division of Cornwall, Mr. J. B. Martindale, prospective Liberal candidate for Widnes, Mr. A. R. Norman, J.P., Mr. and Mr. Samuel Taylor, Councillor Wilkinson (Northwich), Mr. U. Deakin, Rev. E. Evans, and Mrs.Spraut, of the Women's Liberal Association. Mr. J. F. L. Brunner said he could assure all present that the Government were doing very well, and for confirmation of that they had only to read the Tory Press. It was always a good sign when they found the Tory- politicians in a bad temper. The Government were forging ahead with the Home Rule, Welsh Dis- establishment, and Scotch Temperance Bills, all of which were well on their way through the House of Commons for the second time, and which they intended to place on the Statute Book by the aid of the Parliament Act during the life of the present Government. There was an all-night sitting in Parliament last week, discussing the Plural Voting Bill, a. measure which would unload the dice against the Liberal party and the poor man. The Tories had not a single argument worth uttering against this Bill. The Marconi business had been occu- pying the attention of the political world a great deal lately, and what was the objects of the Tory party in trying to carry on that campaign now, when both reports that had been presented stated that there was no charge of corruption against Mr. Lloyd George or Sir Rufus Isaacs. It showed that the Tory party were bankrupt for a policy. They adopted the Tariff Reform policy, which the country would not have, and they appear to have got nothing else to do now but to talk of scandals. Their object in doing this was simply to "down "Lloyd George. He had read that morning a speech that had been delivered by Lord Edmund Talbot, the chief Tory Whip, in which he stated that they were going to carry on the Marconi campaign. When Mr. Lloyd George was attacked, he not only defended himself, but he hit back; and it was to try and stop the Chancellor's future pro- gramme that the Tories had undertaken to persist in propagating this Marconi scandal. He urged all to support Lloyd George, who would in the future do as much good work for them as he had done in the past. (Hear, hear.) Referring to his opponent in the constituency, he said he would like to say something about the way in which he carried on his campaign in that division. Mr. Jersey De Knoop, to his mind, did not fight on polities. He trusted that a change would come over the methods of the Tory party in the division, and that they would keep the constituency clean, as it always had been in past. The Tory party had always been beaten, and if hey were to fight let them fight on politics, and let he best man win. (Hear, hear.) MR. MARTINDALE ON THE FARM STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Martindale, in seconding the resolution, said Mr Lloyd George's land scheme would attack problems which faced the country at the present time. In South- west Lancashire the agricultural labourers had done a remarkable thing—they had gone on strike for better conditions. That was a sign of the times and the result of education. The cause was owing to the landlords not allowing the men better conditions, but he could assure them that the men would benefit substantially as the result of that dispute. The itev. E. Evans said the Conservative policy was one of Protcction, conscription, and vilification. Councillor Wilkinson, Mr. Deakin, and Mrs. Sputa also addressed the meeting. The sports were afterwards decided, Mrs. Brunner pres'enting the prizes. High(leld Tannery Band were in attendance all the afternoon, and dancing and other amusements were indulged in until late in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Stomach Headaches.</head>
        <p>Stomach Headaches.</p>
        <p>Those throbbing, piercing headaches, which hinder yon in your work and make life hardly worth the living, are often due to some derairgement of the digestiNse system. It is well to remember that indi- gestion does cause headaches, so does liver trouble and constipation. An occasional dose of Mother Seigel's Syrup, the famous herbal remedy, so regulates and tones up the stomach, liver, and bowels, that such distressing complaints as bilious or stomach headaches, acidity, flatulence, pains after eating, and languor, become almost im- possible. You can take Mother Seigel's Syrup in perfect confidence, because in the last forty years tens of thousands of peopl,Q, have found that it really does give them good health by banishing and preventing stomach and liver troubles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DEMAND OF THE WELSH PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>DEMAND OF THE WELSH PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Statement of the ease for the Disestablishment of the Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>"AN ACT OF</head>
        <p>"AN ACT OF</p>
        <p>The Warrington Junior Liberal Association is doing a valuable educational work in the constituency by holding open•air meetings at which the chief political questions of the day are expounded. On Friday evening last week there was a demonstration at Bridge Foot in support of the Welsh Church Bill, and the audience listened with great interest to speeches by Mr. F. Rhodes Smith (of the Central Campaign Committee), and Mr. Chas. Peach (secretary of the Northern Counties Education League). It was shown that the Bill is in accordance with the consistent demand of the Welsh nation, extending over many years, and the grounds for devoting the 'ancient endowments to national purposes were fully stated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WILL OF THE WELSH PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>THE WILL OF THE WELSH PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. LLOYD JONES (chairman of the association), who presided, said the question was first raised in the House of Commons by Mr. Watkin Williams, member for Denbigh Boroughs, in 1870, but in 1868 it was very much to the fore in Wales. At that time, however, it was impossible to get into the HOllBO of Commons representatives who were in sympathy with the aspira- tions of the people. That was changed, and in 1872 there was a majority returned, and the question was more prominent than ever. In 1886, 27 Welsh members out of 34 were sent to the House of Commons in favour of the Disestablisbment of the Welsh Church. In 1895, 31 out of 34 were returned in support of the cause, and that was the exact number they bad to-day. The three solitary members returned against Disestablishment only had a small majority between them. He thought that was a sufficient answer to the question," Do the Welsh people demand Disestablishmeut ?" (near, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WALES A NATION.</head>
        <p>WALES A NATION.</p>
        <p>He knew there were men who would deny that the Welsh people were a nation, but any right-minded person realised that in everything possible the Welsh people were a nation. Wales was a nation in language, literature and religion, in sentiment and in aspirations. There was not the slightest doubt that the concession of the Welsh demand was long overdue. They were thankful, however, that to-day the star had arisen and they were about to see that demand conceded. He telt Icertain that when they came to consider everything they could find nothing in the Bill but what was just and equitable. (Hear, hear.) They were told it was a "robbery of God." One scarcely knew what words to -use in describing such an accusation. Wales was a nation that God had chosen for His people tor the last 100 years, and did they mean to tell him that a people guided by that Spirit, which alone guided right, could possibly be guilty of robbing God? .r.V3 a matter of fact, such a thing was quite foreign to the heart and aspirations of the Welsh people. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>AN APPEAL TO DEMOCRATS.</head>
        <p>AN APPEAL TO DEMOCRATS.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. RHODES SMITH said the question was one which they in England might say did not affect them very much, but it was of vital importance to Wales. Appealing to the people, Mr. Smite said, "If you are democrats, I presume ou are, if you believe that when you have sent your member to the House of Commons he should be there to represent your wishes, then the people of England have nothing to do with this meaeure." As had been pointed out by the Chairman, for a long time the Welsh nation had demanded that measure of reform. As a matter of fact, ever since the people of Wales had been able to vote there had been no swerving in the matter—they had been unanimous in their demand—and while in England they had been affected by what was known as the swing of the pendulum, in Wales they had not been affected. The reason they were in England asking the English electors to stand by them in their demand was that the Bishops, who told them that they usually gave January and February to prayer, had this year and last been very busy on Conservative platforms up and down the country talking about robbing God and the nation of Wales being thieves. NO ANTAGONISNI TO THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>They had no spirit of antagonism to the Church of England. He had nothing to say against the creeds and doctrines of the Church of England, but he had a great deal to say about the political connection of the Church of England and the State. They had been called thieves—that was bad. The Tories had taken their lead from Mr. Boner Law, who was leading from behind. (Laughter.) If the movement was in any way an attack on religion, he (the speaker) would have nothing to do with it. They were called political Dissenters, and all sorts of things. Ho was proud to be there as one of these people who believed in what was c3lled robbing God, and he would show them where the robbery came in before he had finished. (Laughter.) Mr. Boner Law said the movement was animated by "envy, hatred, malice, and all uncharitableness." Taking that cue, the opponents of the measure had gone for its eurporters right and left.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ARCHBISHOP'S TESTIMONY.</head>
        <p>ARCHBISHOP'S TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>He would, however, remind them that the Archbishop of Canterbury, speaking at Carnarvon, said, " the Disestablishment movement was supported in England and in Wales by honest, high-minded Christian men." At a recent meeting there were those two great Welsh- men, Mr. Boner Law and Mr. F. E. Smith—(laughter)— demanding that the Welsh nation should keep their hands off the English Church. They started the meeting with the hymn, "The Church's one foundation "—a glorious hymn. If the Church's one foundation was the State, then the foundation was going. (Laughter.) He did not think when they sang that glorious hymn that they meant the State at all. Arch- bishops had appealed to fairminded Nonconformists and Liberals to be just, and they had said that, for the first time in history, if that measure was passed it meant that the Welsh nation would have no further need for the Christian faith. A LIBEL ON THE WELSH NATION.</p>
        <p>That wao a great libel on the Welsh nation which was the home of revivals. Where did they get the religious fervour that existed in Wales ? As Mr. Asquith said in the House of Commons in 1909, in introducing the second Welsh Bill, "they could not ignore the repeated opinion of the Welsh people." The great stumbling block in the path of progress was the House of Lords. (Hear, hear.) Thirty-one members for Wales, representing the voice of the Welsh people, had passed that measure in the House of Commons, and four bishops representing no one, sitting in the House of Lords, gave the word and out went the Bill, but next session it could not be thrown out until it had been to the King. (Applause.) MAJORITY WITHOUT THE IRISH VOTE.</p>
        <p>They had been told that the Bill had been passed by the Irish. On the second reading last year, taking the Irish out, they had a majority of 24, on the third read- ing a majority of 39 ; wkilst on the third reading last week they had a majority of 22, (Fleur, hear.) Quite</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RESTITUTION."</head>
        <p>RESTITUTION."</p>
        <p>apart from the Irish vote if that majority of 42 in the House of Commons was not sufficient for passing the Bill then he wanted to know how big a majority had to be sod who was going to decide it? If the Tories could get in power by 42 they would show the people a thing or two. (Laughter and a Voice : "They won't get in.") If they referred to the" Church Times" for 12 months ago, it said that "If Wales were a separate nation there would be no room for discussion. Disestablishment would be affected straight away by an overwhelming majority." Then their opponents came along and said the question had never been before the country and that no one knew anything about it. Prior to the last election, Mr. F. E. Smith and Mr. Austin Chamber- lain said, "If this Government is returned to power they will no longer be the servants of the people they will be masters." "That," commented Mr. Rhodes Smith, "is what we want to be." (Applause.) The question had been discussed far too long, and it was time the Welsh people had what was due to them and what they had demanded over and over again. (Hear, hear.) The speaker next contended that the bishops in the House of Lords in the past had been defenders of privilege, and continued to say that wherever they got a Welsh community they found Welsh chapels. When they were told that under that Bill they were going to sever the oldest portion of the Church of England he wanted to say that the Church of England had no right to force that Church on the Welsh nation as the Established religion of Wales. The Church of England was not Established in any part of the world but in England and Wales. It was not Established in Ireland, Scotland or the Colonies, but in Wales it has supposed to be the national Church. WHAT IS A NATIONAL CHURCH? Bishop Weldon, of Manchester, in a recent book written for the benefit of Church defenders, laid down that a Church to be a national church should contain a majority of the people, that was to say over half of the nation must be in sympathy with the aims and objects of the church. In Wales they had found that not one in ten of the people belonged to the Church of England. In England one- half of the church-goers went to the church and half went to the chapel, and the rest of the peeple went nowhere at all. (Laughter.) In \Vales, out of a population of 2,000,000, the Church of England could not claim 193,000 communicants. Nonconformity numbered 550,000 full members. Nonconformity in Wales num- bered over 1,000,000 of the people. Then why should the Church of England claim to be the national Church? When it came to a question of places of worship, they found the national Church, with national funds and grants, possessing 1,800 places of worship. Noncon- formity, by the earnings of her own people, without State assistance, built nearly 5,000 what were called " little bethels " in Wales. The national Church in Wales could not seat one quarter of the people. The "little bethels "could seat 1,500,000. They were only asking that the Church should look after herself and leave other denominations to do the same.. (Hear, hear.) The non-established religion in Wales was in an overwhelming majority, and yet the minority were taking £250,000 every year that belonged to the people of Wales. TITHE A TAX.</p>
        <p>Dealing with endowments, the speaker said that tithe might at one time have been a voluntary gift in England. It might have been the custom for the people of this country to give a tenth of their produce, &amp;0., to support the Church, but in Wales there was no evidence that any such system existed. The only evidence that could be found was that under the Norman Conquest the people of Wales were compelled by law to pay their "voluntary offering" to the Church. That so-called "voluntary offering"—tithe---could be collected by the County Court bailiff, and if the people did not feel inclined to give their "voluntary offering" to the Church then the bailiff would help them to do so. (Laughter.) That tithe, which was a national tax collected by the civil arm of the law, was used and kept by one-tenth of the people. Advocates of the present measure claimed that a nutional tax should be used for the benefit of the whole of the people. In the old days the Church looked after the poor and also educated the people, but to-day, by our Poor Law system, we paid our poor rates for the care of the poor and the education rates for the education of the people. The Church, however, had retained the whole of the money, which originally went to those objects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOT AFTER THE MONEY.</head>
        <p>NOT AFTER THE MONEY.</p>
        <p>Opponents of disestablishment said the Noncon- formists were after the money, but they would not touch one penny of the endowments. Time after time in the House of Commons it had been offered to Nonconformists and they had said, "We will share it with you," but Nonconformists had replied, "It belongs not to us, nor to you, but to the nation, and the nation shall have it." In conclusion, the speaker said that instead of the measure being one of robbery it was a simple Act of restitution to the people of what belonged to them, and he contended that there were no more Christian objects to which the money could be applied than to provide for the poor and needy and look after the sick. (Applause.) THE SECRET OF OUR GREATNESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. PEACH said they had to consider whether, on the whole, the Disestablishment of the English Church in Wales would accord with the genius, character, history and mission of the English people. By a mysterious process, we had been made a great people and empire. What was the secret of our greatness and security? What was the secret of the swarming to eur support of nen from all the ends of the earth when the first whisper of danger to the homeland was blown abroad ? It was because the genius of the English people had discovered the great truth that the secret of all security was to rule iu accordance with the will of the people. That was what made the Empire invincible against attack and made it immortal because of the loyalty of its subjects. We had not sought to impose upon the Colonies our political or religious system, and the consequence had been not weakness but strength. Why could we not apply the same principle to our immediate neighbours at home ? That was what the Welsh Church Bill sought to do. By history, language, literature and every- characteristic that marked a people—even by the legis- lation of our own Parliament—Wales was marked off as a definite political unit to which we could apply definite special principles. (Applause.) For many years an overwhelming majority of the Welsh repre- sentatives had gone to the House of Commons pledged to support Welsh Disestablishment. The present measure was just, reasonable and fair. EQUALITY WITHOUT PRIVILEGE.</p>
        <p>In the matter of Disestablisbment, it removed the special privileges of one denomination, and placed it on an equality with all other denominations. While</p>
        <p>Continued in next co/umn.</p>
        <p>From Reynolds's Newspaper.</p>
        <p>3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>PROTECTING THE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>PROTECTING THE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>What The N.S.P.C.C. is Doing in Warrington.</p>
        <p>MAKING LIFE ENDURABLE.</p>
        <p>"Prosecution of offenders is not, as those acquainted with the society's methods know, either sought or desired. The object is to secure better conditions an endurable life '—for the children. That object is achieved in the vast majority of cases without having recourse to prosecution, which is the last argument 'in the society's armoury." The above passages occur in the report on the work of the Warrington and District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children for the two years ended March 31st, 1913, which has just been published. For the year ending March 31st, 1912, 268 com- plaints of cruelty to, or neglect of, children were dealt with, affecting the welfare of 792 children, and involv- ing 386 offenders, In the next year the number of complaints was less-249 and 691 children were con- cerned in the cases for the year. The number of persons prosecuted and convicted was 18 in 1912 and 19 in 1913.</p>
        <p>' In addition to the original inquiries, 1,568 super- vision visits were paid by the inspector in the first year, and 1,300 in the second. Twelve inquiries were made on behalf of other branches in 1912, and no fewer than 22 in 1913 —sufficient demonstration of the , value of the national organisation of the society. A good proportion of the cases were brought to the society's notice by members of the general public (161 in 1912, 159 in 1913), but it could be wished (says the report) that the number was still greater. rlt is in the prompt reporting of cases that the safe- guard of neglected children lies; so that it is a duty incumbent upon every reader of this report to make as widespread as possible knowledge of the society and its work. That knowledge means confidence, and- also the reporting of any case where neglect or ill treatment of a child is suspected. THE FINANCIAL POSITION.</p>
        <p>_ The financial position of the branch has been main- tained. In 1912, after all expenses had been paid, there was a balance of 3:18, and in 1913 the balance was £2O - these sums being available in aid of the national fund of the society. Thanks are tendered to the lady collectors, whose splendid help is such an invaluable asset of the work to the police and other public officials, for the kind cooperation; and to the Press for the aid of publicity from time to time. The Board of Guardians have once more given practical proof of their appreciation of the work, and this is the more gratifying, coming from a public body especially qualified to estimate the value of that work.</p>
        <p>A special word of acknowledgment is also due to tbe organisers of, and all helpers at, the ball held in 1912, through which the funds of the branch benefited by the welcome addition of over ~-C.lO. Through the ball in 1913, another successful event, the sum of £27 was raised.</p>
        <p>The committee desire to draw the attention of the subscribers and friends to the fact that all gifts of old clothing and boots are most acceptable. The Inspec- tor can make the best possible use of any gifts of this sort. They heartily thank those who sent parcels during the year. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>HOLIDAY ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Accidents are far more common at holiday times than at any other period. The youngsters, freed from schcol, get into all sorts of mischief, and even grown- ups, released from the daily round and common task, have their adventures. There is, therefore, double the need for keeping a green box of Zam-Buk close by. Zam-Buk has been qptly described as "a complete surgery in a two-inch box"—so instantly soothing and healing is a dressing of this rare herbal balm. Put a box of Zam-Buk in your holiday tiunk. Zam-Buk can always be depended upon to heal the nasty cut or scrape, and remove the distressing skin soreness that so often spoils the pleasure of a holiday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>DEATH OF BILLY RICHARDSON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF BILLY RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>The death occurred at his home in Liverpool on Monday of Billy Richardson, the well-known stump orator. Mr. Richardson a good many years ago gained great popularity as one of Sam Hague's Minstrels. Latterly he had been performing at New Brighton and other seaside places round about Liverpool.</p>
        <p>DEMAND OF THE WELSH PEOPLE.—Continuad. removing its special privileges as an Established body it was giving to the Church a higher privilege in the shape of freedom. In any just estimate of gain and loss freedom would outweigh immensely, even in the life and activities of the Church, but especially in the hearts and consciences of Churchpeople, when they had experienced it, anything they might seem to suffer in the loss of dignity that followed from Disestablish- ment. When the spiritual life of a nation expressed itself in many ways, for the State to select one of those ways for Establishment and Endowment was to create a disability and to inflict a wrong on the other sections of 41ae community. Finally, Mr. Peach appealed to the meeting to support the demand of the Welsh people, who, in their turn, would help the workers ot England in the fights they had yet to wage against privilege. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TRUE ESTABLISHMENT.</head>
        <p>THE TRUE ESTABLISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER, in proposing a vote of thanks to the speakers, said he felt strongly that unless a Church was established in the hearts and lives of the people, it ought not to be established in the nation. No one could sly that the English Church held such a position in \Vales as should command for it the right to be called the Church of Wales. The people, as a rule, were not sufficiently educated on the question. It did not,perhaps, interest them so directly as Free Trade and kindred political issues, but it was a national ques- tion which was much to the fore at the present time, and he wished that a larger number of the inhabitants of Warrington had heard the case so fairly and strongly put as it bad been that night. (Applause.) The vote was heartily accorded. [A condensed report appeared in our last issue.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as an unrivalled medicine for children. Observe the EE in Steedman. These powders contain no poison, , •</p>
        <p>Oliver could and would do great things unless he had an army at the back of him, he could not take a great citadel like the Knutsferd division. It was, therefore, necessary that they should all do their part. lie was almost tempted to remind them that there was still a Tory party. (Laughter.) That party boasted leaders, but it was difficult to say who those leaders were. (Laughter.) There were Mr. Boner Law and Mr. Austen Chamberlain in the House of Lords. They sometimes had different ways of giving the lead, but still they were leaders. (Laughter.) When they got to the House of Lords it was rather different. Lord Lansdowne led on Monday, and then Lord Curzon came along and led a diff,rent way on Tuesday. (Laughter.) They had only to read the debates in the House of Lords to recognise that there were no arguments against Home Rule. Lord Lansdowne said there ought to be an appeal to the people. They had had one General Election quite definitely on the question—Lord Lansdowne himself said it was fought on that. Then Lord Curzon said that if there were another election and the people said they wanted Home Rule, the Lords would let the Bill have a second reading and then proceed to amend it. (Laughter.)9Were they going to have a General Election to decide whether the House of Lords should reject the Home Rule Bill or only amend it ? ("No.") He did not think they were going to have anything to du with such an idea. He felt confident that Liberals and the great mass of the people of this country would see through tactics of that, kind and would consistently support the Government, so that the Bill, when it came forward a third time next, yeaz, would find its climax on the statute book. (Applause.;</p>
        <p>TUG-OF-WAR IN WHICH THE WHITLEY LIBERALS WERE SUCCESS</p>
        <p>COLLIERY STRIKE TIIREAT AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>The Miners' Federation has been trying for some weeks to ensure that all the men working at Messrs. R. Evans and Co.'s pits at Ilaydock, St. llelens, Golborne, and Edge Green should be rnemoers of the federation, hut as the effort has failed notices were handed in on Saturday giving fourteen days' notice that the whole of the 6,000 federationists employed will come out on strike against the employment of non-federation workmen.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS LABOUR CANDIDATE. SELECTION OF MR. JAMES SEXTON.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Labour party met on Saturday after- noon, at the Co-operative Hall, to adopt Mr. James Sexton, of Liverpool, secretary of the National Union of Doak Labourers and Riverside Workers' Union, as the Labour candidate, in succession to the late Mr. T. Glover.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Simm (chairman of the Trades and Labour Council) presided, and read a letter from Mr. J. A. Seddon welcoming Mr. Sexton to the electcral contest in St. Helens as a fighting electioneer of sterling worth.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sexton addressed the meeting, speaking first of his early days in St. Helens, when, as a boy, he worked and suffered in the great glassworks of the town. He did not come there to boast of his work,but he said that merely as a guarantee of good faith, to show that no matter bow many candidates were in the field, he could claim to know something more about the industrial conditions than any carpet-bagger who called himself a barrister-at-law.</p>
        <p>He had often been at a loss to understand why any industrial borough should send any other than a Labour man to the House of Commons. He had never been able to understand why trade unionists particularly should object to pay for labour representation. If they surveyed the situation they would realise that the industrial and economic conditions which they com- plained of were founded upon legislation made in Parliament and administered in their local councils and boards of guardians. They paid into their trial° union lodges £2,000,000 a year in order to protect themselves against the employers, yet when an election came cn they voted for the employers to go to Parliament to make and administer the laws under which they had to live and work. What an absurd position for workingmen to take up ! They saw how Lord Wolmer, the representative of a neighbouring constituency, Lord Winterton, and other sprigs of nobility were plotting and scheming and intriguing to take away their charter of the Trades Disputes Bill —a Bill which was wrung trent the Government to counteract the evil influence of the Taff Vale decision. What surprised him was that there was even one workingman in St. Helens who as a trade unionist could reconcile his vote cast fora Tory or any party politician as against a Labour man. The Labour party from timo to time wrung from the Governments palliatives that relieved the situation somewhat ; but they would have to go deeper down into their social system in order to relieve the evils present and future generations would suffer if they did not do their duty.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING AT YORK.</p>
