      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>VILLAGE FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>Procession and Revels at Appleton</p>
        <p>Thorn.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The usual quietude of the old-fashioned village of Appleton Thorn was broken on Saturday, and scenes of gaiety and festival were to be witnessed every- where. It was the annual festival, and the sons of the soil dropped their scythes and other agricultural implements and entered into the pleasures, thrills and bustb of a fete in that whole-hearted manner, which is a prominent characteristic of those who toil on the land. Not only do the older inhabitants enjoy this annual feast day, but the children also make it one of the greatest days in the year. The arrangements for Saturday were more complete than ever, and although there were feelings of disappointment at the threaten- ing outlook in the morning, the spirits of everyone concerned rose considerably when the sun forced its way through the scudding clouds and shone in all its glory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PRETTY SPECTACLE.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY SPECTACLE.</p>
        <p>A pretty spectacle was witnessed when about 120 children assembled at the school and marched through the village, headed by the Warrington Pioneer Prize Brass Band. Many complimentary remarks were made as to the neatness and clemiliuess of the children, as they made their way to the splendid open field, kindly lent by Mr. Britch, on which their sports were conducted. A keen enthusiasm prevailed, each child being eager to secure a prize in one or other of the numerous events: WINNERS OF THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the winners:- Flat Race for Boys under 7, 50 yards handicap : 1, H. England ; 2, C. England; 3, J. Percival. Flat Race for Girls wider 7, 50 yards handicap : 1, D. Moulsdale ; 2, P. Itoughsedge ; 3, O. Roughsedge. Flat Race for Boys, from 7 to 9, GO yards handicap : 1, R. Brockleburst ; 2, C. Ditchfield , 3, W. Keen. 'Flat Race for Girls, from 7 to 9, GO yards handicap : 1, M. Riley ; 2, H. Moulsdale ; 3, M. Marsh. Flat Race for Boys, from 9 to 11, 75 yards handicap : 1, P. Garner; 2, W. Brocklehurst ; 3, A. Taylor. Flat Race for Girls, from 9 to 11, 75 yards handicap: 1, D. Mills ; 2, J. Brocklehurst; 3, E. Reunion. . Flat Race for Schoolboys, from 11 to 14, 100 yards, handicap : 1, A. Masterton ; 2, A. Bennion ; 3, J. Garner. Flat Race for Schoolgirls, from 11 to 14, 100 yards handicap : 1, E. Brocklehurst ; 2, C. Moulsdale ; 3, E. Warburton. Flat Race for Boys, from 12 to 16,150 yards handicap : 1, A. Masterton ;2, A. Bennion, 3, H. Simpson. ' Flat Race for Girls, from 12 to 16, 100 yards handicap : 1, E. Brocklehurst ; 2, E. Moulsdale ; 3, E. Warburton. Sacs Race for Boys over 10, 50 yards handicap : 1, J. Clark ; 2, G. Masterton ; 3, H. Johnson. Skipping Contest for Girls under 10 (three minutes) : 1, N. Taylor ; 2, W. Brocklehurst ; 3, A. Moulsdalc. Skipping Contest. for Girls over ten rive minutes) : 1, E. Taylor; 2, J. Brocklehurst ; 3, E. Mouladale. Three-legged Race for Boys over 9, 75 yards : 1, .7 and P. Garner ; 2, D. Percival and R. Brocklehurst ;3, G. and A. Masterton. Egg and Spoon Race for Girls over 9, 60 yards : 1 J. Brocklehurst ; 2, IV. Brocklehurst ; 3, E. Taylor. Wheelbarrow Race for Boys over 9, 60 yards : 1, J. end P. Garner ; 2, H. Johnson and J. Lunt ; 3, A. Taylor and W. Brocklehurst.</p>
        <p>_ BJot-Lacing Contest for Girls ; 1, E. Warburton; 2, E. Renshaw ; 3, J. Brocklehurst. Tug-of-War for Boys over 10 : Team B (Frank Gleave's team). By the kind generosity of Mr. and Mrs. Frank Houghton, the parishioners were entertained to tea in Mr. Britch's barn. Mrs. Joynson, of the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, catered excellently, and the repast was thoroughly enjoyed. At an Interval in the proceedings, Mr. Marsh pro- posed the health of Mr. and Mrs. Houghton,and coupled with it a hearty vote of thanks to them for the kind- ness and generosity which they bad extended to the people of Appleton. They were glad Mr. Houghton was restored to health and hoped he would he with them for many years. (Applause.) They were also . indebted to the willing ladies and gentlemen who had so tastefully decorated the tables and transformed the barn so very much. (Applause.) Mr. Britch seconded, and said Mr. and Mrs. Houghton did all thee could for the benefit of the people of Appleton. (Hear, bear.) The toast was accorded musical honours. AN EVENTFUL YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Houghton &amp;aid they were very pleased to see their Vienr (the Rev. F. E. Evans) and his wife amongst them. (Hear, hear.) He sincerely hoped that that day would see the egiuning of happier times in Appleton for all of them—that happiness and prosptrity would prevail for all time. (Applause.) He thanked the workers in connection with the fete who had worked most assiduously for a successful day. Speaking of the village, Mr. Houghton said they had had serious losses during the year, but they had found willing people who had taken the places of the departed. There were many changes in life, but he hoped he would be spared to do ha t he couid for the parishioners next year.(Applause.) The year had been a most eventful one—they had hau the King and Queen at ‘Varrington and the new bridge had been opened. There had been many cl.anges, but he thought that Appleton would still make progress in many ways. In conclusion he hoped the dull days of winter would be -brightened by the sunshine and happi- ness of summer. (Applause.) On the proposition of Mr. Ilarnish, seconded by Mr. Marsh, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. and Mrs. Witch for the loan of the barn aril their general useful help. The company joined in singing " For they are jolly good people "in ti very enthusiastic and waole-hearted manner.</p>
        <p>In reply, Mr. llritch said both he and Mrs. Brach welcomed the people of Appleton there. That was one of those pleasant occasions which helped to brighten the lives of everyone of them. (Hear, bear.) Mrs. Hougnton then gracefully presented the prizes to the children.</p>
        <p>The field was crowded in the evening when the sports for .the adults were held. Ihe swings and games supplied by Mr. Plinston were exceedingly well patronised, nod Professor A. liorrocles entertainment with Punch and Judy, ventriloquism, &amp;c., wag very clever and amusing. The round of fun and gaiety, including dancing to the strains of the Pioneer Band, was continued until duak, when everyone expressed the view that the festival had been a most successful sni enjoyable one. At the close, sweets, given by Mrs. iloughton, were presented to the children.</p>
        <p>The adult prize-winners were as follows : Flat Race for Youths, 150 yards handicap : 1, A. Gleave ; 2, W. Aloulsdale ; 3, H. Brockleburst. Flat Race for Women, 75 yards handicip : 1, A. Mouhfdale ; 2, E. Wilkin9on ; 3, E. airner. Flat Race for Men, handicap : 1, W. Moulsdale ; 2, W. Clayton ; 3, A. Preston. Egg and Spoon Race for Women, 50 yards: 1, E. Garner; 2, A. Moulsdale ; 3, A. Leech. Hops skip and jump, once only (from a given line), for Men :1, W. Clayton ; 2, W. Moulsdale ; 3, H. 13rocklehurst. _</p>
        <p>Ladies' Committee : 1, Mrs. Houghton; 2, Miss E. Beswick ; 3, Miss M. A. Jones. Gentlemen's Committee : 1, Mr. Hankey ; 2, Mr. Ckyton ; 3, Mr. Moulsdale. Bandsmen's Race : 1, 'l'. Hughes; 2. A. Wadsworth ; 3, W. Berry. High Jump : 1, F. Leigh; 2, H. Brocklehurst ; 3, W. Clayton.</p>
        <p>• Sack Race for Men, 75 yards: 1, W. Clayton ; 2, H Brocklchurst ; 3, F. Jones. Three-Legged Race for Men, 100 Yards : 1, J. and H, Garner; 2, H. Brocklehurst and W. Clayton; 3, A. Preston anti W. Monlsdals.</p>
        <p>Tug-of-War for Men (10) a-side : C. Taylor's team won.</p>
        <p>Obstacle Race : 1, A. Preston; 2, F. Jones; 3, H. Brocklehurst</p>
        <p>- On the proposition of Mr. Lambert, seconded by Mr. Pimlott, Mi. Houghton was heartily thanked for her kindness, and she was loudly cheered, as also were Miss Houghton and Mrs. Joynson. Miss Beswick had worked most energetically as the hon. Secretary of. the fete and fully desnved the thanks she received. The officials were :—Chairman, Mr. Frank Houghton ; hon. ecretari, Miss A. Beswick; hon. treasurer, Mr. Lambert ; committee, Messrs. Batnish,James Britoil, William Clayton, Walter Clayton, ilindley, William Hankey, Joseph Jones, Joseph Knowles, Marsh, Moulsdale, Alan Preston, Thomas Taylor, Herbert Taylor, William Barber, Joseph Barber, Hugh Barber, Edwin Britch, and Pimlott ; Mesdames J. barker, Barnish, Britch, Garner, F. Houghton and Turner; Misses Beswiok, M. A. Jones, Knowles, Parnell, Platt, and G. Taylor ; judges, Messrs. William Barber, James Britch, Marsh, Lambert, and W. Hankey ; starters, Messrs. Edwin Britch, Hindley J. Jones, and T. Taylor; stewards, Messrs. William, Clayton, Walter William Hankey, Joseph Knowles, Alan Preston, Edwin Taylor, and Herbert Taylor ; band officials, Messrs. Barnish and Knowles, and Miss M. A. J ones. Mr. Houghton officiated as referee The day and Sunday school teachers assisted in the ,children's races.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LIBERAL FETE AT HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL FETE AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Pin Ideal Afternoon Amid Beautiful Rural Surroundings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MR. P. M. OLIVER ON</head>
        <p>MR. P. M. OLIVER ON</p>
        <p>The beautiful and pleasantly situated grounds at Kingsley, Hill Cliffs, the residence of Councillor and Mrs. Henry Roberts, were on Saturday afternoon the scene of a gathering at once pleasing and unique. For the first time on record the Liberals of the district held a garden fête and sports, but the innovation proved so popular that it may safely be said that the function will only be the precursor of many others of a like character. The fete was promoted by the Knutsford Division Liberal Association, and it would have been difficult to select a place more ideally suited for the purpose than the grounds which Councillor Roberts so generously placed at the disposal of the association. It was a glolious afternoon, the heat of the sun being tempered by a refreshing breeze, and one could imagine no more pleasant way of whiling away a few hours than to indulge in amusements amid such charming surroundings. Over 600 people availed themselves of the opportunity, and the event was a splendid success, thanks largely to the excellent arrangements made by the committee, under the chairmanship of Captain H. G. Roberts. Crosfieldis prize band was in attendance and played choice selec- tions in its usual admirable style. An entertaining programme of sports aroused great enthusiasm, and the other attractions included houp-la, clock, golf, Aunt Sally and bowls. A number of ladies had arranged a stall where buttonholes and other wares could be purchased. Madame Francesca gave palmistry ' and character delineations. The trimly-kept gardens were looking their best and the visitors greatly, admired the choice collection of roses. Tea was served in a large marquee, the catering being in the hands of the Winrnarleigh Cafe Company. A hearty welcome was extended to Mr. P. M. Oliver, prospective candidate for the division, who along with Mr. J. B. Martindale, prospective candidate for the Widnes division, addressed a meeting in the evening. Mr. Frank A. Haworth (chairman of the division executive) presided, and among others were : Councillor H. Roberts, Captain H. G. Roberts, the Rov• J. A. Payne (Knutsford ), Mr. li. N. Bickerton (one of the vice-chairmen of the executive), Messrs. J. R. Tomlinson and J. Arnold Thompson (joint hon. secretaries), Mr. H. P. Greg (hon. treasurer), Mr. 11. Cliffs ('agent), Mr. J. Longland, Mr. G. Walling ,on, , Mr. S. Albiemon (hon. secretary of the fete), and Mr. W. H. Grounds (hon. treasurer).</p>
        <p>MR. OLIVER FORGING AHEAD.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he had only had the pleasure of being in Stockton Heath 011efl before, and ou that occasion the pleasure was somewhat sullied by the result of the election which had just taken place. (Laughter.) He was glad to think that at the present time Libentlism was not going backward in that part of the division. That gathering was a striking evidence of the fact that it was progressing. (Applause.) Liberalism at the time of the last election was not at its highest point in the division. It was at one time— (applause)—and they would not forget it, but at the Limo Mr. Oliver accepted their invitation it looked as if Liberalism would have to stand a good many "shower baths." However, Mr. Oliver had werked hard, through all kinds of weather, and especially in the winter months, and he had put new heart into them. (Applause.) If Mr. Oliver was a criterion of the general feeling of Liberals in the division, he (the speaker) had very little doubt as to the result of the nest election. (Applause.) It rested, however, with the rank and file to spread Liberal views and so ensure that the Knutsford division should take its part in the fight for progress. (Applause.) LIBERAL MINISTERS VINDICATED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Owa-Rit, in a tribute to Couucillor Roberts, said that gentleman was one of the most stalwart Liberals in that part of the country—(a Voice: "Or in England ")—and he had done a great service to the cause in opening those grounds, 80 that they could spend an afternoon there. During the past year (con- tinusd Mr. Oliver), some of the Liberal Minis ters bad gone through stormy times and a period of considerable difficulty. They had been subjected to is number of gross slanders and calumnies, and it was a matter for gratitude—they ought to thank .God —that they had come through all right, that the nam3 of no Liberal Minister was tarnished or sullied to-day, that the voice of slander bad been proved to be false, and that every charge of corruption had fallen to the ground. (Applause.) If similar slanders had been launched against Conservative Ministers and had been proved to be false, he (Mr. Oliver), although be was a Liberal, would have thanked God that they were false, because be was an Englishman before he was a Liberal—(applause)—and, whether our Ministers were Liberals or Conservatives, all true Englishmen must desire to see their names untarnished, and to know that they wore above the slightest taint of cor- ruption. IgNe knew that now, and it behoved Conserva- tives, as well as Liberals, to rejoice that the name of an English statesman still staod before the world on the highest pinnacle of honesty and integrity. (Applause.) HOME RULE FOR A THREEPENNY BIT.</p>
        <p>The Government had been passing through several measures, under the Parliament Act, and some people might have felt it was a rather slow process. If the old stories had had to he re-told and the old fires re-kindled ; if the old jealousies and suspicious had had to he met again, it had been wholly and solely the fault of the [louse of Lords, who had continually stood</p>
        <p>in the way of the national aspirations of Ireland and Wales. (Hear' hear.) The Home Rule Bill had pasted the House of Commons a second time, and had been thrown out on each occasion by the House of Lords Next year be supposed the same thing would happen, but then, whether the House of Lords willed it or not, Home Rule would go through and we should have an Irish Parliament in Duktlin. (Applause.) The Con- servatives had two bogies with which they tried to frighten first our pockets and then our hearts. They said Home Rule was going to cost us a great deal of money. It was true that Ireland was a poor country and did not pay her way, and that in the fltst year Home Rule would cost us £500,000. That was equal to a threepenny bit each. (Laughter.) If we were going to win the gratitude and loyalty of Ireland for a three- penny bit, he thought it, was well worth doing. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>TORY EXAGGERATIONS.</head>
        <p>TORY EXAGGERATIONS.</p>
        <p>During the Altrinchatu election the Conservatives said Home Rule would cost us £2,000,000, or a shilling each, but Mr. Hamilton eventually had to admit that it would cost only £500,000. At Leicester, the Conserva- tives made it even worse, for they said the cost of the measure was going to be £6,515,000 and that every family in Leicester would consequently have to pay extra taxation to the tune of 15s. Id. a year. Natur- ally the poor Englishman thought that was rather a lot to ask him to pay, until it was pointed out that if Home Rule was going to cost that much per family, e.ich fittni;y would IFtve to consist of a man, a wife and sixty children —(laughter)----in which event, the fortu- nate father would receive £OO in maternity benefit— (fiughter and applause)—so that he would not be very badly off, after all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>"LOYAL" ULSTER.</head>
        <p>"LOYAL" ULSTER.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives bad much to say about loyal Ulster. Well, Ulster had beau with us a long time and bad always done what it was doing now. The old Irish poet, Thomas Moore, wrote eighty years ago : Ulster complains that, being one seventh within a few fractions, Of Ireland's seven millions of hot heads and hearte; We deem it the basest of all base transactions,</p>
        <p>To keep us from murdering the other six parts. —(Laughter.) Although the Ulsterites in these days probably did not went to murder the rest of Ireland, they wanted to keep it under. That, however, was not to be tolerated. it must not be thought that the question was one which concerned Ireland alone. The demand of Ulster was in opposition rp)t only to the rest of Ireland and to England, but to every self-governing colony in the British Empire. It was also in opposition to the desires of the other great English-speaking nations beyond the seas. In the United St Ass and in our Colonies there were millions of Irishmen whose fathers and grandfathers left their own country in the time of its sorrow and poverty. The bitterness which was felt by. those who emigrated in the days of the famine and the ejections was deep down in the hearts of their descendants to-day, and they were waiting for England to do some deed of reparation to lrelaud and to give her the Home Rule she desired. Those who looked—perhaps not in the very distant future—for a world peace, those who wished, at any rate, to find an alliance between the English-speaking nations of the world, realised that Hume Rule was I essential to the accomplishment of that noble purpose. It was, therefore, not simply a national or even an imperial matter, but an international and world matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TO PREVENT RURAL TYRANNY.</head>
        <p>TO PREVENT RURAL TYRANNY.</p>
        <p>Some people thought the Government were only marking time, but they would find that before next autumn they would be. steaming full speed ahead. There was a little place calle4 the House of Lords to be tackled. (Laughter, and a Voice: "Down with them.") Ho did not think there would be the same House of Lords existing berore long. (Hear, hear.) Then there was the question of land and the housing of the people. They would probably have seen a most remarkable statement by Mr. Runcimen. president of the Board of Agriculture, with regard to rural housing. The plan had not yet been fully matured, hut Mr. Runciman had realised that if we wanted to keep a proper countryside and a real peasantry we must have better houses than</p>
        <p>Messrs. P. M. OLIVER, candidate for the K MARTINDALE, candidate for the Widnes Division ; ALBINSON (secretary).</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. mtsford Division ; 11. ROBERTS (the host) ; J. B 1. G. ROBERTS (chairman of fête committee) ; 5</p>
