-,:-:::'.:',VAII::::::::::';',...!::,:;111:01.....'":'''';'-:•..1.:::..:.',.i.;:::.111:11111.01 ....:!0pi041.,i.:1,:,,::::, -..;.: H'...,:::: .•,111::::111,51,' ~(:,,,` '+7',..'i.:..,1.1:).!::::.'11 :;411iuL.',.:::'''.,Otilliti;,:k.:: .. -.7.,,;:i.';.-itile:Z:.., •:.....' .-......:..1,::'''':::.'::.1:;:1-.4iijig 11i241:::1tt'.4-1-itE;ik. •..::::.:,:..t '..,,:",..,',.....:::..1t.1'•1!?':-:;:.;;;Vsf.§--....Z:::::l;io.:Jiciii.li I i7Ar4, .1' -11. :E.itA';'4...!!:!'!l-'."':'liiq:kl:li:'i':fii;::::Z'!:Nil. '. i„:::o..:::•'''itAlil'i-71,L:?f K.::....;!:'"ti:ittl.':..i':'liH.F.'7:',...F.:,.4:,;::ij!r::::,i:'.'-,it'.;:'.'-':':!;,,'";.,a:::,il::::!::::.::::..:::uge:::.,', A.!..;.' ~. ...$T.t::.b.i..'...Tik.ii.K..::::;,..,;,4i1:::!;.;::::::.ak''.4:::0:-;...4.4,t......;;:ii.4,%4',..:4•"-i15.&amp;::,..-,,,,--,..-- ::,,,,..::::.--.:.-..i.:,..t....,.....,4it!...'E:.:::::::•.t5k'.:,:,;:;,.„:,a.,,i,1.:4747,;;;.::::,!i11t1itzi5:11::5........."':.'.-t'.."-:'. :::::::::::::....;::1:.'ill;•-•....„,i::.,:,...::.i.„,,,i,,,:...t;i:,,,,..'7.,...:.:.01:::.,:ago;:iii....:,:L-4.sitim•:,-:,..:,,-,..,,..,-•... 1 it it I 1 ...,..,.•...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LAND AND HOUSING.</head>
        <p>LAND AND HOUSING.</p>
        <p>at present. (Hear, hear.) He had also realised that, with the present high cost of materials, the only way to get, decent houses was to build many of them, and many were required in this part of the country. Dis- cussing the reasons for the provision of more cottages, Mr. Oliver said they wanted houses with a plot of land attached to them, so that the farm labourer might have a chance of bettering his economic position. It was not merely the plural voter, but the worker on the land who ougnt to have some stake in the country. The cottagas were also necessary for social reasons, in order that the occupants might feel free from any fear of oppression. Wbenever he said that, there were always good Conservatives who pitched into him as hard as possible, and asked to be furnished with names and details of any cases of political tyranny. Ho (Ms. Oliver) would tell any Conservative this : that in a part of the division, not, far from there, when a general election came on, they would find by the roadside nice, convenient v,n. It was the Lineral committee room. The foxes had holes and the birds of the air had</p>
        <p>nests, hut in that village there wete many Liberals who, at least, had a back kitchen, and none of them due let the Liberals have a committee rooiu. (A Voice : "They would get their notice if they did.") They would, and everybody knew it. There never would be a free peasantry in this country until they were able to live in homes where they knew they were perfectly safe. Under Mr. Runoirnan's scheme that would be ensured. (Applause.) Lastly, improved housing was necessary for moral reasons. There were some things about which we did not talk very mum] in England, because we wishel to be absolutely respectable. .We sometimes passed by, like the Lavite, on the other side, leaving great evils untouched. Liberals could not always afford to live in a drawing-room, and when there were evils that wauted tackling and remedying those evils must be talked about. (Hear, hear.) Only the previous day he was speaking in a part of that division, near some picture:que cottages. Grown-up families were living in thorn, yet each house had only 003 bed- room. They could not rear an imperial or moral race under such conditions. (Hear, hear.) The Government intended to tackle the land and housing problem next autumn, and Cheshire, as a county whiila needed such reform, ought to give them all possible assistance. He hoped, when the time came, that he would have an opportunity of helping to place the needs and demands of the county before Parlovaent. (Applause.) NOT TO FORSAKE KNUTSFORD</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Mr. Oliver said : I don't know whether I am letting out any secret, but I will tell you this : that I have had one or two tentative offers from other constituencies, and only a few days ago I had only to say" Yes" and I might have got what people cill a rather easier seat than Knutsford. lilt I did not say " Yes "—(applause)—and I won't say " Yes " (applause.) Why ? Becsuse I do not want to go to Westminster? No, übt that at all, because, when these questions come on, in which I am keenly interested, when we have our reform of the housing system, I want to be there and in at it. (Applause.) It is not because Ido not want to be there that I refused, and will refuse, but becaum this is the county in which I live, the county which I know, the county which I love, and for which I wish to work. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TORY " LEADERS."</head>
        <p>THE TORY " LEADERS."</p>
        <p>Mr. MARTINDALE, in the course of a vigorous and racy speech, said they were s.itisfied with the Govern- ment,and they knew, by the signs around them, that the great majority or the people were still satisfied with Vie Government which had given them seven years of plenty. When the election took place, although Mr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CHANGING A TORY ATMOSPHERE.</head>
        <p>CHANGING A TORY ATMOSPHERE.</p>
        <p>Councillor iiOBRIITS, who was received with cheers and the singing of" For he's a jolly good fellow," said that was the first gathering of a festive character of the Liberals of that district. They were making history, and he hoped that was only the first of many similar gatherings, each larger than the last, and each more successful in helping to bring about that which they all so urgently desired— the return of a Liberal candi- date, in the person'of Mr. Oliver. (Applause.) It was suggested to him (Councillor Roberts) ten years ago, when he 'came into that somewhat Conservatively coloured district, that the atmosphere was SO preponderatingly blue that in due time he would prob- ably be found coloured in the same way. (Laughter.) Ho had not much sympathy with the kind of animal which took its colour necessarily from its surroundings, and he was inclined to think that if the atmosphere in which one lived was not particularly conducive to one's own comfort, it was one's duty to try and change the atmosphere, not to allow the atmosphere to clomp him. (Hear, hear.) As far as he could, he was trying to change the atmosphere of that district—(laughter and applause)—but he could only do a little. Whatever Mrs. Roberts and he had done on that occasion was an extremely small matter, compared with the indefatig- able work which the committee responsible for the work had done during the past few months. He took great encouragement from the efforts they had put forward, believing that, whatever good account Liberalism in Stockton Heath and neighbourhood had given of itself in the past, it would give a tar better account in the future. (Applause.) They had a candi- date who deserved their warmest support and they had a cause better and grander than it had ever been before. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>THANKING THE HOST AND HOSTESS.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE HOST AND HOSTESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. TOMLINSON, in proposing a vote of thanks to Councillor and Mrs. Roberts, for their kindness as host and hostess, said there was no stronger upholder and defender oi Liberal principles and of progress in the Knutsford division than Councillor Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>COURAGEOUS GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>A COURAGEOUS GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>Referring to the Insurance Act, Mr. Martindale said it required tremendous, gigantic courage on the part of the Government to put that measure in operation on July 15th last year. Everybody was telling them they ought to postoone it a little longer. Was there a man who, flaying seen that Apt at work for twelve months, could say that it would have been improved if the Government had waited longer? No. After the Altriucham by-election, the" Daily Mail" declared : "No more compulsory insurance." It must, however, be recognised that compulsory national insurance was now established for all time. (Applause.) It did not matter whether they had a Liberal or Conservative Government in power ; it was impossible for them to remove that gigantio piece of machinery which was doing so much good for</p>
        <p>the nation. Over 250,000 men and women drew sick pay every week, to the amount of between £lOO,OOO and £150,000. He did not think 80,600 of those would have had anything in the way of insurance provision if it had not been for the Act. Over 500,000 people had medical benefit every week in the form of free medical attention and medicine. He computed that less than 50,000 of them were insured for medical attention in time gone by. Eighteen thousand maternity benefits were paid every week and in that way £27,000 went into workingclass homes at a time when the money cs'as much more priceless than at any other time. Nearly 20,000 consumptives had already had the advantage of sanatorium benefit, and over 12,000 of them had had institutional treatment. In addition, thousands of ' consumptives were being treated in their homes. Since the Act came into operation. over £4,000,000 had been paid by the insurance Commissioners to the approved societies for the distribution of benefits. Mr. Martindale then explained the proposals of the amend- ing Bill, one of the most important being that people who joined up to the age of sixty-five should have the same benefits as anybody else. The Tories had promised many things with regard to the Act. In some con- stituencies they were going to reduce the contributions, while in others they promised higher benefits. They were, in fact, using all the arithmetical devices which were invented to push forward the Tariff Reform move- ment. (Laughter.) THE MOTOR VOTER. - - -</p>
        <p>Dealing with the Plural Voting Bill, Mr. Martindale said the Tories said they were defending the poor man who was having his liberty filched from him—the motor voter. (Laughter). The only argument against the Bill, which was worthy of consideration, was that it did</p>
        <p>not go far enough. It was, however, better to deal with the major evil at mice, so that they could have a much truer reflection of the opinion of the country at the nest Gene al Election. ( tpplanse). Much more strenuous times lay ahead of the progressive forces. The Government had shown how they could work and they intended in tho future to write more brilliant pages in our history, which would make for a happier and more comfortable nation and for an ever prospering commerce. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BREWER'S FORTUNE.</head>
        <p>A BREWER'S FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>Estate valued at £263,347 was left by the late Mr. John Walker (86), of Osborne House, North Parade, Llandudno, Caruarvon, retired t,retver and wine and. spirit merchant, formerly of Messrs. Peter Walker and Co., 64, Duke-street, Liverpool, and of Messrs. Peter Walker and Co., Ltd., brewers, of Burton-on-Trent. The death duties will amount to over £31,000.</p>
        <p>BAPTIST BOYS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Working for "The Spiritual, Moral, and Physical Health" of its Members.</p>
        <p>The second annual concert and demonstration recently given by the officers and members of the Latchford Baptist Boys' Brigade was witnessed by a large number of friends. The Chaplain (Rev. J. S. Chedburn) presided, and reported on the work accom- plished during the past twelve mouths. He sold that according to the constitution the objects of the Brigade were "to promote the spiritual, moral, and physical health of its members, and to provide training and instruction in such ways as might fit them for being helpful to others." Those oujeets had been Eecured in no small measure during the year. Physically the lar:s had benefited by the regular drilling, the route-marches Into the country, and the gymnastic exercises provided for them; their moral character had been deveioped by the discipline which increased their capacity for obedience, and inculcated habits of cleanli- ness. It also engendered a spirit of self- revect. Their tpiritusl welfare had been rorvel by the regular religious services, which it was gratifying to note were better attended even than the ordinary parades. As chaplain it was his own supreme aim and endeavour, as the boys themselves well knew, to get them to become good soldiers of Jesus Christ. With the help of Mr. D. Hamblett a very successful beginning had been made in first-aid ambulance work, and this it was hoped would be greatly extended during the coming winter season. The spacial thanks of the organisation were due to Mr. Muttrarn, of the Warrington Gymnasium, for the invaluable service he had renderei in training the various teams for their displays that evening, and for all the time and trouble he had so generously expended on their benalf. Fle (the Chairman) was persuaded that tne Brigade had telly justified its existence, and asked for the continued sympathy and interest of the parents and friends of the b ,ys, who, by co-operating with the officers in the good work they had undertaken, would help to secure the success of the movement, and at the same time the highest welfare of the young lives in which they were interested.</p>
        <p>An extensive programme Was gone through, and the items included several displaye of musical drill by teams of boys, under the direction of Mr. Mottram ; choruses by the Brigade choir, under the leadership of Lieutenant Wilkinson ' • recitations by several of the boys ; a recital of Grays's " Elegy " by Quarter- master Appleton ; solos by Privates Isaac Wilkinson and Edward Pearce; a humorous trio by Giptain Appleton and Lieutenants Wilkinbon and Banks ; and a very clever and laughable sketch by Captain Fernley and Lieutenant Homey. Mr. Mottram gave a capital exhibition of club-swinging, and had to respond to an enthusiastic recall. _ _</p>
        <p>The proceedings terminated with a grand finale, the boys, to the number of over fifty, being massed in a pyramid on the platform and united with the audience in singing the National Anthem. The proceeds of this function were devoted to the fund for the summer camp, which will be held, as _last</p>
        <p>year, at Whitley, by the kind invitation of Air. John IL Wilkinson.</p>
