      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RECTOR AND THE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND THE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Left to right : Standing :—Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Reeves, Miss Bridge (Ist prize), Rector, Miss Silccck- (2nd prize), Mrs. Tilling, Mrs. Owen. Sitting : —Mrs. Frank Webster, Mrs. Walter Dennett.</p>
        <p>The ladies' bowling main in connection with the Parish Church Men's Bible Class was held on Satur- day on the green at the Rectory. The Wf ather pros- pects in tlis morning gave cause for uneasiness, but as the day wore on they improved and the afternoon and evening was all that could be desired. Some of the ladies bowled with splendid judgment and in many instances very close " ends " were witnessed. An interval was called at 5 o'clock and the parties par- took of tea on the lawn, the catering being again kindly undertaken by Miss Hapkinson and Miss Reeves. Bowling was resumed after tea with more keenness than ever, and ultimately the following ladies were successful in winning the special prizes :—Miss Bridge, Miss i!cock, Mrs. S. Tilling, Misq Reeves, Mrs. Owen, Mrs. Walter Dennett, Mrs. T. Wilkinson and Mrs. P. Webster. A pleasing addition to the main</p>
        <p>this year was that every lady who took part received a minor prize. Mr. It. W Antwis, in his opening remarks, said it was his ambition last year that another year every lady who entered the main should receive a prize if possible, and he was very pleased to say his ambition had been realised through the kindness of many friends. He had much pleasure in calling upon the Rector, who was always willing to come amongst them, to present the prizes. The Rector in a humorous speech presented the prizes Mr. J. Hill proposed a vote of thanks to the Rector. This was seconded by Mr. S. Tilling and heartily carried. Mr. Walter Dennett proposed that their best thanks be given to the donors of prizes. Mr. T. J Hopkins seconded, uncut was carried with applause. The caterers and members of the committee and club were also thanked for providing a real afternoon's enjoyment for the ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Association.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN ANGLERS.</p>
        <p>The reports from the different wfoters during the NvFels htt,e out very bright, in tact, not one specimen has been regist All interest at present seems to be centred 'Li toe contests</p>
        <p>SUNDAY'S MATCH. Tie o7enine match of the sea:on takes place to-moriow (Sunday) at Hartford. Competitors will fish the Newbridge length, commencing from the first gate. Conveyances leave the Wadstone Arms, Bay Horse and Cuopet's Arms at S a.m. Tie draw will take place at 12 noon, and fishing will begin at 12.30 and continue until 2.30. The return journey will be made at 6.30. Members who are not in benefit will not be allowed to compete. Entries close to-night. NORTHERN ANGLERS' CHAMPIONSHIP MATCH. All members who intend entering this contest must pay an additional entry of 6i. when entering for the opening match. The contest is on the same lines as last year—ane out of every five to go to the centre match, and one out of every five to the final. Example: twenty-five entries, five top weights to centre match, five in centre match, one in final. The members who succeed in entering the final will be certain of a prize that will cover all expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LAWN TENNIS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>CAUSEWAY v. WINNINGTON PARK.</p>
        <p>The Causeway played their return fixture with Winnington Park on Saturday, the first team losing by the odd match at Wilderspool and the second team winning by 'Lliree matches at Winnington Pars. Scores : CAUSEWAY IST V. WINNINGTON PARE: IST. GENTLEMEN'S DOUBLES. W. Woods and IS. It. Gandy lost to Misses Cain and Caldwell,3-6, 2-6 ; beat Misses Davies and Williams, 3-6, 6-1. 6-1 ; and Misses Capper and Hughes, 6-0, 6-3. C. E. B. Sutton and R. Harding 135 t to Misses Davies and Williams, 4-6, 5-6 ; Cain and Caldwell, 2-6, 3-6 ; Capper and Hughes, 4-6, 5-6. H. Bibby and W. Greene beat Capper and Hughes, 6-1, 6-2 ; lost to Davies and Williams, 4-6, 3-6; Cann and Caldwell, 4-6, 6-4.</p>
        <p>LADIES' DOUBLES.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hope and Miss Wiiton beat Miss Maltby and Miss Williams, 6-1, 6-5 ; Mrs. Hughes and Mrs. Hodge 6-0, 6-5. Nirs. Hawkesworth and Mrs. Woods lost to Mrs. Hughes and Al iss Hodge, 5-6, 5-6; and beat Miss Maltby ann Miss Williams, 6-0, 6-2. Result :—Causeway won 6 events 13 sets and 133 games; Winnington won 7 events 15 sets and 115 games. CAUSEW AN 2ND TEAM V. WINNINGTON :PARK 2ND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>GENTLEMEN'S DOUBLES.</head>
        <p>GENTLEMEN'S DOUBLES.</p>
        <p>E. Hawkesworth and H. Jones beat Messrs. Newel and Brocklehurst, 6-5, 6-1 : Dien and Laycock, 6-0 6-1 ; Leicester and Dare, 64, 6-2. J. Cox and %V. Minards lost to Newell and Brockle- burst, 3-6, 4-6 ; beat Dobell and Laycock, 6-1, 6-4 ; and Leicester and Clare, 6-2, 6-0. H. Massink end S. H. Smith lost to Newell and Brocklehurst, 6-2, 5-6, 3-6 ; beat Dobell and Laycock, 6-2, 6-1 ; and Leicester end Clare, 6-0, 670. LADIES' DOUBLES. .</p>
        <p>Miss Smallwood and Mr.?. Bibby lost to Mrs. Picker- ing and Miss Williams, 1-6, 3-6 ; Miss Burton and Miss, Wilkinson, 3-6, 1-6. Miss G. Wilson and Miss D. Isaac beat Mrs. Picker- ing and Miss Williams, 6-5, 6-2 ; lost to Miss Burton and Miss Wilkins;n, 1-6, 2-6. Result :—Causeway won 8 matches, 17 seta and 128 games ; Winnington won 5 matches, 10 sets, and 89 games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB v. ST. HELENS RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>This match was shot on the Appleton range on Saturday. The conditions were : Six aside ; ten shots at 25, 50, and 100 yards. Highest possible score, 300. Scores:—</p>
        <p>-Appleton. 25yds. 50yds. 100yds. Total. C. P. Day lOO ... 95 ... 93 ... 288 F. J. Grocott J. Hartley S. James I. Broady O7 ... 92 ... 91 ... 280 F. C. Benni on BB ... 91 ... 96 ... 273 --- 1685</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>St. Hen.</head>
        <p>St. Hen.</p>
        <p>A. E. Rigby 92 ... 98 ... 93 ... 283 H. Hyde W. H, Male W. T. Cos 94 ... 90 ... SG ... 270 F. Smith J. H. Fearnley 9l ... 86 ... 75 ... 252 1635</p>
        <p>Appleton won by 50. DARESBURY v. HALTON.</p>
        <p>The Daresbury club entertained Halton on Saturday the conditions of competition being : Shootiog at`.2 5, 50, and 100 yards range, eight men a-side, the best six to count. A pleasant and keenly contested game was witne?zed, Daresbury winning only by 14 points. Tea xas provided in the pavilion by Mrs. it. P. Booth. Scores :</p>
        <p>Daresbury. 25 50 100 T'tl. George Wallworth, capt. J. Havenscroft C. Ryder D. Priestley lOO 97 93 290 Arthur Ryder J. Ryder, jun 94 94 93 281 J. Tickle W. S. Adams Halton.</p>
        <p>25 50 100 T'tl. W. Ray, wt. S. Row linson Fughes IV. Forster</p>
