        <p>J. Johnson P. Barrow C. Ryder and J. Tickle for Dareshury and W. Ray tnd W. Forster far Halton were counted cut.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>HOWARD DAVIS.</p>
        <p>The New Three-quarter For Warrington F.C.</p>
        <p>SUPERIORITY OF N.U. FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howard Davis who has, as we announced last week, been secured by the Warrington F.C., is 25 years of age. He was born May 12, ISSS. He is Gft. and weighs 12st. 31b. He began to play Rugby football at the age of ten, on joining King Edward's Grammar School, Aston, Birmingham, and his first game bid fair to be his last, as he was placed in the pack and received a severe kick in the lower region of the chest which put him in bed on slops for some weeks. He has never played in the pack since that time.</p>
        <p>After persuading his parents to let him play again, by telling them after the match, his schooldays passed vuthout further injury. During tbis period he had the pleasure of scoring 14 tries in one match. The team won by over 120 points and he never wishes to play in such another game He does not care to refer to a certain match in which Coventry participated last season with a similar result.</p>
        <p>At 16 years of age he Pined the Old Edwardians and secured a place in the first team during his second season. Not much chance of county honours came their way as they were not sufficiently powerful to attract attention. There was much hard and interest- ing football, nevertheless, and he judges opioion of his ability by the strenuous opposition to his joining the Coventry club. This be did, as he could not afford to be a strict amateur at the time, and he has never regretted the change, Northern Union football being, in his opinion, infinitely superior to the Rugby Union code Its rules are more up-to-date, its players better trained and superior in tactics. During three seasons with Coventry Howard Davis has played against every team at present in the Northern Union League. He has turned out against Warrington on four occasions, the results being a draw, a win and two losves. He hopes to enjoy playing with them as much as he did against them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Warrington Club's Lancashire Combination Fixtures.</head>
        <p>Warrington Club's Lancashire Combination Fixtures.</p>
        <p>The Warl-ington Football Club has arranged the following fixtures in connection with the Lancashire Combination :- 1913 Sep. 6 Leigh away 8</p>
        <p>13 Leigh Shamrocks 20 Broughton Rangers 27 Swinton Oct. 4 St. Helens 11 Oldham home 18 25 Rochdale Hornets home Nov. 1 8 Leigh Shamrocks 15 Broughton Rangers 22 Widnes away 29 Dec. 6 Salford home 13 Pemberton 20 Wigan 25 Leigh home 26 Oldham away 27 Salford 1914 Jan. 1 Runcorn 3 Hudoerafield 10 Wigan Hignfield 17 Huddersfield 24 Runcorn 31 Wigan Feb. 7 14 Swinton 21 St. Helens 28 Mar. 7 St. Helens Rees, away 14 Pemberton 21 28 Rochdale Hornets Apr. 4 St,. Helens Rena... home G.F. 10 Wigan Highfleld (11 a.m.) away 11 Widnes E.M.13 )8 25</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, truly 26, 1913, THE CYCLISTS NOTE BOOK.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, truly 26, 1913, THE CYCLISTS NOTE BOOK.</p>
        <p>News from the Isle of Man—but not Douglas or Ramsey. -- LOVE OF " NATURALNESS. "</p>
        <p>[BY_" STLVER-STREET."7,</p>
        <p>lam writing these notes from the t` Lil 'slap" heautitul Mona. I dare say those who have followed this note book vt ill be able to guess they are not being written from Douglas, cr Ramsey either. For although I stayed more than once at the latter place when I paid my first visit to the island, it was then a fishing town and not spoiled with too many fair grounds. Now it is a second Douglas, on a con- temptible scale, and gr ,ws more 8) every year. There are two towns on the Isle of Man that are still unspoiled to the visitor who loves naturalness rather than nature, one is Peel, the other Pelt St. Nary. The first has been our choice this year, and as we have come here for a holiday and a complete change we have left our cycles behind us and are doing every- , thing on shanks' mare. And after all, it would he difficult to use a cycle to go over Corrin's Hill and Contrary Head to Glen Maye and Niatbyl Point. Climbing up 300 feet along sheep paths to the edge of he western cliffs the track goes at that elevation with little variation for three miles to Glen Nave, when a descent to the shore is required. It is quite a novel experience for a Lincastrian to watch birds' backs as they are flying, but it gives you a good idea how aeroplanes ought to act as you watch the figure skating (flying, I mean) of the gulls and kittiwakes, and the taring rush pist of the gannets and shags (cormorants) as ou sit at your ease on that dizzy height and envy them their pocier cf just leaning backwards on the ledge to take a volplane do,vu to some friend on a rock two hundred feet below.</p>
        <p>Of course, there is a deal of screaming over the business, but what sort of a row would 100 aeroplanes make? THE GULLS AND THE HERRINGS.</p>
        <p>By the way, have any of my readers ever watched a seagull swallow a herring whole? This morning I was watching the trawlers—steam and sailing—come in from the grounds into the harbour, and the fisher- men immediately set to work picking the herrings and mackerel out of the meshes of the nets. Sometimes a fish gets torn in the process of pulling its gills out of the mesh and over it has to go. As often as not it will be caught before it reaches the water. Caught broldside, it is twisted round in the mandibles of the bird's yellow beak until the head or tail comes fore- most. Then the fish disappears. It is the finest illustration of how to get a piano through a sitting- room doorway, only in this case the doorway has to stretch, and stretch it does. Then after settling a distance off in the water to wash the breakfast down, it flies to the beach, faces the wind and there stands and thinks about what it has done, and digests it, I suppose. When the cast-off herring reaches the water the fun begins. If the first gull gets hold of it endways all is well, :Alt if 'orca-lside, gull number A never swallows it. It is whipped off by B and so passes to letter 11 before you, as spectator, can tell where it is. If H manages to grip it conveniently there is an end of it, and all but one go to another boat ready to struggle, if need be, again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>KIPPERING.</head>
        <p>KIPPERING.</p>
        <p>The kippering process, of course, is in fall swing at this town. To watch a row of eight fine fisher lasses cutting open the fish and cleaning them is a lesson in smartness The entrails are placed in tubs ready to go for fish glue or fish manure. So nothing is wasted, and it is appetising to see how cleanly the fish are prepared before being smoked. Peel is a delightful place, and as we found Appledore in Devon last year, there is not a single dirty house, no matter how narrow the streets, and some of them are not motoring thoroughfares, especially those leading down to the quay. And every window contains flower- ing plants, generally pelarginiurns and geraniums ; and I saw yesterday one of the finest amaryllis in a house window I ever saw.</p>
        <p>I had fo'rgotten that these are cycling notes. Oh, yes, I have spent more than one week in the Isle of Man with my steed, and found it just the right place for a cycling holiday, and a real holiday. Roads good, especially since the races were established here; hills net too bad, and scenery, especially that made accessible by the machine, all that can be wished for. The glens are tbis year lovely, after model spring. especially Glen Maye, Glen Wyllin and others on the west or far side of the island, and as long as the fair weather lasts the passage is all right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CHESHIRE REs r HOUSES.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE REs r HOUSES.</p>
        <p>One of the best and most reasonable of the public- houses in Delamere Forest is the Abbey Arms, Dela- mere, on the main road from Chester to Manchester, and two miles this side of Kelsall. The cross road runs from Tarporley to Hatchmere. and so the inn stands in a fairly central position. Anyone who takes a meal there should ride to the Eastern outskirt of Kelsall village, and inquire direotions to obtain access to " the look-out.' It is a vell laid-out plantation on the brow of a hill overlooking the plain of Chester, and a splendid view is obtained of the city and its surroundings. Of course, the far view is bounded by the hills of the Clwydian Range in Denbighshire, with Moel Famma and its George 111. tower, the highest point in the view. The look-out is a good objective for an afternoon's cycle outing, and is not the least of Delamere's many attractions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ELECTRICIAN'S SUICIDE. Warrington Man who said he was "Weary of Life."</head>
        <p>ELECTRICIAN'S SUICIDE. Warrington Man who said he was "Weary of Life."</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse (county coroner) held an inquest I at Warnneton on Wedneiday, on the body of James Dooley (37), electrician, 13, He lsby-street, who died on Saturday from the effects of prussic acid poisoning. Mrs. Annie Stockton, sister of deceased, with whom he had lived for 17 years, said that deceased was formerly employed by the St. Helens Cable Co., but left on his OAR account six weeks ego. About three weeks ago he complained of feeling unwell and Dr. Liston said he was suffering from nervous debility and insomnia. At the end or two weeks he said he felt better, but last week he appeared to be very depressed. He, however, never threatened to de away with him- self. He hal breakfast on Saturday, but said he , would not require any dinner. About 2.15 he went into the front room with a pint of beer and a newspaper, hut came out shortly afterwards, and exclaimed: "Oh, Annie, Ido feel ill," and collapsed on the kitchen finer. A neighbour named Thomas Catterall helped witness to get deceased on the sofa, ha he died before he could be removed to the Infirmary. Oa going into the front room she found a portico of the beer and the bottle (produced) which, it wee ascertained by Sergeant Reddington, contained prussic acid. Her brother had often said of late that the was weary of life and had commented upon the quick I manner in which Whittaker Wright committed suicide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WHERE WAS THE POISON OBTAINED?</head>
        <p>WHERE WAS THE POISON OBTAINED?</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Reddington &amp;Aid he had failed to discover where the prussic acid had been purchased. Deceased could not have obtained it at the works without the knowledge of the head chemist. Dr. Liston said there was no doubt that the man had died from prussic acid poisoning. The Coroner: The mystery seems to be where he got it from. Obtaining prussic acid is surrounded 14 so many safeguards. Detective-Sergeant Holland : In Warrington you have to obtain a note from the police and a ‘4,itness has to sign with you before you can get it. The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide whilst temporarily insane," the foreman remarking that they ! would 'lite to know where deceased obtained the poison.</p>
      </div>
