        <head>4e, •.• . :4 -.7 , • • ..;,i,•-••</head>
        <p>4e, •.• . :4 -.7 , • • ..;,i,•-••</p>
        <p>have been proving their value all over the globe. That they are a useful preventive of disease is an undoubted fact: since they exert upon the organs of digestion and assimilation —stomach, liver, kidneys and bowels—both a tonic and a corrective influence, it follows, naturally, that the functions of nutrition are more perfectly exercised, and thus the con- stitutional resistance to the onset of disease is largely increased. The absence of bilious- ness, indigestion, constipation, and all the ills for which such complaints are responsible, is an essential to the establishment of health, and the consequent enjoyment of life. The curative properties of Beecham's Pills in this connection</p>
        <p>Are of Great Val4Le.</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in boxes, price (56 pills) &amp; 29(168 pills).</p>
        <p>ARE YOU BEING POISONED BY URIC ACID?</p>
        <p>DO YOU have Rheumatism, Sciatica, Lumbago, Backache? They are caused by URIC ACID. To all who say " Yes " to any of the above questions we offer that safe and efficient remedy, • MARTHU." " MARTHU " searches out the Uric Acid poison, dissolves i' t and expels it. from the system. " MARTHU " is not a secret preparation—your doctor knows its value. " AJAR-1111; " is used in many hospitals, we guarantee it to do you good. " MARTHU " is the one unfailing remedy for Gout, Rheumatism, Lumbago, Eczema, Gravel, Headache, Backache, Stone, and all ailments of the Kidneys. "TAKE 'MARTHU' AND THROW OFF YOUR PAINS." Sent post free on receipt of P.O. for 2/9 by— THE MARTHU Co., ABERYSTWYTH</p>
        <p>A NEW CODE OF RULES FOR GOLF. May we look for a good time coming, and do we see hopes of golf under a more simple and satisfactory code of rules? It is devoutly to be wished, and there is evidence that, at all events, some other people than the members of the official Rules of Golf Committee are about to set their hands to plough this thankless furrow, in the following words which we find in the paper which devotes itself exclusively to golf : " The main fabric [of the rule-1 is sound enough, and all that is required is restoration. By the simple process of lopping off the pettifogging excrescences that have been added since 1882 the code could be reduced to reasonable and rememberable proportions. and, at the same time, be made to show a simpler, saner and manlier game. In the coming months we hope to show how this can be done." A " pettifogging excrescence " sounds like a growth of very terrible activities, but we may look to the coming months to produce a more healthy and normal offshoot; and in any case this is what we have been desiring to see for a long while—not quite so much criticism of the acts of the Rules Committee, but something a little more creative. Even if the new act of creation is to consist mainly of the lopping off of useless excres- cences, it will be interesting to see what kind of a coda results from it. Of one thing the off-lopper may rest securely assured. that if he can produce a code which is more simple and more sane than that evolved by the multifarious labours of the Rules Committee, the members of that Committee will be the first to congratulate him on a really remarkabla achievement, and to accept his improved and revised version with all gratitude. What there is greater reason to expect, than a more perfect code devised by his single brain, is that it may prove a basis on which a code of more simplicity may eventually be built. But after the long and sad experience of the past, even that expectation is severely chastened by incredulity. —" Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOLBOYS' RECORD.</p>
        <p>Some remarkable records of school attendance were reported at the Runcorn Parish Churel school on Wednesday, when the awards were distributed by Mr. J. Littler. It was stated that there were 126 boys who had made a complete year's attendance. Five of them were credited with full attendance for seven years, eight fc,r six years five for five years, sixteen for four years, and twenty for two years. Gold and silver medals were awarded to the following boys for special distinction in attendance :—Emil A. Lawrence. William Peacock, Atthur Scott, James Atherton, and Frank Riley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>' OHOIR ON STRIKE.</head>
        <p>' OHOIR ON STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Dissatisfaction among the adult members of Holy Trinity Church, Runcorn, has resulted in a strike. The choir, which is entirely voluntary, numbers between thirty and forty, and is composed of men and boys. The choir sermons were held a week ago. Usually the Mariners' Mission Church is closed in order to swell the congregation, but on this occasion it remained open, and the choristers regard it as a want of appreciation of their services. They also consider they have a grievance in regard to the collection, for the offertories on this occasion amounted to less than £5, which worked out at 2s. per head for the choir excursion. Last Sunday all but three adult members absented them- selves. An effort was made to persuade the organ- blower to come out in sympathy, but this venture was not successful. The protest has created some surprise in the neigh- bourhood.</p>
        <p>FATAL CUP OF HOT TEA. SAD SCALDING ACCIDENT IN POOR HOUSEHOLD.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquiry at Widnes on Monday on Ernest Wilkinson, the two-year-old son of James Wilkinson, a chemic%l worker, of Irweli-street, Widnes. The deceased pulled a cup of hot tea over himself, and received severe scalds about the neck and arms. He succumbed in the Accident Hospital a few days later. When the accident happened the boy's mother was ill in bed and his father was attending to her. The Coroner remarked that people like the parents of the deceased had to do Ole best they could without servants. It was a very sad case, and he allowed the mother ..T2 out of his poor-box. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and made a collection on the parents' behalf. FUNERAL OF A VETERAN PRIMITIVE METHODIST.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at Runcorn Cemetery on Monday. of Mrs. Elizabeth Lewis, widow of the late Mr. James Lewis, formerly of Runcorn, and mother of Councillor D. Lewis, of Widnes. By her death the local Primitive Methodist Circuit ban sustained a loss. The deceased lady, who was seventy-eight years of age, had been sinco 1853 one of the most devoted members of the church. Death took place at the residence of het esughter at Uarston after a painful illness. A memorial service. at G reenway -road Primitive Methodist Chapel was conducted by the Revs. J. S White and S. Johnson. The chief mourners were Messrs. D. Lewis and S. P. Lewis (sons), Mrs. Hesketh and Mrs. Horrox (daughters), and Mrs. D. Lewis (daughter-in-law). Others present included Mrs. H. P. Showan, 'Miss Lewis, Miss Hesketh, F. J. D. Lewis, and D. Berry Lewis, Messrs. H. P. Showan, Charles Hesketh, W. Hesketh, Ernest Hesketh, Ernest Lewis, R. Hilliage and Mrs. Hilliage, Thomas Hilliage, R. Hilliage, jun , J. Leathwood, Mrs. R. Owen, Mrs. Kilcross, Mrs. J. Stubbs, Miss Hilliage, Miss NI. Hillisge, Mrs. R. Hilliage, jun., and Mrs. J. Leathwood, Mr. and Mrs. StreaL, Councillor and Mrs. T. Showan, and Mr. and „Mrs J. H. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RACECOURSE LIBEL SUIT.</head>
        <p>RACECOURSE LIBEL SUIT.</p>
        <p>The long-drawn-out libel suit in which Mr. Wootton, a trainer of racehorses, sued Mr. Robert S:ever, London journalist, for libel, came to an end on Wed- nesday, when the jury found that the words com- plained of were libellous and untrue, but written without malice, and assessed the damages at a farthing, lir. Justice Darling giving plaintiff judgment for that sum, with costs.</p>
      </div>
