      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>4, Museummsts, Warrington.</head>
        <p>4, Museummsts, Warrington.</p>
        <p>-'</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</p>
        <p>E PASSING WEE i‘</p>
        <p>NEVER WORKED. Cornhill is famous for other things than Tariff Reform meetings. Gatherings take place here under almost every conceivable banner and attract crowds on a more or less extensive scale. The last Tariff Reform meeting we had the privilege of seeing was attended by nine men, three women, and a constantly varying number of boys and girls, but as the speaker was delivering himself of such abshrdities as the statement that Free Trade had been powerless to prevent a rise in the price of food stuffs, the absent War- ringtonians did not miss very much. It was different with a Socialist meeting held on this classic ground on Thursday night. Three young men, thoroughly in earnest, well read in their special subject, and gifted ,in the matter of speech, which, in one particular case, was fluent as the sea, attracted an audience that would have filled any of the larger-sized assembly rooms in Warrington. There were many interruptions from the listeners, who, somewhat oddly it seemed, objected to Socialist doctrine the more as they • descended in the social scale. It may be that the broad hint that under Socialism all must work who wish to eat upset the day-dreams of these weary Willies. However, the princi- pal obstruction came from the very poorest in the crowd and the climax arrived when the fluent gentleman, to whom we have referred, I asked the workingmen to think for them- 'selves, and to compare the luxurious conditions in which their masters, whom he lumped together as capitalists, lived, with 'the horrible conditions, which he described with his own peculiar wealth of detail, in which the workers lived. The blood of one, at all events, of the audience had been brought to boiling point. "I live in better conditions than them meself, sometimes, and I NEVER work," he shouted. The laugh that followed this open and public confession lasted long enough to break up what had been a very interesting and attractive gathering.</p>
        <p>ing tempting offers from other constituencies, Mr. Oliver has no intention of forsaking the Knutsford division to which he is strongly attached. We believe that no one could more vigorously and convincingly champion the progressive cause when the next fight comes and the Liberal party is to be counted fortunate in having a standard bearer of such exceptional gifts and sound convictions.</p>
        <p>ROYAL AUTOGRAPHS. Their Majesties the King and Queen have been graciously pleased to sign, the visitor s book that is kept at the Town Hall. Time idid not allow of their doing so when they visited Warrington, but the book was recently sent to Buckingham Palace and was returned duly signed. The page upon which their Majesties were asked to make a record of their visit had been specially inscribed by Mr. Thomas Evans, son of Alderman James Evans and a member of the staff in the Borough Surveyor's department. This page, which will be on view to-day in the window of Mr. Birtles, Sankey-street, reads as follows :</p>
        <p>Visit of their Majesties King George V. and Queen Mary</p>
        <p>to the County Borough of Warrington, Monday, the 7th of July, 1913, on which occasion His Majesty the King opened Warrington Bridge.</p>
        <p>GEORGE R.I. MARY. The visitors' book was begun on January 14th, 1896, and the first signature is that of Mr. George Dakin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH WEATHERCOCK.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH WEATHERCOCK.</p>
        <p>Men have been at work this week at the very top of the Parish Church spire, which means they have been labouring at the dizzy height of 281 feet, our celebrated landmark being one of the highest spires in England. Close observers will have noticed that the weathercock, which, in normal conditions, ought to turn so readily with every wind that blows that its name is used as a figure of speech to signify a man or woman of unstable mind, has persisted in staring in one direction for months. The cause we know not, but as the cock looked towards Runcorn Gap,whence all the rain troubles of Warrington come, it may be surmised that last" summer" the bird was kept looking that way so long that he forgot how to turn when better times arrived. However that may be, it was feared that a fixed weather- cock, which presented a large surface to all the winds that blow (except two), might bring down the tip of the spire ; and steeple- jacks were sent up,to make investigations and put matters right. They found that other repairs were needed, and the Church Council sat in solemn conference on the requirements of the situation. It was suggested that a deputation should accompany the steeple- jacks aloft to get some first-hand knowledge, but the Church Council is not composed of Alderman Bennetts. Some are of such ample proportions that they questioned whether they could get near a ladder, at all events ladders so near the perpendicular as those that now lie against the church steeple; others are so slender that they might be expected to slip through the rungs of the ladder long before they reached the top. It was unanimously agreed that if the weathercock was to be interviewed the meeting would have to take place in the churchyard or some equally convenient and accessible place. It was not long before Mr. Hewitt made this possible, for the bird was brought down on Wednesday and sent to the painters to be gilded. When he is put upon his perch again he will doubtless be able to announce to the surrounding country that he has forgiven last summer its sins and forgotten the sad experience that took all power of motion out of his solitary but substantial leg. We have heard many remarks regarding the wonder- ful size of this interesting counterfeit of chanticleer, but our illustration shows that it is quite small, relatively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SWAN SHOOTERS.</head>
        <p>SWAN SHOOTERS.</p>
        <p>Many will be surprised to learn of the plentiful supply of swans that Warrington and its neighbourhood can boast. The river, the canals, Ackers pits, and, other waters have recently been visited by these most graceful birds, which appear to have been willing to settle in our midst and make our walks abroad the pleasanter for their pre- sence; but mischievous boys and those "scoundrels with guns," whose diseased imagination leads them to think that the more beautiful and rare a bird or animal may le the greater is the call upon them to shoot it, have adversely affected the desire of our visitors to stay with us. Some are prolong- ing their visit at points mentioned by Mr. Dunlop in his " Countryside " article. At least one has fallen before the ruthless aim of an alleged sportsman, and another has been damaged. It is to be hoped that any person having information that would lead to the identification of those , who, in the future, may feel impelled to shoot a swan, will have no scruples about passing on his knowledge to the Town Clerk Mr. Whittle may, with confidence, be left to do the rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WHY SHAKESPEARE WENT.</head>
        <p>WHY SHAKESPEARE WENT.</p>
        <p>The many admirers of our correspondent "A. M." recognise the seriousness of purpose that runs through all his contributions, and will acquit him, though he undoubtedly has the saving grace of !humour, of having an undue strain of levity about him. Circum- stances bring about curious combinations,and reverend signors have before now been alarmed at the way in which Mr. Mounfield has discoursed upon Warrington pubs. and cockfighting. This week the faithful his- torian turns to Shakespeare. In 1585, says Mr. Mounfield, Shakespeare was married to Anne Hathaway and had three children—a girl and then a boy and girl who were twins. Then he disappears." Then he disappears!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LIBERALS AT HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>It was a happy thought on the part of Councillor Henry Roberts to place his beautiful grounds at ' Hill Cliffe at the disposal of the Knutsford Division Liberals for their garden fete on Saturday. The function was unique, inasmuch as the Liberals of the locality had never previously embarked upon such a :festival, but its splendid success should ensure its repetition as an annual event. Mr, P. M. Oliver, who is making most encouraging headway in his candidature in this erstwhile Tory strong- hold, delivered one of those sparkling yet deeply suggestive addresses for which he has gained a reputation. The assembly heard with much gratification that, notwithstand-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FISHING IN THE MERSEY.</head>
        <p>FISHING IN THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>Those far-off days when salmon 'abounded in the Mersey at Warrington have been recalled this week by the recital, at a local property sale, of the conditions of an inden- ture by which the fishery rights in the river were specially reserved. The purchase to which the document referred was of a house on Wilderspool Causeway, facing what is now known as the old river, and at the time the deed was drawn up, it was no doubt- the pleasurable habit of the owner to betake himself to the river bank and angle for the wherewithal to replenish his tables. By the courtesy of Mr. H. J. Westbrook, we are able to print [the following condition from the deed :</p>
        <p>Save and except, out of these presents and always reserved unto heirs and assigns, all such sole and separate fishery, or other right of fishing within tbe river Mersey, adjoining to the said premises, as they or the former owners. of the said premises have exercised, and enjoyed, or of right to have exercised within the said river Mersey, or dny part thereof, as appurtenant or belonging to the said premises, together with the full, free and uninterrupted liberty and privilege, power and authority to and for them, the said their heirs and assigns, and their or either of their servants, agents, workmen, fishermen and others, by their or either of their appointment, to enter into and upon any sand bank, or sand banks which now is, or are, or hereafter may happen to be opposite the said premises, and there to draw, laud or place any boat or boats, net or nets, or other matter or things, as shall or may be necessary to be had aud used for the better and more convenient use and enjoyment of the said fishery or other right of fishing. COST OF WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The rather startling statement that the cost of Warrington Bridge, when completed, will be about £24,000 is made by the writer of an article in this week's engineering supplement of the "Times." In view of the fact that the original tender for the construction of the bridge amounted to £14,310, we have made inquiries with a view to ascertaining what, if any, grounds there are for anticipating such a greatly enhanced expenditure. In the first place, the tender was accepted subject to such modifications as the engineer might require, and the amended plans and specifications, as finally approved, involved an additional out-</p>
        <p>lay oi 41)00. We are informed that there is no reason to suppose that the structure itself will cost anything like £24,000; and the assumption would appear to be warranted that the estimate includes the cost of widen- ing the approaches to the bridge and acquir- ing premises for demolition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Presentation to Councillor Chrimes.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Councillor Chrimes.</p>
        <p>The annual outing of the Warrington -Coopers Society to Southport on Saturday was marked by a presentation to theSecr etary(Ckancillor John Chrimes), in recognition of a quarter of a century's services. The gift took the forma of a handsome gold medal, which bore the following inscription :—" July 19th. Presented to Mr. J. Chrimes, of the Warrington Coopers' Society, for valuable services rendered." Mr. Brown, president of the Warrington District, in making the presentation, said it was mainly through Mr. Chrimes' faithful Allegiance to duty that the society was in such a strong position at present. His untiring zeal had always commended itself to the general body of members, and the medal would be a reminder of the many happy days he had laboured amongst them. Messrs. Alec Baird, W. Brown, T. Newton, George Silverside, and others paid a high tribute to their secretary. Mr. Chrimes in acknow- ledging the medal, said what he bad done had ..been but his duty, and the greatest prizethe treasured was the issue of the members' good work—that the society was in a flourishing condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>SUFFRAGE PlLGRlMS,—Continued from</p>
        <p>column 6. , - when the Government had scheduled four trades? The Government itself was one o,f the greatest sweaters in the country through the contractors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WOMAN'S DISABILITIES.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S DISABILITIES.</p>
        <p>In thq eyes of the law a woman was not "a person," and she was not the parent of her own child. Not until she became a widow did she become the parent of her child; yet if she committed an offence she became very much "a person," and had to bear the penalty. When a, hus- band died intestate the wife got one-third the estate he left, but when a wife died all went to the husband. If a i;Tn or woman. stole a pe.nny it was felony in the eyes of the law; but to , ruin a girl's life was only a misdemeanour. It was a less rime to ruin a girl's life than to steal, a penny. Women, instead of being put back, ought to have a voice in the work of lifting the sex out of the gutter. .1' EQUAL PAY FOR EQUAL WORK. There should be equal pay for equal work. If women had equal pay it would mean that more men woud be emptloyed, and many women would, be able to go back to their homes. They were told that women knew - nothing about foreign affairs, Imperial affairs, and Indian af- fairs. As a matter of fact members of Parliament did not trouble, about these matters themselves, but left them to ex- perts. It was pot fair that M:P.'s should neglect the women's point of view, ;and it was in .the interest of the race that this neglect. should cease to be. (Applause.) Lady Rendlesham, in calling upon the next speaker, said Miss Waring's address was the hest she had ever heard from man or woman,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LIVING PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>A LIVING PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Against the moribund policies of the bank- rupt political party tint can do no better than fly to the colonies for a cloak to hide its nakedness, the Liberal Party can, produce a living programme, some of which has been duly presented, some of which is in the course of presentation, and some of which is to come as the crowning glory of the record that has been leading up to it. This week we have had a feast of political advances somewhat out of keeping with the hot holiday atmosphere usually associated with July. Two great measures were introduced to the Commons on Monday. Perhaps in the circumstances the Irish Land Purchase Bill ranks first, though English social reform presses hard for treat- ment. Two-thirds of the process of Irish Land Purchase have already, as MR. BIRRELL told the House of Commons, been completed. MR. WYNDHAM'S estimate of the amovnt needed has been largely exceeded, partly owing to the very generous terms which were offered to landlords. Something like sixty millions more will be required—and provided—with another million for labourers' cottages. When the Government has com- pleted its operations, the Irish labourer will be the best-housed peasant in the world. The straits to which the Opposition have been reduced is well illustrated by the policy of negation which the Lords have taken upon I themselves, with characteristic energy, to uphold. By a majority of 194 the Lords refused, as they had done with the Home Rule Bill, even to consider the Welsh Church Bill "until it had been submitted to the judg- ment of the country." In the Home Rule debate, LORD LANSDOWNE made the Con- descending announcement that if the electors declared, after being once more consulted, their wish for the Home Rule Bill, the Lords would " give it them." Aghast, almost as soon as he had made this concession to democratic institutions,at his own generosity, LORD LANSDOWNE employed his front-bench colleague, LORD CURZON, to explain that the "Lansdowne permit" really meant only per- mission to a Home Rule Bill on which the electors had pronounced approval by a sub- stantial majority, to pass its second reading. The Lords would then set to work in Com- mittee to give the country, not the Bill asked for, but a "more palatable" measure— palatable, that is, to Tory Peers and their friends. After yet further consideration, LORD LANSDOWNE gave, in the Welsh Church debate, yet one more turn to the" Lansdowne permit." The country may get more tired of the Lords than they are at the present moment if these dog-in-the-manger tactics are pursued much longer by LORD LANSDOWNE. But to return once more from the dead to the living. The other Bill produced on Monday had reference to the troubles which MR. LLOYD GEORGE'S reforms are said to have brought upon the shoulders of the building trade. These troubles are to 'be surmounted just as the Insurance Act is being amended in the light of actual experi- ence. By the Revenue Bill, the last legal loophole is closed against the possibility of increment duty being charged upon builders' profits. In the valuation of agricultural land, unexhausted improvements,such as drainage, embankments, walls, and the like, effected within thirty years prior to April 30, 1909, are to be deducted. If money is spent on roads and sewers to the amount either of the full site value or of £2OO per acre, the land is exempted from increment duty for five years. Reversion duty is to be subject to relief in cases where land was leased on favourable terms for charitable purposes, or a lease has been effected on condition that the lessee effect improvements. As to the future, we are promised an early installment of an attempt at educational justice and fair play. On Tuesday, Mr. J. A. PEASE introduced a one-clause Bill to give some immediate financial relief to local education authorities. The Bill itself is very modest, in scope, but the PRESIDENT of the BOARD OF EDUCATION asked the House to regard it, not as a substitute for, but as an introduction to, the comprehensive measure which the Government will do their best to pass into law next year. The promised Bill will do for intermediate education what the famous Forster Bill of 1870 did for elementary education. Few changes are proposed in elementary education, but the grievance of the single-school area—the most acute of N onconformist grievances—is to remedied, and local authorities will be required to provide baths, playing-fields and nursery schools, and to extend their provision for the feeding of necessitous children. There will be co-ordination of the various kinds of intermediate schools, secondary; trade, technical, and the like. Advisory Provincial Councils are to be set up in grouped areas, with administrative powers, but the big boroughs will enjoy complete autonomy. The State will be prepared to find a large and increasing additional sum yearly, while the rating 'limits on local authorities will be abolished.</p>
        <p>H</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
