      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SUFFRAGE PILGRIMS. How the Non-militants have Approacbed London for To-day's Demonstration.</head>
        <p>SUFFRAGE PILGRIMS. How the Non-militants have Approacbed London for To-day's Demonstration.</p>
        <p>RESPECT FOR THE LAW.</p>
        <p>[BY A LADY CORRPPONDENti The Suffrage Pilgrims are now nearing their, journey's end and to-day (Saturdl the huge army which invades from all over England will meet in - Park from the four quarters of our ! try. There will be about twenty differeu platforms, each decorated with banner: telling from what part of England theb.l6 come. No doubt the platfOrms will arranged in a huge circle, each platfor; representing a Federation, i.e., Lona° platform, Ridings of Yorkshire Platf°l3l; Lond.odo and so on.. Each will have a chairtil. 0 and different speakers. The countrie which have already granted the suffril% to women will be represented. There,:jg Ito be rather a unique way of colleotiiho money, so I hear, and this money Avi used in the autumn and winter for ' paganda work. So far, everything has gone off in c1:115 a .fine style. There have been OserTeci accidents, and everybody has nWe'btis. quite gaily ankcheerfully. No one given up out of weakness or lack of svble, No vehicle has broken clown, arid I T 0 lieve the women from Lancashire Its seen fun in everything; nothiug daunted their resolute spirits. rl''ed t. have had also an astonishing way of gis ting on good terms with surly village;t; At times they have been pelted Wid eggs, tomatoes, flour; in fact all kindsa, things, but in many cases, let us Iworip these would-be warriors couldn't</p>
        <p>straight. The late Mayor of Oldham has tra've'dy with other ladieS in a caravan, and Ltaor, Rochdale, although she has had a 111„°"00 has marched with the best nearly a",„0, way, and used her motor-car to carrY gage and literature. The people on oj way have been most hospitable, aof when they found out that the $011,374,03 marchers were not militants, they listened to the addresses in real earrie;.s The word "law-abiding" on the banll,,ney must have appealed to them. In 1.11';0 cases the different speakers have iripo cheered vigorously. When the travej,7l3g have been getting tired ihey have °B-1.0, their marching hymn, which was cf,4l- posed by Miss Brown, one of the Pla;110 ers. It was sung to the tulle of 00.4 Brown's Body, and one of the verses 111 as follows</p>
        <p>" Comrades lift your tired eyes Unto the hill and see • Mist and clouds and prejudice</p>
        <p>Rising_ rapidly:"</p>
        <p>til°</p>
        <p>As a soc-iety- we have been hoping many good results will come from pilgrimage. There are so many Pe9Ppsi', who do not know the difference betwevi': the militant branches and our own y abiding societies that we trust thCTg speeches which have. been given (11, dle past month in various towns and vtia: ages will bear good fruit and sbov the people of England that there,.ogi other women (even if they do nQt bombs and damvge iiroperty) who CO fp deadly earnest and require the vdt' better the conditions of women's life Li labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MISS WARING CREATES A GOO° IMPRESSION IN EAST ANGLIA.</head>
        <p>MISS WARING CREATES A GOO° IMPRESSION IN EAST ANGLIA.</p>
        <p>Legal Sex Anomalies. of It was a very wet but cheerful Fal suffrage pilgrims, says the East Anic-ite Daily Times, who arrived at the Hart Hotel, Hotel, Wickham Market, OI day afternoon on the way to L011(10'310 - "at home" was given by Lady' Ile ham, and after .tea her. ladyshiP 'who duced Miss Waring, of Warrington' od' was in charge of the party form ham. -</p>
