      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>t Brat Jinttilignre</head>
        <p>t Brat Jinttilignre</p>
        <p>Alexandra Day.</p>
        <p>The final meeting o! the Alexandra Day Committee will be held at tht, Infirmary next Tuesday at half- p.tst two. All helpers are invited to attend. Audit of the Corporation Accounts.</p>
        <p>The first. GovernmeQ, audit of the Warripgton Cor poratioo Accounts, tinder the Warrington Corporation Act, 1011, began ou Thai sday. Stakton Heath Garden oc!ety.</p>
        <p>This enterprising scciety will hold its annual Show ju nd,A.spool Park on August Bank Swien see excellent attractions will be presented. Museum Wild Flower Table.</p>
        <p>The exhibits on tlie table This vitek-end make an exceedingly fine display. Alti,getJier ihere are 201) species of our lec.a.l flora repiesented on the table. It is well with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Music in the Pai.,k.</head>
        <p>Music in the Pai.,k.</p>
        <p>_ The Borough Band will play the Park to-morrow (Sunday), conductor, Mr. T. Griffin. The iilue,Coat School Rind will play ti selection of popular music in the Park on Thursday at 7.15 p.m. I=3l Success.</p>
        <p>Mr. Percy FurnEss lioardrnan, son of Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Boardman, 14, Norris-street, Warrirgion, who rtceived isis eaily traioing at the National Schools and secondary School, has beercsucaesg.-41 in obtaining his M.Sc. degree at the University of Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Children's Camp at Prestatyn. On Saturday 132 girls left Warrii,gtrin for the Children's Summer Camp at Prestityn, and a splenoid time they have had. Mrs. Bill, Missi-s Emson, Pennington, Morley, Roberts and Grounds have been in charge of the girls. Choir Trip to Llandudno.</p>
        <p>Llandudno was the resort selected by the adult members of St. Paul's choir for their annual trip on Tuesday, and the outing proved one of the most enjoyable on record. The day was gloriously tine, and the sail down the Straits to Bangor was an exceedingly pleasureable feature of a delightful excursion.</p>
        <p>Secondary School Prize Distribution. A feature of the entertainment in connection with the Secondary School prize distribution was the excellent staging uf the two plays which were pre- sented by the scholars. The scenery had been placed at the disposal of the school by the manage- ment of the Royal Court Theatre. Priory School : Musical Successes. In the R.A.M. and R.C.M. Associated Board Examination recently held in Liverpool, the follow- ing pupils of the above school were successful:— elementary division, Gertrude Hunt (pass) ; primary division,Doris Kerfoot (bons.).</p>
        <p>Death of a Teacher. The death occurred at Abergele, on Thursday, of Miss Ada Bessie Gittins, for over twenty-five years head mistress of Abergele Schools. The deceased lady was the eldest daughter of the late Mr. Edward Gittins, stonemason, Warrington, and sister-in-law of Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Chessum. The Rev. A. J. Chetsum, the newly-appointed superintr ndellt of the Warrington Primitive Methodist Ciicuit, was hem tily welc )med at a meeting held in Legh-street Schoolroom on Thursday evening. Mr. R. Bridge (circuit steward) presided, and the speakers included the Rev. 'N. P. Jaye, Mrri. Wright, Mrs. Mills, Messrs. John Griffiths, H. 0. Bennett and Cox. Police Swimming Match.</p>
        <p>At the Corporation Baths on Thursday evening an interebting 50 yards handicap for members of the Police Force was de c.ded. The final was very excit- ing, P.C. Gibson winning the first prize (given by the Mayor) in fine style, off a 25 seconds hindicap. P.C. Witlib was second and the third prize was won by P.C. Anderson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Fall From a Roof.</head>
        <p>Fall From a Roof.</p>
        <p>John Barker (27), lodging in Tanner's-lane, and employed as a " handyman " by Messrs. Sutton Bros., of Market-staeet, was engaged in removing some decorations from the Hop Pole Hotel, on Monday afternoon when he fell from the roof, a distance of 2Oft. He was removed to tae Infirmary, where it was found that he had sustained severe internal injuries The Plcturedrome.</p>
        <p>" A fatal invention, the flying torpedo," a modern drama with a strong love story and sensational finale, will be the principal picture at the Piciuredrome for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next. For Thurs- day, Friday and Saturday, " Facii,g the footlights," a story of a wife's sacrifice, will be shown in three reels. Miss Hinny Porter play a the leading part.</p>
        <p>Esperantists at Appleton. On Wednesday a party of the local Esperanto Club went to the Dingle at Appleton, and spent the lovely summer evening in good comradeship, to which Esperanto conversation and songs ministered in no small degree. This was the last " meet" of the season, during which a number of beginners joined the Ciub. Classes will begin again on the first Wednesday in September,in the Club in Bewsey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>King and the Children's Holidays.</head>
        <p>The King and the Children's Holidays.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening, it was reported that the School Management Committee bad been informed of the request of his Majesty that an additional holiday should be given to the school children in honour of his recent visit to the borough. It was decided to defer fuither consideration of the matter until the next meeting of the committee. Death of Mrs. Eckersley.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of a well-known Warrington lady in the person of Mrs. Patience Eckersley, who passed away on Thursday, at the home of her son, Mr. John Eckersley, 30, Padgate-lane. The deceased lady, who was seventy-five years of age, belonged to a generation of tradespeople of whom few now remain. She was the widow of the late Mr. Joseph Eckersley, who many ago carried on the business of a baker and corndealer in Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>Knoeked Down by Motor Cycle. Another accident occurred on the Knntsford- road on Wednesday evening. Adam Ritchie, " Anderson Drive, Aberdeen, was riding a motor cycle in the direction of Warrington, and when near St. Mary-street, Arthur Williams, son of Thomas James Wiltiams, 11, St. Mary-street, stepped into the roadway and was knocked down. He received a cut on the forehead, and, after first-aid had been rendered, was taken to the Infirmary, where it was found by Dr. Law, the house surgeon, that he was suffering from concussion:</p>
        <p>Photographers at Aston Hall. Saturday's outing in connection with the Warring- ton Photographic Society was to Aston Hall and seven members travelled by the 2.15 p.m. train from Bank Quay to Halton. 7 hey then walked to the Hall and immediately commenced a tour of the grounds which were in splendid condition. Only colour plates could do justice to the brilliant colours of the flowers,both in the gardens and in the numerous hot houses which were inspected. The leader (Mr. J. W. Itoxvland), secured one or two good colour subjects, also one of the official group. Lady J. S. Barmood Banner kindly provided au excellent tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Workingmen's Md for infirmary :</head>
        <p>Workingmen's Md for infirmary :</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Musical Festival and Concert.</p>
        <p>Alderman Burton presided over a largely-attended meeting of the Executive Committee of the Working- men's Infirmary Committee, held in the Board Room on Wednesday night, to make the preliminary arrange- ments for the holding of the Sunday open-air musical festival in the Park and the Saturday concert in Parr Hall, that usually opens the concert season each year. It was announced that the Council had granted the use of the Park for Sunday, August 17th, and it was deckled to hold the festival on that day, the choirs of the following places of worship having consented to make up a united mixed voiced choir for the occasion— Wycliffe Congregational Church, Bold-street Wesleyan Church, Legh-street Prim itive Methodist Chapel, Brick-street independent Methcdist Church and Workingmen's Mifsion. The conductors of these, choirs, which will aggregate 100 voices, have elected Mr. W. H. Payton to conduct, a eelection that will give universal satibfaction. Croslield's Band will play selections and. lead the hymns, in which the general public will join. The concert in Parr Hall was fixed to take place on Saturday, October 11, and a small sub-committ.ee was appointed, subject to the approval of a meeting of the General Committee, to make arrangements.</p>
        <p>TENNIS, CROQUET, CRICKET 8; other outdoor Games S. GARLAND, 51,53,4% 55,Bridge st.,Warrington.—Advt</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 191 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WEDDINGS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>WEDDINGS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>Greene—tibby.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Church was the scene of a pretty wedding on Tuesday afternoon, when Mr. William Greene, 8.A.., second son of Mr. and Mrs. John Greene, 22, Crosfteld- street, was married to Miss Janet Bibby, third daughter cf Mr. and Mrs. William Bibby, 3, Arpley- street. The bride and bride4room are particularly well known and steemed in the teaching profession, and a large number of their fiends assembled to witness the ceremony. The Vicar (Ilev. W. Brace- camp) officiated. The br ide,w ho wets given away by her father,was handsomely attired in ivory sat in charmeuse, with a full Court train of ivory satin, kiedly lent by , Mrs. H. Libby, and a veil of Brustsels net embroidered ty her aster, Miss Ada Bibby. She carried a lovely bouquet of Madonna lilies. The bridesmaids were Miss Ada Bibby (siste r cf the hi ide), Miss Mary Greene (si,ter of the bridegroom), and the Misses Bessie Dickens and Connie Dickens, and Miss Dora Bibby (nieces, of the bride). The two fir st-r :lined were attired in white satin bijou. o ith hats to match, and the three junior brideAmaids wore very dainty dresses. The bridesmaios carried choice flov:ers, the two former pink carnations and the younger pink sweet peas. Miss Ada Bibby wen e a gold bangle, Miss Mary Greene a gold jendant, and each of the other brideswaids a gold signet ring—all the gifts of the bridegroom. • Mr. Herbert Greene (brother of the bridegroom) acted as best man, and Mr. Tom Libby (brother of the bride) discharged the duties of groomsman. Mr. W. Milner, a friend of the two families, was at the organ. The reception was he'd at Atkinson's Cale, Bridge- street, the guests numbering from SO to 90. The health of the newly-married couple was honoured on the proposition of Mr. J. H. Calvert, of Southport. Mr. William Greene responded. Later Mr. and Mrs. William Greene left for London and a cycle tour in the South. The bride travelled in a fawn whipcord dress, with a bat of black velvet, trimmed with ostrich feathers. After the departure of the bride and bridegroom, a whist drive and concert served to entertain the guests very pleasantly. A cablegram. was received from Mr. and Mrs. H. Ingram, of Winnipeg (brother-in-law and sister of the bride), wishing the newly-married, couple every happiness.</p>
        <p>-- LIST OF PRESENTS. Bridegroom to Bride, gold watch Bride toßridegroom, camera Father and Mother of Bride, cheque Fattier and Mother of Bridegroom, canteen of cutlery Mr. and Mrs. A. Dickens, trinket set Mr. and Mrs. John Bibby, toilet set. Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Bibby. jun., bedroom chair Mr. and Mrs. Harry Bioby, silver tea and coffee service Mr. Tom Bibby and Miss H. Davies', music duet stool Miss Ada Bibby and Mr. W. Hughes, American rocking chair Master Ewart I3ibby, kitchen utensils Mr. and Mrs. Robert Lees, dinner service Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Knight, tea service Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Knight, silver sugar basin Mr. Herbert Greene, bedsteads Miss Mary Greene, crotched table cloth Mr. W. Milner, cooking scales and mincer Mr. and Nlrs. Haworth (Wigan), silver pepperettes Miss 0. Bennet, serviettes Misses May sod Winnie Haworth (Wigan),silver vases Mrs. R. Wilkinson (Leigh), duclaesse set A Friend, inlaid oak tray Mr. J. Haworth (Glazobury), clock Mr. B. Sus ton, salt cellars Mr. and Mrs. Jones (Manchester), eiderdown quilts ' Mr. and Mrs. A. Jones (Crewe), tea trays Mr. and Mrs. B. Isaac, crotch supper cloth. Misses W. and M. and Master J. Isaac, iruit dish Mr. R. Lees, ja.,and Miss M. Booth, afternoon tea knives</p>
        <p>Mrs. A. J. Knight, damask table cloth Mr. Is. A. Finignt, cut glasses with jug Misses Connie and litssie Dickens, Ina dishes Masters Arthur and Charles Dickens, jam and vinegar dishes Miss Dora Bibby, set of jugs Mast, r Bibby, p'ckie jsr Master Jackie Bibby, fruit and cake dishes Miss B. Pugh, coal vase Miss M. Hallwood, painted satin cushions Miss N. Elliott, seltzogene Miss A. Hosketb, fower vases Mr. and Mrs. J.Whitehosse. silver fish knives and forks Misses Kate and Maggie Hughes (Festiniog), silver pb( to frames Mr. and Mrs. H. Horrocks, music cabinet Mr. and Mrs. J. Critetiley, oil painting Mr. end Mrs. J. R. Calvert (Southport, clock Mr. V. Cshert and Miss Molly 'rood (Southport), flower stand Mr. and Mrs. T. Locker. silver salt cellers</p>
        <p>. . Councillor and Mis. J. T. Locker, Councillor and Mrs. Lindley and Mr. and Mrs. liriraelow, PidErroovai (juilts. Mr. and Mrs. W. Stoddard (Blackpool), sop ea Misses Nellie and Alice Stodcard (Blackpool), serviettes Mr. and Mrs. Nixon, bedspread Mrs. R. Bolton, tea service Mrs. Kisby, table cloth Councillor and Mrs. Halllwell, table cloth Miss Cuppage, oak tray Miss Kathleen Bibby, photo frame and set of jugs Mr. Baskerville, kitchen rug Misses Dowler rind Bennett (Llandudno),silver butter knife Mrs. and Miss Lawrence, hot water jug Mrs. Bate, vases Mrs. T. Beswick, Daresbury, fruit dish and water jug Mr. and Mrs. Craik, clock Dr. and Mrs. Martin, silver cake stand Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Baker, silver jam dish Mr. and Mrs. W. T. Williams, plant pot Teachers and Students 'Trinity Branch Technical School, occasional table and rug Trinity Evening School, woollen door mat Miss Davies, glass Scholars Trinity School, clock Harrington Council School (Liverpool), oak barometer Managers Harrington Council School, ink stand</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>AUGUST HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>AUGUST HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>An Attractive List of Excursions</p>
        <p>from Warrington.</p>
        <p>The August Bank Holiday will soon be upon us and those in search of information as to " where to go," need only consult the attractive programmes of the railway companies. The Great Central Railway Co. announce express excursions to the Eastern Counties, embracing Yarmouth, Lowestoft, Cromer, ac., for 7,8, 14 or 15 clays; also to the South and West of England, Isle- of-Wight and Channel Islands (via Banbury or London), and to London (Marylebone) for short snd long date periods. The North-east coast has not been overlooked and bookings will be in operation on Saturday, August 2nd, to Bridlington, Filey, Scarborough, &amp;c. The Midlands are also provided for and passengers:may book to Nottingham, Leicester, Rugby, Stratford-on-Avon, Oxford, Grantham, Ayles- bury, &amp;c. The usual excursion nill also be run to Sheffield, Worksop (for the Dukeries), Cleethorpes, Lincoln, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Tickets for these excursions may be obtained in advance at the station and from Mr. Isherwood, 37, Bridge-street, Warrington. The I.ondoti and North-Western Railway Co. are running cheap excursions to all parts of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales, including London and the South Coast, South and Central Wales, North Wales, West of England, English Lakes, Eastern Counties, North-Eastern Line.—Handbills at the stations give full particulars, or intending excur- sionists can apply to Mr. W. M. Turnbull, Lime- street, Liverpool. No Englishman has seen his country at her best, who has not visited " Glorious Devon" and the Great Western line are offering exceedingly tempting facili- ties for intending visitors. Express trains run from all parts, and passengers are assured of comfortable travelling. The local agency is at 42, Sankey-street. where every courtesy is extended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Water Polo.</head>
        <p>Water Polo.</p>
        <p>In connection with the second division of the Liverpool and District Water Polo League, War- rington entertained Everton second in the Corpora- tion baths on Thursday evening. Everton, who defended the shallow end in the first half, played a hard game, and Keir, Bisseth and Clemenshaw scored before the interval. In the second half the Warring- ton forwards gave the visiting custodian a busy time, and Turner opened Warrington's account. Parker and Keir scored for Everton, and Turner again netted the ball, making the final scores: Everton, five goals; Warrington, two.</p>
        <p>FLAGS, STREAMERS, BUNTING.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 53, and 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Longshaw—Ogden.</head>
        <p>Longshaw—Ogden.</p>
        <p>_ On Tuesday morning a very pretty wedding was celebrated in the Golborne-street Baptist Church, the interior of which was tastefully decorated and adorned wirh p Orris and flowers. The cccasion was the marriage of Miss Lily Ogden (daughter of the late Mr. Edward Ogden, Tochnurden), sister, of the resident minister, Rev. H. Ogden, to Mr. Thomas Longshaw (son of the late Mr. Samuel Longshaw, of Warrington), the secretary cf the church. Brfore the service corn mencrd the church was almost filled with friends end ‘vell-wisbers of the bridal pair. When the bride entered the church, accompanied by her brother, the Rev l a Ogden, who gave her away, the organist, Mr. S. flickers, tas'efuLy rendered the "Bridal Chorus" from " Lohengrin." The service opened with the hc inn "0 Love Divine and Gulden," and the ceremony ihroughint, conducted by the Rev. G. Curtis, of Hill Cdffe, was impressive. As the bride and bridegroom left the church the organist played Gailmant's "Wedding March." The bride was charmingly dressed in a French grey silk ninon dress, trimmed with pale cream lace. ller ornaments were a rnilkstone brooch set in and ornamented Ncith gold tracery. Her bouquet was of lilies of the valley, adorned with streamers. The chief bridesmaid was Miss Mitchell, of Nelson, niece of the bride, who was attired in dove grey silk eoline, trimmed with cream lace. Her ornament was a gold bangle, the gift of the bridegroom, with a bouquet of sweet peas and streamers. The little brides- maids were Miss Florence Jones, the daughter of Mr. r. Wynne Jones, of Warrington, and Miss Lena Taylcr, of Warrington, niece of the 'bridegroom. They were sweetly dressed in cream silk, and wore gold yendants, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried baskets of sweet peas. After the ceremony the guests adjourned to the Winmarleigh Cafe for the reception, followed by a lunch. At 12,30, the brich, and bridegroom left Bank Quay station for Aberystwyth, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride travelled in a blue serge costume and mauve hat trimmed with roses.</p>
        <p>LIST OF PRESEN S. Bridegroom to bride, gold-mounted hand satchel Bride to bridegroom, solid gold cuff links and studs</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden, cheque Miss Mitchell (Nelson), hand-worked covers Mrs. S. Mitchell (Nelson), set of carvers Mr. F. NI (Nelson), silver dessert spoons Mr. and Mrs. A. Taylor (Warrington), silver-mounted Wedgewood biscuit barrel Mr. and Mrs. Walter Taylor, cheque Miss Vright (Chester), Chenille table cloth Miss Mehlor (Little Neston), case of brushes Miss Wright (Grapcenhall), pair of water colours, by G. Williams _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. B. Few, silver sugar bowl and sifter Mr. and Mrs. E. NV. Jones, silver salts and case Mr. and Mrs. G. Balmford, silver-mounted cut glass marmalade jar Miss &amp;filen, silver egg stand, and hand-worked table centre _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. S. Fernley, silver candlesticks Miss Spencer, bend-worked hair tidies Office Str:ff and Workmen, Water Department (War- rington), ease of cutlery Mr. and Mrs. W. Balmford (Manchester), linen table cloths</p>
        <p>Mrs. Spurr and Mrs. Birtwistle (Oldham), white bedspread Miss Gvenwood (Nelson), pen psinted table centre Miss Pilling (Todrnorden), supper cloths Mr. and Mrs. Whittaker (Oldham), towels Misses Sutton, milk bowl and messure,and table mats</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Glover—Wright.</head>
        <p>Glover—Wright.</p>
        <p>A marriage which aroused considerable interest in Warrington, as well as in Daresbury, took place at All Saints' Church, Daresbury, on Wednesday, the parties being well-known mtmbers of the teacning profession in this town. The bridegroom was Mr. Mriurice John Glover, son of Mr. John Glover, Bewsey-road, Warrington, and the bride, Miss Gertrude Wright, daughter of Mr. John Wright, of , Daresbury. There was a large congregation to witness the wedding, which was one of !the prettiest seen in the village for some time. The service was' choral, and included the hymn "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden." The officiating clergy were the Revs. T. T. Lee Jones (vicar of Daresbury) and W. firacecamp (vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington). Mr. Gerald B. Feber, of Litchfield Theological Collee, was at the organ. The bride, who was given away by her father, looked charming in a dress of ivory satin charmeuse, trimmed with Honiton lace, with a Court train of satin, lined with shell pink satin and chiffon and edged with orange blossom. She wore a veil of handsome-embroidered Brussels net. The bridesmaids were Miss Ethel Glover (sister of the bridegroom), Miss Agnes Wallington, Miss Lilian Wright (sister of the bride)and Miss Rachael Ambrose (cousin of the bride). The two senior bridesmaids were attired, in dresses of soft creme satin, with it atees of floral brocade, and carried bouquets of sweet peas. The two children wore pretty Empire frocks of embroidery, with Dutch bonnets and carried baskets of sweet peas. Mr. Percy Wright acted as best man and Mr. U. Newburn as groomsman. The reception was held at Daresbury School, and hearty congratulations were extended to the happy couple hy the large number of guests assembled. Later, Mr. and Mrs. Alaurice Giver left for their honeymoon tour in Scotland, the bride travelling in a grey, tailor- made costume and blue Tagal hat, trimmed with red roses. Many handsome presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>O'Brien---Davenport.</head>
        <p>O'Brien---Davenport.</p>
        <p>A quiet yet pretty wedding was solemnized at Christ Church, Latchford, on Saturday, the contract- ing parties being Mr. L. P. O'Brien, and Miss Susie Davenport. The service was conducted by the Rev. G. C. Briggs. After the breakfast, the happy couple left for the Lake District, where the honeymoon is being spent. The presents were numerous and useful. Miss Amy Davenport acted as bridesmaid, whilst the duties of best man were carried out by Mr. W. P. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PROPERTY MARKET.</head>
        <p>THE PROPERTY MARKET.</p>
        <p>Important Sale of Land and Houses at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Messrs. John Mainwaring and Co. conducted an important sale by auction at the Blue Bell Hotel on Wednesday, of land and properties in Stockton Heath, Latchford and Warrington. There was a good attendance, but the bidding, -on the whole, was not brisk, only three of the twelve lots being disposed of. The first lot was a plot of freehold building land, containing 2,00 S square yards, on the southerly side of Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, with a frontage thereto of 100 feet.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mainwaring mentioned that he offered the land for sale in that room many years ago, but it had now come into the market again. The situation of Whitefield-road xvat3 one of the best in the township—in fact, he thought that road took the palm as a residential neighbour- hood. Most of the land in the vicinity was already built upon and it was very rarely that the opportu- nity of purchasing such a nice, freehold plot presented itself. In his opinion the market price of the land was 3d. a yard.</p>
        <p>Bidding began at £2OO, and the lot was knocked down for :c.:400.</p>
        <p>There were no Lids for lots two, three and four— seven dwelling-houses in Kimberley-drive, and the semi-detached dwelling-houses, " Thorn Lea " and " Helvetia," in Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath. Lot five—the detached dwelling-house " Ashlea House," Grappenhali-road, Stockton Heath—was sold for</p>
        <p>No offers were made for lots six, seven, eight. nine and ten—the send-detached dwelling-houses, 21 and 20, Whitefield-road, and the dwelling-houses, 117, 119, and 121, London-road, Stockton Heath. There was a bid of S-500 for lot 11—the freehold family residence, " Latchford Lodge," Lower Wpish- lane, Latchford- but as this was lower than the reserve price, the lot was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"FISHERY RIGHTS" RESERTED.</head>
        <p>"FISHERY RIGHTS" RESERTED.</p>
        <p>Some amusement was caused by the reading of one of the conditions of purchase for lot 12—the free- hold dwellinghouse, " Willow Cottage," Wilderspool Causeway—which was to the effect that "the right of fishery in the river Mersey and the liberties in con- nection therewith, are excepted_ and reserved."</p>
        <p>The house was sold for 455. The solicitors tr) the vendors were :—A s to Lots 1 2,3, 4, and 5, Mr. Thomas Ridgway, as to lot 5 Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co., as to lot 11 Mr. J. Longland, and as to all the lots, Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.</p>
        <p>GARDEN FURNITURE, TENTS, HAMMOCKS, CHAIRS, &amp;c.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge at., Warringtort.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>BUILDERS' PROFITS,</head>
        <p>BUILDERS' PROFITS,</p>
        <p>How Mr. Lloyd George Proposes to Meet the Small Investor. INCREMENT DUTY EXEMPTIONS.</p>
        <p>An official statement issued by the Treasury on Monday night explains the provisions of the new Revenue Bill which Mr. Lloyd George has prepared to safeguard builders' trade profits from any possible levy of increment duty, and to grant concessions to small investors, to builders and others developing estates for building purposes, and in certain cases to holders of agricultural land. The official statement is given below : The principal provisions of the Bill are framed to confer exemptions from increment value duty on builders and others developing estates for building purposes, and on small investors in land and house property. NO TAXATION OF BUILDERS' PROFITS.</p>
        <p>The Bill gives k fleet to the intentions ot the Govern- ment, as expressed in the House of Commons, to make it absolutely clear that no part of a builder's trade profits shall be subject even to the possible operation of the Increment Tax.</p>
        <p>The clauses of the Bill which frank building estates in course of development and free builders from the possibility or apprehension of any claim for duty in respect of trade profits were settled as the result of a conference between the enancellor of the Exchequer and the legal representatives of the National Federa- tion of Building Trade Employers, and were approved and accepted by them as removing the apprehensions occasioned by the decision in the Lurnsden case, and as satisfactory from the point of view of those engaged in the building trade. As regards houses and shop property and other build- ings, in all cases L+ here the value of the building exceeds the value of the site, the vendor may have the increase in site value (if any) on a sale determined by valuation without reference to the price obtained, _</p>
        <p>No increment value duty will therefore be payable on a sale of such property by a builder or investor on any profit be may make, except as to profits which may arise out of an increase in the value of the bare land apart from the buildings, and profits which are not due to any expenditure, or the execution of any works, on his part. A sale of such property at a price above its market value, or at an ihcreastkl price due to a rise or recovery in the value of the builuings, will not involve any claim for duty. SMALL INVESTORS AND BUILDERS.</p>
        <p>The small investor in land or houses, whose income does not exceed £l6O, is wholly relieved from the incidence of the tax in respect, of any sale of a property not exceeding £.500 in value. Builders and landowners developing land for build- ing purposes receive further important concessions under the Bill.</p>
        <p>The clauses in the Finance Act which exempt from taxation any increase of value in land attributable to the execution of any works or the expenditure of money by the owner are extended to exempt any increase of price obtained by reason of financial assistance given to buyers, of by reason of any other arrangement made by the vendor to facilitate sales.</p>
        <p>In the case of laud in course of development by a builder or landowner for building purposes, though the value of the bare land may have risen from causes independent of ths development of the estate, no claim for increment value duty will be made during certain periods allowed by the Bill. Alter the land has been prepared for building purposes by an expenditure (e.g., on roads or sewers) equal to the full site value of the land, or £2OO an acre, whichever be the less sum, the land is franked from increment value duty, and a certificate of exemption is given for five years, and, as suitable houses are erected, each building, with its appurtenant land, is further franked from duty for five years from the date of erection.</p>
        <p>When these periods of five years have elapsed, builders' profits and occasional profits on sale, not due to an increase in the site value, will still be exempted from duty under the provisions of the Bill.</p>
        <p>AGRICULTURAL LAND.</p>
        <p>The valuation roll of agricultural land is to be com- pleted, in accordance with the desire of those repre- senting agricultural interests in tbe House of Commons, by the addition of a column allowing the "unimproved value "of the land. For this purpose the valuers are to deduct the value of all unexhausted improvements such as drainage, embankment, walls, &amp;c., made at any time within 30 years before April 30th, 1909.</p>
        <p>The amended valuation will thus afford a basis for any future legislation to exempt improvements in the assessment of agricultural land for local rates.</p>
        <p>Among the remaining sections of the Bill are clauses giving certain relit f from reversion duty where land was originally leased on favourable terms for charitable purposes, or where a lease has been extended on the lessee undertaking improvements in the property. Property owners who have neglected to claim "substituted site value" within the time allowed by the Finance Act (190-10) are given a further opportunity to make the claim, if it is in their interest to do so, within three mouths after the first occasion on claim for duty arises</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LAND DUTY CONCESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LAND DUTY CONCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The political correspondent of the " Manchester Guardian" writing on Monday night, said :—The principal provisions of the Revenue Bill, which is printed to-day, are framed to confer exemptions from increment value duty upon buildersend others develop- ing estates for building purposes and upon small investors in land and house property. The Bill gives effect to the intentions of the Government,as expressed in the House of Commons, to make it absolutely clear that no part of a builder's trade profits shall be subject even to the possible operation of the increment tax. The clauses of the Bill which frank building estates in course of development and free builders rrom the possibility or apprehension of any claim for duty in respect of trade profits were settled as the result of a conference between the Chancellor of the Exchequer and the legal representatives of the National Federa- tion of Building Trades Employers, and were approved and accepted by them as removing the apprehensions occasioned by the decision in the Lumsden case, and as satisfactory from the point of view of those engaged in the building trade. As regards houses and shop property and oi her build- lugs, in all cases where the value of the buildiog exceds the value of the site the vendor may have the increase 1 in site value (if any) on the occasion of a sale deter- mined by valuation without reference to the price obtained. No increment value duty will therefore he payable on a sale of such property by a builder or investor on any profit he may make, except as to profits which may arise out of an increase in the value of the bare land apart from the buildings, and which are not due to any expenditure or the execution of any works on his part. A sale of such property at a price above its market value or at any increased price due to a rise or recovery in the value of the buildings will not claim for duty. The small investor in land or houses whose income does not exceed 160 is wholly relieved from the incidence of the tax in respect of any sale of a property not exceeding £5OO in value. DEVELOPED BUILDING ESTATES. -</p>
        <p>Builders and landowners engaged in developing land for building purposes receive further important con- cessions under the Bill. The clauses in the Finance Act which exempt from taxation any increase of value in land attributable to the execution of any works or the expenditure of money by the owner are extended to exempt any increase of price obtained by reason of financial assistance given to purchasers or by reason of any other arrangement made by the vendor to facilitate sales. In the cave of land in course of development by a builder or landowner for building purposes, though the value of the bare land may have risen from causes inde- pendent of the development of the estate, no claim for increment value duty will be made during certain periods allowed by the Bill. After the land has been prepared for building purposes by an expenditure (e.g., on roads or sewers) equal to the full site value of the land, or .C2OO per acre, whichever be the lesser sum, the land is franked from increment value duty and a certificate of exemption given fora period of five years, and as suitable houses are from time to time erected each building with its appurtenant land is further franked from duty for a period of five sears from the date of erection. When these periods of five years have elapsed builders' profits and occasional profits on sale not due to an increase in the site value will still be exempted from duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>AGRICULTURAL LAND.</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURAL LAND.</p>
        <p>The valuation roll of agricultural land is to be com- pleted in accordance with the desire of those repre- senting agricultural interests in the House of Commons, by the addition of a column showing the "unimproved value" of the land. For tlts purpose the valuers are to deduct the value of all unexhausted improvements, such as drainage embankments, walls, &amp;c., effected at any time within thirty years previous to the 30th April, 1909. The amended valuation will thus afford a basis for any future legislation to exempt improvements in the assessment of agricultural land for local rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>OTHER EXEMPTIONS.</head>
        <p>OTHER EXEMPTIONS.</p>
        <p>Among the remaining sections of the Bilhare clauses which give certain lead from reversion duty where land was originally leased on favourable terms for claritable purposes or where a lease has been extended on the leseee undertaking to effec,t improvements in the property. Property owners wllt. have neglected to claim "substituted site value" within the time allowed by the Finance Act (1909-10) are given a further opportunity to make the claim, if it is in their interest to do so, isithin three months after the first occasion on which a claim for duty arises</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MARKET PRICES.</head>
        <p>MARKET PRICES.</p>
        <p>WHEAT, firm at late rates, viz. : 4s. 6d. to 4s. Bd. per 701bs. OATS, slow; fewer on offer, prices ranging from c's. 3d. to 2s. Od. per 451bs. WHEAT STRAW, 38s. per ton. OAT, 38s. to 40s. per ton. XX FLOUR., 23s 3d. per 2401b5. BRAN, 9.d. per 201bs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
