      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>COUNTRYSIDE</head>
        <p>THE COUNTRYSIDE</p>
        <p>Warrington Swans' Checkered Experi-</p>
        <p>ence and Persecution.</p>
        <p>SCOUNDRELS WITH GUNS.</p>
        <p>My attention was attracted to the swans by the appearance during the last five weeks of one and sometimes two birds on the old river at Wiiders-pool. Hearing a rumour that the two had a nest higher up the river I began searching for it, only to :earn from the lock-keeper at Walton that the rumour was base- less. I was further informed by him that one of the birds—a young one in the grey plumage of a „first year bird—was only an occasional -visitor and .never stayed very long. So I turned my inquiries in another direction, and for the facts embodied in this account I am much indebted to Mr. James Lyon Whittle, the Town Clerk who has accomplished so much to foster the country, - side spirit in the town, as witness the aviary, the fish hachery, and the aquarium in Bank Park. And, now, we are further indebted to him for introducing, under great difficulties, these beautiful birds. The history of the swans is as follows :—About three years ago, the old male known as " Peter " was- given to the Corporation, and placed on the ponds in the Town Hall gardens. Be became very tame, and readily answered to his name. A year or so later a swan was caught in ths Old Canal at Stockton Heath, which Mr. Whittle bought and placed on the p eid with "Peter." During the spawning season the swans attacked the female fish, and by pressing them with their bills forced cut the eggs which they devoured. This was a case of "killing the goose that laid the golden egg " ; conse- quently they had to be recooved from the pond. The water in the river having much improved they were penned in an enclosure on the liver bank in Victoria Park, near the new bridge. When they were turned into the river later on they became great pets of the people living in the neighbourhood, especially of the boatmen and the mill girls working near. "Peter," when called, would come up to them and take food from their hands.</p>
        <p>The park-keeper, on one occasion, was just in time to prevent a man shooting at the birds. When spoken to tie said he thought he had a right to shoot! Another " fool with a gun," some little time after this, shot old "Peter," whose body was found floating on the river. The Corporation offered a reward of £5, but were unable to discover the miscreant. Warrington as Swan Centre. At the request of the people living in the neighbour- hood, the Town Clerk bought three more swans—two males and a female—and after pinioning them placed them on the river. One pair built a nest on the bank, and the female bird laid three eggs. They were unfortunately disturbed by lads and dogs, and they forsook the nest and commenced another in some pits at Woolston. There, it was alleged, they were doing damage to the spawning fish which belonged to a local angling association, and they had to be removed and they are now penned up by the river side at Howley. Last year six swans took up their abode at Ackers Pits, Stockton Heath, attracted no doubt by the birds in the river. It is supposed they came from Pickmere or Marbury Mere, or possibly from the mouili of the Weaver, near Froclsham, where there has been a small swannery for many years. The birds at Ackert: were welcomed and regularly fed by the people living in the distlict. They were not to be allowed to settle quietly, however, for another gunner shot one of the birds and dispersed the rest. The wounded bird was attended to by Mr. George Pilling and one or two other gentlemen. The bird had a broken wing and a hole in its aide. It is now all right again, and two of the birds have returned again to Ackers Pits. One of the scared birds is at present on the Ship Canal at Stockton Heath, and another is the one already referred to on the old river at Wilderspool. It is reported that this bird is being persecuted by boys using it as a target for stones. It is earnestly hoped that the residents in the neighbourhood will see that the bird, and the one which is to be placed with it shortly, are protected and enceuraged to make their permanent abode in the river. It is difficult to understand the state of mind of the men who, from pore wantonness, shoot these harmless and handsome birds, and perhaps now that the facts are known the public will assist in future in bringing to justice any miscreant who attempts to kill the birds. British Swans.</p>
        <p>The swans are the most handsome of our British aquatic birds, their gracefulness of form and especi- ally the beautiful curving of the neck giving them a very attractive appearance. Although their bodies are so large and bulky they are birds of powerful flight, and are more aquatic in habit—living more on the water—than their relations the geese. At the same time they walk gainly and well on laud. When in flight they stretch their long necks straight out in front. and expand their large wings fully out. Many of the swans in the winter leave the inland waters for the open sea. Some difference of opinion exists as to which species our local swans belong. I have heard them called the Whopper or Whistling swan. That species, however, while it has many points wf resemblance, is a wilder and shyer bird. The Whopper swan is about the same size as the mute swan—cur local species - but can always be distinguished by its short and rounded tail. Its peak from the base to below the nostrils is yellow, the remainder being black. The mute swan,, on the other hand, has a wedge-shaped tail, and the beak from the base to the nostrils is just the reverse, black from the base tn the nostrils, and the terminal portion orange red. The mute swan may be further distinguished by the large black tubercle at the junction of the head and beak. The tubercle increases in size as the swan grows older. The Whooper does not breed outside the Arctic circle, and only visits our shores on migration. Friendly and Fierce.</p>
        <p>The mute swan is always found in a semi-domesti- cated state, and, if unmolested, will live on the friendliest terms with man. It is only during the breeding season that the male assumes alaything like a fierce and threatening attitude, and then only if intruders trespass too near the nest on which the female iv sitting. The bird then becomes a really formidable antagonist, rushing with outstretched wings at his opponent, dealing blow after blow with powerful strokes of the wing-bones. Numerous instamces are on record of men having their arms and legs broken by the fierce attack of the mute swan. The bird usually breeds singly or in small companies, but at Abbotsbury, near Weymouth, there exists a very large swannery of eight hundred birds, which dates from ancient times. Like ducks, swans pair for life. The popular name of mute swan does not signify that it is a silent bird, for when angry it hisses loudly like a goose, and when in fighting mood gives loud trumpet-like calls. The term is used rather to distinguish it from the noisy Whooper, who has a louder whistle and sometimes a trumpet call. There is another and much smaller swan which visits the British Isles —Bewick's swan. This bird breeds even further north than the W hooper, and can be identified by its smaller size and yellow bill. A. DUNLOP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Local Yachtsmen's Trying Experience.</head>
        <p>Local Yachtsmen's Trying Experience.</p>
        <p>To be adrift all night in a gale on the open sea was the trying experience of several local yachtsmen vrho went for a ciuise on the yacht lone last week-end. The craft is the property of Mr. Walter Knowles, cashier of Messrs. J. Shaw Green and Co., accountaars, &amp;c., Mill-street, Warrington, and Mr. L. D. Thompson, of Southport, and the adventure marked the beginning of the annual summer cruise round North Wales. Mr. Knowles was accompanied by Mr. Walter Sutton, of Market-street, Mr. William Smalley, of IS, Willis- street, and the crew, Walter Miller, of 4, Bart's-couit, , and S. Rustage, of Liverpool-road, all of Warrington: Leaving Rock Ferry for Beaumaris at five o'clock on Saturday afternoon, the party first experienced fairly calm weather. After crossing the bar.however,they en- countered a terrific gale which continued throughout the night, with the result that the yacht was blown at least twenty miles out of its con! se. All the occupants were drenched to the skin, as the vessel tossed about for many him' s; the bo:.,m. was broken and the cabin flooded. The pumps had to be kept going all night. At five o'clock on Sunday morning the party were able to land at Beaumaris, and, after they had recovered from their exhaustion end dried their clothes, the cruise was resumed. Mr. Walter Sutton returned home, for business reasons, on Tuesday evening, but his friends are still enjoying their sail under normal weather conditions.</p>
        <p>For the holidays, DRESS BASKETS, TRUNKS, SUL CASES, &amp;C.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-swr Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>• = I. .1</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDlTOR.—Continned.</p>
        <p>in ten of the Welsh people belong to the Church. One iv ould imagine be had not beard of the purely Welsh petitions against Disestablishment and Dis- endowment signed by over half a million people. Simple arithmetic makes such a statement as this latter one ridiculous. There are Nonconformist Churches as well as the Episcopal Church alike covering in religious work both England and Wales under their respective systems. These Nonconformist Churches have con- sidered and rejected dismemberment between their English and Welsh communities, and no Government would enforce such dismemberment any more than Churchmen would desire anything of the kind. Why, therefore, do Nonconformists and Parliament (or the Government) desire to forcibly dismember the Church by tearing her Welsh adherents from her English adherents, and in addition to that seek to rob her of much of her funds? Of this, which from a religious point vf view is the crux of the whole matter, Mr. Smith says not a word! CONWAY STIDSTON-BROADBENT. Lynim.</p>
        <p>Can you see both near and distant objects, (each eye to be tested separately) if not, consult JOHNSTON WATSON, SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON. Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Gold from 8/6.</p>
