      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>,015TRIcT riEw3,,4</head>
        <p>,015TRIcT riEw3,,4</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAND.— A good number of young people were present at the above meeting on Monday evening, when an interesting and helpful address was given by Mr. Arthur Thomas on" Nehemiah." Miss Eva Miller presided. BOWLING.—A bowling match between Lymm and Oughtrington which aroused considerable local enthusiasm took place on Tuesday night on the green at the Plough Hotel, when Lymm won by the narrow margin of 24 points. MoTons COLLIDE.—What might have been a most serious motor accident happened near Agden Smithy on Monday night, when two motor cars travelling in opposite directions collided. Fortunately, no one was injured, but one of the cars had both front wheels and axle broken.</p>
        <p>POLITICAL PICSICS.—Lymm appears to have been chosen as the political playground for quite • a large district. To-day (Saturday) a party of Liberals from East Manchester are having a picnic here, whilst last Saturday Lymm was rendezvous of the Earlestown branch of the 1.L.P.. who turned up in strong force, accompanied by the Earlestown Viaduct Band, and the Stcckport St. 'Thomas' Conservative Club. Both parties wore their respective colours, but intermixed with the utmost good humour ; in fact their divergent views provided them with a suoject for continuous ard witty badinage. In the evening the Labour party held a short meeting at the Cross, which was addressed by Mr. Seddon and Mr. Gosling. DEATH OF MR. SAMIIEL JACKSON.—There passed away, at the age of sixty, at his residence in New-road, Lymtn, on the lithf inst., a well-known and respected public servant in the person of Mr. Samuel Jackson. For over ten sears Mr. Jackson was employed at the Lymm Gas Works under the Local Board and later on under the Urban District Council. During the past two years he had been ailing from heart disease, and had only been able to take light duties on the roads. The deceased took an active interest in the old 3rd Cheshire Volunteers, of which he was a Lance-Sergeant. Being one of the comp tny's crack shots he was an annual competitor at the Altcar meet- ings of the Cheshire Rifle Association where be won the Company Challenge Cup. He was also awarded the long service medal. The funeral took place at the Lymm Parish Church on Monday, when the Rector, the Rev. G. B. Thurston, M.A., was the officiating clergymae. Amongst the mourners were : Thomas Jackson (brothers) ; - Sarah, and Mary Jackson (daughters) ; Ernest, Herbert, Frank, Walter, and Mark Jackson (sons) ; and John Worrell. Numerous wreaths were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>BOY SCOUTS' FETE.</head>
        <p>BOY SCOUTS' FETE.</p>
        <p>Demonstration and Sports at Heatley. That the many and varied charms of the Scout move- ment have not ceased to attract the boys of the Heatley district was evinced by the splendid muster et their third annual fete and sale of work, which was held on Saturday in the prettily situated field adjoining Heatley Station, kindly lent for the occasion by Mr. Sam. Davies, J.P. Though the weather in the afternoon was of the bright and cheery order, it is certain that the good number of persons who visited the field would have been considerably augmented had it not been for the rain which fell earlier in the day. The opening ceremony was performed by Mr. Alfred Watkie,J.P. (High Sheriff for the County of Chester),who whilst expressing his pleasure at being present, gave a description of some of the numerous benefits to be derived from joining the Boy Scout Movement. Speaking of the scout laws, he made special reference to the one which specifies that a scout must do at least one kind action every day. He said that the movement trained them for their after-life, whatever they might become, and their I experience as scouts must be of advantage to them. He desired to impress on all boys the necessity of joining the B.P. Scouts and asked that parents would help forward the movement by getting their sons to become members. He stated his appreciation of the efforts of the ladies' committee and friends in giving their time to the furthering of the movement, and asked the public to support the splendid efforts of the ladies which were so well shown in the marquee. He also gave a few words of thanks to Scoutmaster T. Nash for his devotion to the boys. Mrs. T. J. hidgway, a sincere friend of the Heatley Troop, then presented the flags with a few words of kindly advice; Patrol-Leader Welter Jones, of the Lion Patrol, receiving an ensign, and Patrol-Leader Sydney Ruse, of the Kangaroo Patrol,a troop flag An inspection of the scouts by Mr. A. Clegg, Scout Commissioner for the district, then followed. After endorsing Mrs. Ridgway's message, Mr. Clegg thanked that lady and Mr. A. Watkin for their presence and support, and at his call they were accorded three hearty cheers; ho also being next accorded a similar honour. Amongst other friends present were Mrs. A. Watkin, Mr. T. J. and Miss Ridgway. In addition to the Heatley troop, under the com- mand of Scoutmaster T.Niish and Assistant Scoutmaster H. Acton, members of the Ist Warrington, Brooklands, and Broadheath troops, under the respective direction of Scoutmasters Bennett, Turner, and Wolstenholme, were also present. During the remainder of the after- noou a programme of scouteraft was riven, comprising such items as tent pitching, fancy cycling, ambulance , drill, maze march, field cooking, and, from a spectacular point of view, a good display of semaphore signalling to musical accompaniment. The Ist Brooklands troop gave a clever demonstration of morse During the tea interval one had the opportunity of examining aud purchasing from the well-stocked stall of fancy and useful articles; partaking of the refreshing teas supplied in the marquee; and of having a shy at the cocoanuts. In the evening a programme of sports was enjoyed, the humorous element being provided by football match with the players in sacks, and a costume race.</p>
        <p>After numerous heats had been run, the finals took place and resulted as follows : Wheelbarrow race.-1, H. Robinson and J. Robinson ; 2, W. Mills and J. Washington ; 3, W. G. Jones, jun., and S. Jones.</p>
        <p>Boot race (open).-1, C. Forrest; 2, Hatton (Warrington) ; 3, A. Clare. Costume race.-1, W. G. Jones, jun. ; 2, C. Forrest ; 3, N. Barber. _</p>
        <p>In the tug-Di-war between Heatley and Warrington the former won by two pulls. At the conclusion of the sports, Mrs. Clegg presented the prizes ; after which the Lymm Subscription Band, which had been in attendance during the afternoon and evening, played selections for public dancing, which was readily taken advantage of. It is pleasing to note that the efforts of the committee, of which Mr. W. G. Jones is the enthusiastic hon. secretarV, and the lady helpers Mesdames W. G. Jones, A. Davies, Landless, and Robinson, Miss Priestlier, and others, have resulted in a financial success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>CROFT.</head>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>• A PRETTY PAGEANT.—The village was en fete OD Saturday when the children at the schools held their annual procession and field day. The procession, which presented an exceedingly pretty spectacle, was headed by the band of the Culoheth Cottage Homes, conducted by Mr. Moss. Some of the principal features were : Messrs. John Houghton and Joseph Monks on horse- back, and representing dragoon guards; Mr. Owen as Oliver Cromwell; Mr. S. H. Sankey and Mr. Noel Smith as cowboys ; Britannia, Miss Maggie Taylor, accompanied by six prettily-dressed flower girls; john Bull, Mr. George Birchall ; decorated lurries filled by the younger children, followed by the elder girls and remainder of the scholars; the original Croft Burlesque Band, dressed in all kinds of costumes • and a convey- ance containing inhabitants. Miss Dorothy Sankey appeared as Gipsy, Alice Taylor as Winter, Annie Monks as Cowgirl, Grace Coates as Porticea, Mildlei Coates as Kitty Gray, Margaret Roberts as Mushroom, Emily Houghton as Cards, Alice Battersby as Joan of Arc, Miss Wright as Quaker Girl, Beatrice Sharpies as Spanish Dancing Girl, Mabel Ball as Tobacco, Leah Houghton as Norwegian Peasant, Reginald Tucker as Japanese, Percy Houghton as Page Boy, Willie Wardo as Bear, J. L. Broom as Beefeater, Bert Daintith as Pierrot, Fred Broomfield as Toreador, Samuel Stretch as The Prince, J. Warburton as Policeman, John Taylor as Bandit, Walter Taylor as Prince, Samuel Miller as Bear, W. Worthington as Napoleon, Albert Broomfield as Policeman, Frank Green as Gollywog, &amp;c. The adults and children sat down to tea in the school, and sports of various kinds took place, the scholars competing for prizes. The Cottage Homes Band played selections for dancing until dusk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>PENKETH TANNING Co'S. ANNUAL TRIP.—The employes of the above firm had their annual excursion no Saturday, when thcy journeyed to Morecambe. When the train left Fiddler's Ferry at 7.16 a.m., the weather gave every appearance of a wet day, but better things were in store for the trippers. On arriving at Morecambe the rain had ceased, and the sun shed forth its welcome rays. The company dispereed at the station to enjoy the beauty spots of Morecambe and surrounding district. The promptitude of tbe return journey gave great satisfaction. Leaving Morecambe at 8 p.m. the train steamed into Fiddler's Ferry Station at 10.4 p.m. Over 400 men, women and children took advantage of the outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH.—On il onday evening a very interesting meeting was held in the above place of worship, when Mr. J. W. Johnson, of Sunderland, President of the Connexion, addressed a fair congregation. He spoke from the words "I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me." Gal. ii. 20. The address was an inspiration to all. The great need of a real livening Christ in our midst, said the preacher, was I specially manifest to-day, when sin and pleasure seemed to have such a firm hold of the people.</p>
