      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. UNLICENSED PEDLAR.-At the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday, before Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Thomas Finn, a tramp, was charged with hawking pictule postcards at Stockton Heath on Monday with- out a licence and was sent to prison for seven days. POLICE DUTY AT STOCKTON HEATII.-At a meeting of the Cheshire Police Committee, at Chester on Saturday, the following resolution was received from the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee :—" That this committee respectfully urges upon the Standing Joint Committee the desirability, if not necessity, of there always being one officer on duty in the Stockton Heath County Police Station." In answer to the Chair- man (Colonel France-Hayhurst), the Chief Constable (Major 'Malcolm) said he could not agree with the 'Stockton Heath local authority upon the ne3essity of there always being one officer on duty. He should be inclined to consider that if there were an officer always at the police station he would he wasting his time and be woulo be better employed outside. The result of giving effect to the application would be that three additioual men would be needed, and the expense would fall on the rates. There was always an officer close at hand.—Dr. Hodgson moved that tire resolution be not approved en the ground stated.—The motion was seconded and carried.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE AND FIRE PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Parochial Committee met at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening, Mr. U. Pilling presiding. There were also pre3ent :—Messrs. F. Pearson, F. Rose, C. Dukes, J. McDermid, F. Warburton, J. Hallows, H. J. West- brook, .1 Whitley, with A. S. Wright (surveyor) and W. H. Warburton (acting clerk).</p>
        <p>THAT :11KLL !</p>
        <p>The County Archite-J-Wro-te-requesting that the plan showing the position of the propoaed new fire bell, to be fixed on Stockton Heath police station should be forwarded for tho approval of the Chief Constable and himself.</p>
        <p>• The Surveyor submit.ei the plan, which indicatod that the bell would be fixed 22ft above the ground. It was decided to forward the plan to the County Architect as requested.</p>
        <p>CONDITION OF STREETS. • Having received a report from the Surveyor to the Rural District Council with reference to the condition of Mitchell-street and Farrell-street—recently made under the Private Street Works Act—the committee decided to go into the contract to ascertain whether \he five years' guarantee was still operative.</p>
        <p>FIRE PRECAUTIOiIS. The Acting Clerk read a letter from the Town Clerk of Warrington stating that the committee's resolution, asking upon what terms the Borough Fire Brigade would place its services at the disposal of Stockton Heath,would be laid before the Fire Engine Committee. Messrs. Greenell, Whitley and Co., who had also been applied to for terms, wrote that as their brigade was an entirely voluntary one, they were not In a position to make binding terms. Hitherto the practice had been to attend any fire within reasonable distance (except in Warrington), if asked to do so; the men invariably being paid for their services and the firm for the use of the steam engine by the insurance companies. In the event of the property not being insured, they bad to take their chance of being paid. So far as they were concerned, that practice would be continued. Tbe suggested arrangement with regard to telephonio com- munication between Victoria Square and the brewery could be carried out at any time. The Chairman : That is a very satisfactory letter. He added that the Warrington Corporation were charging Latchford Without one penny in the £ on their assessments for the services of the borough brigade,</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson said the committee need not he over anxious as he could say that the Wilderspool Brigade would always be at their service. Never in its history had the brigade refubei to respond to a call. The Chairman suggested that the committee should eo into the matter, quite apart from the Corporation. He was certain they would not come to terms with that body. The Wllderspool brigade was much nearer, and was prqmpt at turning out. Mr. Dukes pointed out that occasions might arise when Stockton Heath would require the services of the Corporation and Messrs. Greenell's brigades at the same time.</p>
        <p>It was decided, punding the reply from the Warring- ton Fire Engine Committee, to go into the question of communication between the village and Messrs. Greens!! and Whitley's brigade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>FOR THE OLD FOLK.</head>
        <p>FOR THE OLD FOLK.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose asked whether tbe committee could arrange to place a seat in the garden adjoiuing Mr. Bennett's shop for the use of the old people. Mr. Westbrook : It would kill some of the old age pensioners off on a breezy day, (Laughter.) It was decided to approach the owner of the plot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>BRAVERY REWARDED.</head>
        <p>BRAVERY REWARDED.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man's Rescue of a Child from Drowning.</p>
        <p>Fitting recognition of a brave deed was made at the Borough Police Court yesterday. George Wild, 7, Hulo3e-street, a crane driver in the employ of the Bridgewater Navigation Company, who saved a child from drowning in the Mersey on April 14th, was pre- sented with the medal and framed certificate of the `Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society, together with a postal order for £l. Tha Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), in making the presentation, that a child named Thomas Ashton (4), 46, Wellington-street, went to echool and was not heard of again by the mother, who is a widow, until shortly after five o'clock, when she was informed that be had fallen into the river: It appeared that a Mrs. Burroughs was passing over the bridge at Howley when she saw the child in the river. She attracted the attention of Mrs. Gresty, who was, howevor, uusuccessful in her endeavours to get the child out by means of a clothes prop. Some workmen close by, with whom Wild was working, were informed, and Wild jumped into the river, fully clothed, and brought the child to side. The rescuer was in an exhausted condition and bad to be helped out of the water. Artificial respira- tion was successfully applied and the child tully recovered. Mention was also made of the fact that Wild was not a strong swimmer, and also that he had a wife and three children, and that he was most unassuming about the rescue. Those facts wete all to his credit and showed the true nobility of the action. The other persons concerned in the matter—Mrs. Burroughs, Mrs. Gresty and those who applied artificial respiration—were all wortby of praise, for they brought Wild's gallant deed into full benefit. In conclusion, the Mayor said he was glad to see there were not many prisoners, in the dock. Even the few there could contrast their own position with that of the man they were about to honour and reward. Be hoped his wish was not in vain, and that in the future even they would endeavour to mend their conduct in such a manner as to merit the approbation of everyone, instead of reprobation. The Mayor then handed the awards to Wild, amidst loud applause. Wild briefly thanked the Mayor for the presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>DEEP BREATIIING.</head>
        <p>DEEP BREATIIING.</p>
        <p>Much has been written of late years about the importance of deep breathing, but gener- ally speaking all the advice given is to prac- tise taking deep inspirations voluntarily, so many breaths so and so, and so often during the day. This is to take a very narrow view of the matter, as it SCQMS to us, says a writer in Health. Beyond question deep breathing is very important; but the only practical and by all means best way to secure it is by doing something to compel it in a perfectly natural way. Hard, sharp, muscular, all-round exer- cise is the proper thing. Walking, running, rowing, swimming, hill or stair climbing— anything, everything, that will engage all the</p>
        <p>muscles-and keep Them supple, fide, strong, and free from fatty degeneration. Attend sufficiently to the.s things, and no one will have to tell you how to breathe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
