      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>YMINGT</head>
        <p>YMINGT</p>
        <p>rztters to tbt Oita</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. IDE EDITOR of the " Examiner " invites correspond- ence on all subjects of public interest. LErrEns should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. LETTERS must in all casos be accompanied by the name and address of tne writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week—except urgent matters—can always be given the hest attention.</p>
        <p>R.,ga 71-dare Mark COFFEE ESSENCE is the best beverage. No waste—no waiting—just pour boiling water on a spoonful of Symington's in the cup. Thos. Syw:-.Tton J.:7. Co.. Edinher,c-ii and London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>RELIGION AND THE KING'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>RELIGION AND THE KING'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Sir, —lt has never been my custom to take any notice cf anonymous letters, either about myself, or addressed to me on any matter. Therefore, my anonymous friend, who replies to my letter in your issue of the 12th instant, will pardon me, I hope, if I do not depart from that custom now, further tnan to say that the phrase "Fair Play " does not seem quite an accurate description cf his present interpoiition.</p>
        <p>JOHN YONGE. 26, Elleemere-rosid, Stcckton Heath, July 25t1i, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LOCAL VETO AND LOCAL OPTION.</head>
        <p>LOCAL VETO AND LOCAL OPTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the account of a temperance meeting at Warrington, published in your issue of the 19th inst., Mr. Leslie is reported to have said: "Local option would give the public in any community the right to say whether they would have public-houses in their midst or not. That was a democratic measure to which no one would object, except the publicans and brewers." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Local option is undoubtedly democratic in principle. It is, however, necessary to point out that the measure for which teetotallers are agitating is local veto, which is a vastly different thing from local option. The people in any district are not to be trusted to say whether they would like more or fewer public-houses ; they are only to be allowed to reduce the number of public-houses or prohibit them altogether. A majority in favour of an additional licence is to be powerless.</p>
        <p>It is because the prorosal is so one-sided and 'undemocratic that many others besides publicans and brewers are strongly opposed to its adoption. EDW. TUCKER.</p>
        <p>July 23rd, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>WAKE UP, WARRINGTON!</head>
        <p>WAKE UP, WARRINGTON!</p>
        <p>Sir,—After having been a resident in Warrington Tor over six months I must express my sorrow at the inactivity of the workers of the temperance cause in regard to the Band of Hope work, which is mostly work to instruct and bring before the children of this present age the advantages and benefits of total abstinence. I have made inquiries at the different Sunday schools and I find that, with the exception of one place of worship, in the winter season they only hold one Band of Hope meeting once a month, and that in the summer season they do not bold any meetings at all inside and only occasionally outside. 1 went to the Band of Hope meeting last Wednesday, the 16th, held in the open-air in Ellesmere-street, and was surprised at the interest shown by the children to listen to the speakers. The reason I was surprised was that when I was making inquiries RR a temperance worker why they did not have Band of Hope meetings in summer they (the temperance workers) told me they could not get the children to attend. I wish some of those interested in temperance work had seen the quick interest shown by those children who listened to Mr. Leslie speaking, for they would have come to the same conclusion as myself, and that is twit there is abundance of scope for work am-ingst the children of this town. We must remember that we shsll have to rely on the children of the present day to make the future generation a more sober and hewr generation than this present one. Why then this neglect in regard t r this noble work of teaching cur boys and girls the benefits of total abstinence? In my own observation, I do not think it is because Warrington is short of temperance workers, but that the slid workers are not ready and willing to do what they can (whether it be much or little) to bring before the children the benefits they themselves have derived from total abstinence. Having myself experi- enced both sides of the question, end proving in my .own life both physically and morally the value of total abstinence, I for one will do all I can to help on this glorious cause of temperance. I would suggest that a few temperance workers band together and have weekly open-air meetings in different parts of the t earn in connection with the Band of Hope movement. To prove that there is a great need for this work let me state that after the Band of Hope meeting in Ellesmere-street on the 16th inst. I got 26 pledges ti;gned and was besieged by 12 more, but as J had emptied my pledge book I had with me it was with great and deep sorrow that I had to deny young men and women that great opportunity of signing. I may add that the ages of those who signed the pledge ranged from 11 to 17 years. I hope this letter will be the means of waking up the slothful in this matter; if if does then I shall be only too pleased to know that my letter has not been written in vain. A REFORMED DRUNKARD. July 21st, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT.</head>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have noticed the report in your last issue of a speech made at Bridge Foot by Mr. F. Rhodes Smith, of London, on the Disestablishment question— to which, with your permission, I should like to refer. Mr. Smith says "the nation " has consistently demanded Disestablishment, but unless be charac- terised Wales by this title it is difficult to understand him. Even then it is not easy, for Wales can hardly be called" a nation " until it becomes distinct and separate from the other parts of what only can be • called" our nation." The claim of the larger part of Ireland is to become a nation—an admission that something is needed before it can so be. The measure Mr. Smith refers to has only been " demanded " in the sense of being one thing in many- -one plank in a platform of many—demanded simul- taneously. Wales has not been given the advantage of declaring the view of Welsh people on the subject of Dieestablishment and Disendowment alone. This ;is the very thing the largest party in the State are asking for at this moment —and unfortunately asking for in vain. If only all parties would agree to give Wales this opportunity it would clear the air advan- tageously. Could not Mr. Smith help to bring about such a teat? _ _ .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Smith gave a quotation from the Archbishop of Canterbury—hut, as reported, he did not quote him accurately, nor even quote the whole sentence con- taining the extract. The quotation was from a speech of the Archbishop's made at the Swansea Congress in 1909, and is as follows :—" None of us can know intimately many of the men sho are foremost at tilts hour in endeavouring to bring about such a change (Disestablishment) without feeling convinced that they are good, earnest, capable, God-fearing men, who are saying and doing what they believe to be right." To leave out the part of the sentence in italics does not give a fair quotation. And the sense and spirit of the Archbishop is further explained and emphasised in a further quotation which I will take from a much later speech of the Primate—delivered in his charge to the diocese of Canterbury in 1912. It is as follows :—" I have said no word to impugn the motives, or to challenge the religious honesty, of those who promote what I regard as the disastrous proposals which are shortly to come before Parliament. Their action is due rather, as it seems to me, to lack of adequate knowledge and to 'shortness of thought.' " Mr. Smith pointed out that the Bill received recently (3rd reading this sessionra majority of 42 over and above the votes of the Irish Members; but Mr. Smith did not point out that during what scant consideration the Bill 'received in committee during last session, apart from the Irish vote, it was beaten repeatedly. Had the Irish Roman Catholic Members abstained from voting on a matter which does not concern Ireland, and about which they do not care a button, except for- other reasons, the Bill would never have reached th 3 stage to which Mr. Smith triumph- .antly refers. , It would be very interesting to read en attempt on Mr. Smith's part to prove his allegation that the Church" forced itself " upon Wales, and that not one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="5" unit="words">
