      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TARIFF " REFORM " SLUMP.</head>
        <p>TARIFF " REFORM " SLUMP.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett " A Voice Crying in</p>
        <p>tile Wilderness."</p>
        <p>AMERICA'S SIGNIFICANT MOVE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Grindle, of Eccles, who was the principal speaker at the weekly open-air meeting held at Bridge Foot on Monday, in c ainection with the Progressive Labour League, is a political lecturer who is well- known to local Progressives. The Councillor is a very convincing speaker, and quotes facts and figures with the greatest of ease. He has always an eye for the I humorous', and during the coupe of his speech causes many a laugh at the expense of the Tariff Reformers. One of his best jokes on Monday wa4 that which related to the Tariff Reform "programme." Councillor Grindle said that he had picked up the following lines a few da3s before: " Wanted, a policy. Liberal price for one that is convenient, bandy and nice. Not wanted for use, only for show. Apply, stating terms, to Bonar Law and Co." The- audience fully appreciated this jibe at the politically 1 bankrupt party, and gave the speaker every eneour- agement. Mr. E. A. SANDERS was the chairman. He spoke highly of the past record of the Liberal party and bald out good hopes for the future. AN INSECURE PLANK.</p>
        <p>Councillor G KINDLE said they had found so many people wandering away from the fundandental principles that he thought it was as well to recapitulate their creed before they reached the modern aspects of the situation. Free Trade was a system under which no protective duties were levied on impprted goods to protect particular trades. The duty on tea was not a protective duty, because it was not imposed to protect any trade. Therefore, it w%s not in conflict with Free Trade principles. They did not for one moment say that Free Trade would he a cure for all the depressions of trade, or that it would regulate all the social evils. They offered it as a protection against Protectionism. After referring to the wild charges and accusations made against the Liberal party by the Opposition, the Councillor said that there was not the least doubt that Tariff Reform was the only plank iu the Tory platform, and not such a solid one at that. (Laughter.) AMERICA'S SIGNIFICANT MOVE.</p>
        <p>Continuing, he said that Woodrow Wilson had been elected the American President very largely on a promise to remove the tariff. The duty under the present system was ninepence on every shillingsworth of British-made goods going into America. Mr. Wilson's Bill,which was nearly through the Senate, would reduce that duty by sixpence on every shilling. " What," he was asked," would be the effect on the British trade, if there was a reduction of the tariff in America ?" There were two opinions and he did not care to say which would happen, because he was neither a prophet nor the son of a prophet. Some thought that when the tariffs were taken off we should send more goods into Amelica, but. what he was afraid of was that when the duties were taken off the goods which went into America the American manufacturers would be able to manu- facture so much more economically that the English would not have a look in If, however, toe reduction was to the benefit of the 90,000,000 people living in the United State 3, then they ought not to be too narrow- minded over the matter, but let them enjoy such an advantage. Woodrow Wilson had started the Free Trade principles in-America, and they had every cause to rejoice. (Hear, hear.) WHERE FOOD IS DEAR.</p>
        <p>Tariff Reformers said a great deal that was wrong about the price of food in America. In his presidential campaign, Woodrow Wilson said, "It is not as easy for us to live as it used to be. Our money will not buy as much now. High wages, even when we get them, yield us no great benefit. We used to be better off with less money, because then a dollar could buy so much more. The majority of 114 have been disturbed on finding ourselves growing poorer, even though our earnings are slightly increasing,bectiuse prices climb up faster than we can fetch up our earnings." With regard to work under the excessive tariff, the President said, "I want the men out of a job to know that there are fewer jobs in America because of the tariff." " Why," commented Councillor Grindley, " they used to tell us that Tariff Reform meant work for all, and even Mr. Chamberlain had said Tariff Reform will mean two jobs for everyone.' rhat is quite true, but in a humorous sense. It will be a job to get a job and a job to keep it under Tariff Reform." (Laughter and applause.) In America there were hundreds of thou- sands out of work on account of the Tariff. It was monstrous that Tariff Reformers should talk such rub- bish as "The foreigner will pay," for it was the poor housewife who would suffer under such a system. (Applause.) THE TARIFF "REFORM" SLUMP. Mr. E. WYNNE JONES said it was only right and proper that they should still keep the question of Free Trade to the front, as it was more essential to the welfare of the people of this country than ever it was. (Applause.) There was absolutely no doubt that the policy of Free Trade had been abundantly justified by events, although in Warrington at the present time there might not appear to be the same need to argue the question as in the past, seeing that there was a slump in Tariff Reform. (Hear, hear.) There were alleged to be quite a number of Tariff Reformers in Warrington, but none of them seemed to be taking much interest in the question. The only sincere Tariff Reformer appeared to be Alderman Bennett, and he was like a voice crying in the wilderness. Not even his own party seemed to take any notice of his advocacy of Tariff Reform or Protection as it ought to be called. (Hear, hear.) MR. HAROLD SMITH'S " CORNHILL S rYLE." He noticed that a few days ago Mr. Harold Smith delivered a speech at Cornhill, and it was quite up to the Cornhill style. He stated that no speaker must be allowed to speak on any Liberal platform until he had answered some of the questions he had mentioned. He hoped they would see that the questions were put to the speakers, whoever they were, Mr. Crosfleld or anyone else. He, Mr. Jones,would like to remind the Member, or any of his followers, that they on that platform were quite prepared to answer any questions which would be asked them at the close of any meeting. But to sly that no speaker must be allowed to proceed without answering questions first was a statement they strongly resented. Only an autocrat would make eke of such an expression, and they absolutely refused to be bullied by Mr. Harold Smith or any of his friends. DEMOCRACY ON A FIRM FOOTING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones went on to point out that the Liberal party had accomplished more than any party since they came into office. If only for the Parliament Act, Progres- sives of every shade of opinion should give the Liberal party their solid backing; or by that measure the Liberal party had placed democracy on a firm footing for the first time in this country. They were the only party who were in a position to bring about any effective social reforms, and that was a matter which deserved the best study and attention of every Pro- gressive. (Applause). Votes of thanks closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>ANCIENT FSMITIES,</head>
        <p>ANCIENT FSMITIES,</p>
        <p>Most of our resentments dwell in our hearts, not because of any present claim to be there, but simply because 'having once,, found entrance they have newer been put out.. Most of our enmities are old-timers, owing their existence to the fact tha, pride will not let. us admit that they never had any right to exist at all. If hearts, like homes, had their periodic house-cleaning days and burnt up the rubbish, how much easier life would be! •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <p>[LLL ItIGIITS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>IDA, THE FRAUD.</head>
        <p>IDA, THE FRAUD.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. COULSON KERNAHAN Author of "House of Rimmon," Trewinnot of Guy's," "No Vindication," 'An Unwise Virgin," "A Beautiful Savage," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>Ida Bourke was stamping violently with one pretty foot upon the floor. A rosy colour shewed under the tan which a South African sun had dyed her face. It was angry blood which gave this• becom- ing red. Her eyes were flashing indignant fires. She as in a royal passion. "If there is one thing I hate more than another upon this earth. Nell, it is In- Laws.' I told Tom when I married him that I would never consent to know his relations. I thought it would be easy to keep clear of them as we were to live in South Africa and they in England—and now Tom has been so mean, mean, mean! as to let them know I have come to England for some months, and here is a letter begging me to spend .Christ- mas with them. Well, I won't, and that's fiat ! "</p>
        <p>The sober little matron whose ears were assailed by this explosion of wrath answered her sister with emphasised quietness. "I think, my dear, that instead of being angry you ought to be immensely flattered that your husband is so bent on presenting you to his relations. It shews that he is proud of "I don't care what it shews," broke out Ida. "Tom knows that I hate in-laws,' and he has taken this mean way of forcing me to know his people. The very last thing I said to him when he saw Me off at Cape- town was, 'Be sure you don't let your peo- ple know I am coming to visit my sister !; and directly my back is turned he writes to them, with this result." And Mrs. Bourke flung the offending letter into her sister's lap. *Mrs. Redfern glanced through the letter, and then looked up at her sister with some indignation in her eyes. "An extremely nice letter very warm-hearted and cordial. These 'in-laws' must be very nice people. I must say you are both unreasonable and ungrateful. They might easily have behaved very differently. . Their eldest son goes out to the war as a doctor, and marries a nurse he meets on the veldt, of whom they know nothing, and settles there—" "I mean them to know nothing," inter- rupted Ida. "We have corresponded plea- santly, and my letters have made them like me. If they saw me it would undo every- thing. I should quarrel with them just be- cause they are in-laws,' and they would hate me, and then Tom would get his back up. There is a sister about my age at home and a brother who is an Army man, and there IS, I believe, a maiden aunt. I won't •go! " "Open your other letter, Ida," said Nelly, soothingly. "It may contain news to please you better." Mrs. Bourke tore open the envelope with- out glancing at the address. "Oh ! " she exclaimed, with a gay little laugh, "here's a way out of it. This is from Netts Gray. You remember my writing to you about her from South Africa? She was a nurse there with me. A charming girl, and 60 pretty! She says she has a holiday and nowhere in the world to spend it gaily, and that she longs for some fun. Netta shall go to the 'in-laws' as Mrs. Tom Bourke. She is game for anything." Oh, Ida, how wicked! But, of course, you are joking?" "Joking? Never more serious in my life, Nell! The in-laws" have not seen my ,photograph. The South African suns have left a permanent record on Netta's skin. She knows all about the war, having been .a nurse there. She has visited our home on the veldt. She knows as much as Ido about all the Bourkes—from me, of course. She is about my age. With a wedding ring she will carry the thing through splendidly. I will write her straight, Away."</p>
        <p>"Snowing! How delightful!" cried Lily 13ourke. "nd snow to last by the look of it. We shall have a real good old Christmas. Will, do you know what the time is? You promised to drive to Bartomly Station to meet Tom's wife. You will be late, and she will think it rude."</p>
        <p>The young soldier to whom these remarks were made glanced up lazily from the depths of a particularly easy chair and smiled—a leisurely smile—at his sister. "Bother Tom's wife !" was what he said.</p>
        <p>"What was that, sir? What was that you e,aid? came in stentorian tones from the doorway of the smoking-room, where the brother and sister had thought themselves out of earshot of the rest .of the household.</p>
        <p>- A portly, fussy old gentleman, brimming with energy and good humour, consulting a time-table which he held in his hand, went on with his remarks, while his fat forefinger ran down a column. "You are to pay every attention to your brother's wife, sir—Just un- derstand that She shall receive a good Eng- lish welcome. Yes, Will. By Jove! you have only just time. Get ready, and I will order the closed carriage round. Ida said, 'I shall arrive in time for dinner.' Just like a woman not to state time of train. But there is only one likely train to bring her in time for dinner, and that is the 6.10. Mr. Bourke looked over the top of his gold- rimmed spectacles, to find that he was alone. His son and daughter had fled. Then the grating of carriage wheels sounded on the drive, and there was a dash from earring,. lamps through the window. "Will is off in miraculously quick time," he commented, as he trotted out of the room and, along a passage which led to the old-fashioned square hall, in which a gigantic log fire shone on old armour, Mr. Bourke's special pride. The hall door was open. John, the solemn old servant, who had far more dignity than his master, was admitting someone—and there was Will, still in his loose smoking jacket! It ,was a very brilliant vision which ap- peared in the firelight—a "tailor - made" girl, with an oval face, shaded by .a big beaver hat, the chin nestling among dark furs—a girl with a pair of sparkling brown eyes and a mobile mouth, red as carmine. She came forward towards the old gentleman, with a smile that slimed a row of white teeth.</p>
        <p>"Are you ' rather '? " she asked in a VOICE which was frank and musical.</p>
        <p>"And proud to he so. my dear." answered tli7, old gentleman, gallantly, "to such a charming daughter." She gave him both her little gloved hands and presented her cheek. . . _ _</p>
        <p>But it was on the red mouth that the old gentleman's kiss descended noisily. Will watched this proceeding with con- siderable interest-. " What a deucedly pretty woman ! " he mentally commented. "Tom has taste."</p>
        <p>Then he bethought himself that his own appearance was not irreproachable, and would hate made good his escape had not his father called him. " Coma, Will, this is Ida." Very awkwardly for a gentleman of Will's usual self-possession, very awkwardly indeed for a soldier in his Majesty's service, young Captain Bourke came forward to greet his brother's wife.</p>
        <p>"He may kiss you, Ida, may be not? Brother and sister, you know," said the old gentleman, rubbing his hands with delight for he had not got beyond the age when a beautiful woman was a pleasure to look upon. Ida lifted a roguish face to the young Captain's, and he could not do other thar kiss her cheek.</p>
        <p>But that moment's contact left him tingling. He disappeared as his mother and sister carried off Ida to her room. He smiled a good deal while he made a careful toilet. Christmas bade fair now to be jolly. What a " stunner " was this sister-in-law ! " and no silly nonsense about her." And the pretty impostor? What of Tier? She was bubbling over with fun. She had refused the services of a maid, chiefly that she might get rid of some of her pent-up merriment. Having turned the key in the door, she had sunk down on the white woolly rug before the fire, and was laughing till the tears ran down her cheeks.</p>
        <p>The firelight flickered o'n. the wedding T ing upon the hand held out to the blaze, the sight of which set the airl into another stifled peal of laughter. " Oh, tins is going to be fun!" she told herself. "What a ninny ;41a, wan to send me instead of coming hertelf."</p>
      </div>
