      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>"I REMEMBER REMINISCENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON. BY VARIOUS CONTRIBUTORS.</head>
        <p>"I REMEMBER REMINISCENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON. BY VARIOUS CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>BANK QUAY AND SANKEY GREEN. Old inhabitants of various parts of the town have given their" remembrances," but up to the present none have given a description of the Bank Quay district "in the days of their youth." This district has,how- ever, altered in appearance probably more than any other part of the town, and natives who have been away from the good old town for a long period would be astounded at the change there is from earlier days. I cannot claim to be an old inhabitant because my recollection does not go back to forty years, but as I have either lived at or passed through Bank Quay and Sankey Green during the whole of that period, I have a vivid idea of what has taken place. it will, perhaps, be interesting to readers of the " Examiner " if I give my remembrances in the form of a walk round.</p>
        <p>A Changed Prospect. I will commence at Bank Quay Bridge. The prospect from the top of the bridge look- ing westwards was quite different from the prospect to-day. Instead of looking along a comparatively wide road, there was then a narrower road with no tram lines. Where is now the fire station of Messrs. Crosfield's there was then the bottle works of Cart- wright's, just closed down. Three or four of the curious conical erections which characterise this kind of works, filled all the space now covered with different depart- ments of the soap works, and they looked very gaunt and dreary, owing to the fact that they were rapidly going to ruin. I well remember as a boy watching with interest the work of demolition. Huge rents were "chimneys," and on one side of the large chimneys," and large baulks of timber were then put in to keep them up while the holes were made larger. When everything was in readiness the timber was set on fire, and then the whole erection toppled over with a tremendous crash. Crosfield's works reached out to the road much further than they do now, and there was only a small lodge at the entrance, for I daresay the employes were not more than one quarter of the number they are to-day. Whitecross was also much smaller than at the present time. The end which abuts on Bank Quay bridge was then a portion of Cartwright's glass bottle works, also derelict, and a glance over the wall revealed a vast amount of broken glass. There was then no bridge over Milner-street, and Whitecross works only extended along three-quarters of one side, the other side, past the row of cottages which join the cocoa rooms, not being then b-ailt upon. The cocoa rooms at the corner of Milner-street was then a kind of confectioner's shop kept by Mr. Winstanley, who afterwards emi- grated, and is now with his son farming in America. The church of the Sacred Heart was then a thing of the future, and the house now occupied by the priest was a gentleman's ' house surrounded by its pretty garden and high wall. The resident was Mr. Edward Bolton, of Bolton's glass works. Mr. Bolton was an astronomer, and had a fine tele- scope for which a movable pedestal .was erected. He joined the Salvation Army soon after they were established in Warring- ton, and become one of their leading supporters. I well remember General Booth, then a man with a long black beard, coming to the Public Hall one Sunday, and I followed him all the way from Rylands-street to Mr. Bolton's house. The General, I may mention, did not ride. He walked. The next house at the corner of Green-street, in front of which the not very sightly shops are now erected, was then a fine residence, occupied by Mr. W. Dampier Jeans. I often saw Mr. Jeans and his family having a walk about the district. Going along Green-street, there was then the blank wall which forms the boundary of the Bowling Green, and on the other side there was also a long wooden "wall," where are now the houses with small front gardens. The three houses just before Garibaldi-street were erected before I came upon the earth, but there was then no Garibaldi-street. It was simply an open space, and at the end, in line with Green-street, was a rickety wooden pallisade, and many have been the feats of gymnastic daring which my com- panions and myself have performed there. Priestley-street was then called Froghall- lane, but otherwise it has alterecl very little. the only new part being the row beginning with the greengrocer's shop a little up the street on the right-hand side, and this hides Whitecross works on that side. The portion of Green-street beyond Priestley-street is known as Sankey Green, then it was a real green, but I am sure that the name must puzzle many of the new comers. When I first knew it, it was a pretty district practi- cally in the country, and together with Lovely-lane formed an ideal west end to the town. The butchers' shops on the opposite sides of the street were there then, and so was the row of houses as far as faces the end of Bostock-street on the left-hand side of Green-street, but how different then from now was the appearance of the street. From the Liverpool House to the end of Bostock- street was a row of white, thatched cottages, very picturesque to look at, but I am afraid not very picturesque inside. They were a genuine piece of old Warrington. Kissed the Late Sir Gilbert. Bostock-street was a short street, and I am afraid I have gained an ineradicable prejudice against this street and the very name of Bostock,by the fact that there were continued rows then between the women of the street. A very amusing or part disgusting incident of which I was a little witness occurred there during the 1880 election. The late Sir Gilbert Greenall was driving in his yellow coach along Green-street when two or three drunken women supporters from Bostock- street saw him. They made a sudden swoop upon the carriage, shouting " Greenall for ever," and then wrenched open the door, and before poor Sir Gilbert could be rescued they gave him two or three resounding kisses. " Save me from my friends 1" must have been the heartfelt prayer of Sir Gilbert on that occasion. The houses beyond Bostock-street, In Green-street, were then without paved footpath, and with the exception of the one at the corner of Bostock-street and the other at the corner of Lovely-lane were all private houses. The occupier of the first- named shop was a Mr. Heaton, a great Tory, who christened his eldest son Gilbert "in honour of Sir Gilbert," he , said. Boys of slightly different ages—and play- mates of mine—who lived in that row were Councillor Broadhurst and Codncillor Hawthorn. On the opposite side of the street was a high hawthorn hedge, and nothing but fields extended as far as Liver- pool-road, and I have eaten many a pear, not got honestly, I am sorry to say, from trees then growing where there are now rows of houses. Two Irish " Characters." There was a blacksmith's shop kept by a man named Houghton, in a recess opposite (continua/ in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>HOW GIOTTO BECAME A PAINTER. More than six centuries ago, a little peasant boy tended his father's flocks in a village about forty miles from Florence. He was a happy little fellow, full of life and spirits, and seemed as free from care as one of the lambs in his charge. As ignorant as the other peasants of his day, he wandered over meadow and plain in the wake of his flock. Sometimes he would throw himself on the grass, and, selecting a nice flat stone or a patch of smooth sand, draw pictures of the surrounding objects. More often than not, one of his own lambs was chosen as a subject for his pencil. The pencil, by the way, was merely a hard stick or a sharp piece of stone. One day as the boy lay in the midst of his flock, very intent on the sketch he was making on a stone, a traveller chanced to pass that way. Curious as to the task in which the boy was so engrossed, he paused and stole a glance at his work. He saw the sketch of a sheep,drawnwith such boldnessand so true to life that the stranger was amazed. "Who are you ? " he asked eagerly. The boy looked up startled, for he had not known that anyone was near. "I am Giotto and my father is Bondone, the labourer, so please the signor," he made reply. "Well, then, Giotto, how would you like to come and live with me and learn to draw and paint sheep, and horses, and even men ? " The child flushed with delight. IT would like it better than anything else," he said, "but first I must ask my father's permission." "We will go to him together," said the stranger, laying his hand on the boy's head. Bondone was much astonished when he heard the stranger's proposal, but he gave his consent. He himself went to Florence with his boy, and left him there under the painter's care—for the stranger was no other than the painter Cimabue. Giotto's progress surpassed Cimabuc's fondest hopes, and he was justly proud of his talented pupil. In delineating nature Giotto soon excelled his master. One morning the latter came into his studio, and looking at a half-finished head, saw a fly resting on the nose. Cimabue went to brush it off and, as he ' tried to do so, perceived that it was only painted. " Who did this ? " he cried, half angry, half pleased. Giotto came slowly out of a corner, where he had hidden himself, and he confessed his fault. But instead of scolding him, his master gave him the warmest praise. The artist loved art too well to be angry at this new proof of his pupil's genius, though it was given at his own expense. Fame soon came to Giotto, and his name was on every tongue. The great men of his country delighted to honour him, and among his friends he counted the poet Dante. He died in the year 1836, mourned by all who knew him, for he was a good man as well as a great one. Such is the story of the little shepherd boy who rose to be one the greatest of the early Italian painters.</p>
        <p>THE MAGIC LETTER. There was a little maiden once</p>
        <p>In fairy days gone by, Whose every thought and every word Always began with "I." " I think," "I know," "I wish," "I say," " I like," "I want," " I will," From morn to night, from day to day, " I " was her burden still. Her schoolmates would not play with her.</p>
        <p>Her parents tried in vain: - To teach her better, and one day Peer" I cried out in pain. " Help me, 0 fairies !" she besought, "I'm worn to just a thread. Do save me from this dreadful child, Or I shall soon be dead ! " The fairies heard, and heeded, too; They caught poor" I" away, And nursed her into-her health again Through many an anxious day ; And in her place they deftly slipped A broader, stronger letter. "The more she uses that," they said, With roguish smiles, "the better." The little maiden wept and sulked At first, and would not speak, But she grew tired of being dumb ; And so within a week, She used the substitute; and, lo ! Her playnrites crowded round, Her parents smiled and all were pleased To hear this novel sound. She grew to use it steadily And liked it more and more, It came to fill a larger place Than "I " had done before. And each year found the little maid</p>
        <p>More kind and sweet and true ; What was the magic letter's name? Why, can't you guess? 'Twas " U."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>I REMEMBER—Continued.</p>
        <p>to the end of Bostock-street, and somewhere near Mr. Walsh's butcher's shop there were two old cottages, one occupied by a milk dealer named Lomax, and the other by Pat and Anastasia Hanlon, an aged Irish couple who were" characters." " Anastasia " was too hard for Englishmen to pronounce properly, so she was generally known as "Old Auntie." Both old people were about 80 years of age, and were very popular with us youngsters, though why, I could not tell. What an Irish wake there was when they died! There was a stable connected with " Old Auntie's" house, and here was kept the donkey, about which the legend ran that it was being taught how to live without food, and it was just getting nicely used to it when it ur fortunately died. Old Sankey Hall was then a large farm kept by Mr. Howard, the father to the Rev. Joseph Howard.ef Sankey, The farm came right to the side of Mill-lane and Green-street, and right across Thewlis- street, which, with the exception of a few houses, &amp;c., at Liverpool-road end, did not then exist. From where Thewlis-street now is to the old building was the stack and farm- yard. Mill-lane was then a lane with corn- fields at each side, and behind the present Co-operative Stores was a lodge in a large garden, a very pretty spot until some years before its demolition. When I was young St. Barnabas' Church was just making a start in an old- barn in Mill-lane, and I remember going there several times to hear the Rev. M. Duke give a course of readings to children from "Pilgrim's Progress." The church or rather mission was then only a bare barn with rows of forms and a small pulpit. There was a bell outside, which monotonously called people to service. Later on, a small Wesleyan mission was erected here, and continued until the developments which have resulted in present- day conditions began. Pickmere-street was then a narrow country lane, with a ditch and hedge on one side and a cornfield on the other, and it was then known as " Howard's- lane." It led through the farmyard into Mill-lane. and was a favourite walk for courtiers. There are many other changes —very great—which have taken place about here, but I think what I have already mentioned will give an idea of the state of things which once existed at Sankey Green. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>IN SHAKESPEARE'S COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>IN SHAKESPEARE'S COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>The Literary Mecca of the Whole English=speaking Race.</p>
        <p>A PILGRIMAGE TO STRATFORD-ON-AVON. Typical April weather pursued us, depressing and dazzling by turns, during the day we spent at Shakespeare!s home. Rain clouds sped across the sky, sprinkling small showers over a landscape that sparkled in emerald green when touched by the brilliant sun.</p>
        <p>the homes of his daughters, grandchild, his friend and neighbour Julius Shaw, who</p>
        <p>witnessed his will; as well as the fine old timbered house, where dwelt Katherine Rogers, the mother of the founder of Harvard</p>
        <p>University. But the reader will desire to examine these old-world retreats in detail, and so we begin.</p>
        <p>I am glad it was so. Was it not on an April day that Shakespeare was born? And the landscape would smile fresh and green like this when on his 52nd birthday he closed his eyes upon it for ever. For although Stratford has been -for generations the literary Mecca of the whole English-speaking race, though 30,000 people tramp every year through his early home and invade his quiet resting-place by the river's brim, the countryside remains pretty much the same as it was when Will Shakespeare, the successful playwright, came back from London and settled amid the tranquil scenes of his youth. We did not regard the countryside as specially beautiful nor very interesting. It is pretty and has a most beautiful river, but is not striking. In Shakespeare's day it was a sheep country and the wolds were sheep walks. To-day it is a fruitful countryside, pleasant to look upon, and typically English.</p>
        <p>THE BIRTHPLACE. There is an upper chamber here trodden each year by thirty thousand visitors. They have written their names upon ceiling, walls, door and window,until not the space of a finger-nail remains to receive another, It must surely be one of the best known chambers in the world. But the cold historian must have his word. There is not a shadow of evidence that Shakespeare was born here. The only scrap of evidence that exists suggests a room at the eastern end. Not that this will matter to the visitor. It does seem proved beyond doubt that this was the poet's home. Here is the chimney nook where the wood fire blazed, the hooks that held the bacon as it got its smoking in the chimney, the garden where he played. This firm old home, after being exploited by private owners, was purchased by the nation in 1847. No memorial was ever better managed. There is no hurry or hustle. Many visitors notwithstanding, the place breathes the very spirit of peace. If the visitor has any interest, however small, either in Shakespeare or Shakespeare's age, he cannot fail to delight in this old conglomeration of timber wattle, bricks, and clay. His first delight will be in realising that Shakespeare did, indeed, know these old timbers well. That this was his father's house and that he grew here there is no manner of doubt; that he was born here is one of the most probable of all things.</p>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE'S TOWN. The town itself is a bustling little place. Of course, there would be no such thing as forgetting, however hard one might try, that Shakespeare was born here. A dozen shops thrive upon souvenirs; we can have the poet in either white clay or silver and all the metals and clays that lie between ; picture post-cards depict everything from Anne Hathaway's rush bed to the Memorial Theatre, and a fiery red flag hangs out to invite us to try a ninepenny dinner at the "Swan of Avon" cook-shop. And have we not the Memorial Theatre, and the fountain built by Mr. Childs, of Philadelphia, with its happy Shakespearian quotation, "Honest water, which no'er left man i'th mire " ? Yet Stratford-on-Avon is not spoiled. The famous birthplace hides so modestly in quiet Henley-street that we had to inquire for it, though it stood not fifty yards away. The church stands lone and beautiful by Avon water, and the thrush sings in Shakespeare's garden at New Place, under the shadow of the old guild chapel. Perhaps our readers will like to read a little about these world-famous shrines and may tolerate therewith some spice of opinion. OUR SCANTY KNOWLEDGE OF THE POET. There is naturally all sorts of information to be had about Shakespeare at Shake- speare's home. It can be had from loquacious guides or obliging street boys, and biographers have written it in books. But the real Shakespeare eludes us. No connected story of his life has ever been told, or is ever likely to be. The facts that have been gathered make a scrap heap, not a tale. To show how little is known about the world's greatest poet, let us set it forth here. We know that he was the first son of John Shakespeare, a country trader who settled in Stratford and lived in the house in Henley-street, that he was baptised April 26th, 1564. In 1585, he was married to Anne Hathaway and had three children, a girl and then a boy and girl who were twins. Then he disappears.:</p>
        <p>It consists of two buildings joined by door- ways. The one on the left is the house proper. To the right is a house purchased by John Shakespeare in 1556 and used by him as a toolshop. The property came in 1601 to the poet upon his father's death. His mother I finished her days here, and after- wards be allowed his sister to live in the old homestead rent free. By his will he gave it to her for life, and it afterwards came to Susanna Hall, Shakespeare's eldest daughter, who had it till 1649. The identity and title of the property is so clear, and the structure so obviously old, that almost without effort we see the tiny Shakespeare sitting in the spacious chimney corner or creeping hence "like snail unwillingly to school." The front kitchen, by which we enter, has happily been altered least. Its stone floor, battered to pieces by ages of wear and wood chopping, is possibly original. But the chief , feature, as also in the back kitchen which adjoins, is the spacious lopen fire-place. A MUCH AUTOGRAPHED ROOM.</p>
        <p>We ascend a narrow stair from the back kitchen to reach two bedrooms above. In the front room, according to tradition, Shakespeare first saw the light on April 23rd, 1564. It is a dim chamber, for its walls are almost black with the pencilled names of thousands of visitors. At an earlier time, we are told, the custodian kept no -visitor's book, but asked callers to write their names on the wall! Those who had diamond rings scratched the window panes, the rest wrote either on the low ceiling, the walls or the door.</p>
        <p>Legend fills in the space very effectively, but it is important to remember that a full hundred and fifty years elapsed before one of the stories got into print or writing. He is said to have run away after a poaching affray. It is asserted that he was a country school master ; that he held horses outside the theatre doors; and that he took a place in a theatrical company. Rash surmises have made him a lawyer, a soldier, a seaman, a printer. What uoes seem fairly certain concerning this obscure period is that he joined the Earl of Leicester's theatrical company at Shoreditch, and acted with it at the Globe Theatre and the Inns of Court. Possibly, he here began to try his hand at improving the plays of others. Events are a little easier to trace when we get to the period during which his plays appear. He wrote much and made money. He must have toiled hard and carefully handled the money he made, for when he re-emerges he has bought a house called the New Place, invested in land and tithes, and is living a life of comfort in his native town. We know that be made a will, for it is preserved at Somerset House ; and we know his body lies seventeen feet deep in the chancel of Stratford Church. Only two pieces of paper exist which are known for certain to have been touched by his hand. One is his will, the other is a letter asking him for a loan of money. SHAKESPEARE PORTRAITS. Nor do we know what he looked like. Certainly, his portrait is everywhere and as familiar as any in the land. But the portraits have sprung up like the legends, at the voice of a great demand. Of course, there are representations of Shakespeare which have to be considered. There is the bust over his grave in the church. This was carved by Gerard Janssen, seven years after death. It is a coloured half length figure on the wall, crude, but probably true in the main. Then there is a panel painting done, it is supposed, in 1609. It is also crude, and w e really don't like to think that Will Shake- speare looked like that. No, we would ' rather picture him as brighter and happier. It was not, surely, from behind such a visage as this that Falstaff and such hosts of merry men leaped forth. The third picture is the one placed as a frontispiece to his collected works, but this is evidently engraved from the painted panel. And this is the only picture which can ever be considered as representing Shakespeare. But what inward rebellion if we should try to accept it! CLASSIC GROUND. Yet though the pilgrim take with him nothing but doubts, and has refused every legend, he will find Stratford-on-Avon a delightful place. Of the myriads who have travelled that winding quarter-mile from the old house in Henley-street, where he was born, past the old school house, and past New Place to the lovely church in which he was laid to rest, was there ever, we wonder, a disappointed one? "This is classic ground," said Boswell, as he contemplated it. Classic ground it is for the whole English-speaking race. Classic ground, too, for many besides; for my companion in this pilgrimage is Dr. Arnim, Professor of Vienna University, and its Hellenistic expert, and who is at pains to assure me that Shakespeare will prove a true and sufficient peace-keeper for ever between England and Germany. The quarter-mile which the poet would walk to church contains not only his place of birth, death and burial, but the Granuna:. School and its old Guild Chapel,</p>
        <p>Upon the glass, by the aid of the custodian, we can trace the names of Sir Walter Scott and Washington Irving; upon the pillar of the fire-place we may decipher the names of quite a string of famous actors, and patience and a wry neck would discover the heroes of many a song and story. A REVENGE THAT FAILED.</p>
        <p>Possibly the old woman at whose wish</p>
        <p>,hese_narnes were pencilled here was a little</p>
        <p>proud of the autographs. At any rate, when an unhappy day came on which she was called to give up her post she carried out a I scheme of revenge. The night before she delivered up the keys she carefully white- washed the room: But she forgot to mix glue in the whitewash and in a short time it peeled off. The woolshop is used as a museum, and both the upper and lower rooms contain interesting things, such as autographs of the poet's relations, deeds relating to the property, copies of Shakespeare's works printed in his lifetime, copies of contem- porary books which refer to him and his work, these last exposed, we imagine, for th e special advantage of the champions of the Baconian theory. There is also the old desk at which tradition says he sat at school. More interesting still is the case contain- ing books which he is known to have read, and pressed between glass is a letter from Richard Quiney addressed to Shakespeare in London and asking for a loan. THE SHAKESPEARE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>While the student pores over the cases, the average visitor admires the oak timbers or strays into the garden,: for here, behind the house, the bit of land on which the poet played his earliest games and felt the first breath of English garden air is turned to the most happy use imaginable. Careful hands have planted it with every tree, plant or flower that is mentioned in his writings. And so :</p>
        <p>- - Here's flowers for you: Hot lavender, mints, savory, marjoram ; The marigold that goes to bed with the sun . . bold oxlips and The crown imperial ; lilies of all kin cis, The flower de-luce being one To make you garlands of. And there is" rosemary for reme„mbrance " and "pansies that's for thoughts." Such is Shakespeare's early home. The woolshop is appropriate enough as a library and museum, the kitchen is left, as it should be left, unadorned and consequently unspoiled. But the garden of Shakespeare' flowers is the happiest idea of all.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>A WIGAN WORTHY'S DEATH. _</p>
        <p>_ Wigan has lost cr,e of its leading public men by the sudden death, following an attack of appendicitis, of Alderman Sam Wood, who was the Mayor of the borough from 1907 to 1911, and had been a member of the Council from 1899. During the recent Royal visit he was on of those presented to the King and Queen. Mr. Wood made his mayoralty memorable for three things. In the first place, he organised the fund for the colliery disaster at Abram, by which a sum of close upon £250,000 was raised. Then be took a lading part in defence of the "pit- brow girls," whose employment was threatened by by boys and youths, and by humanitarians who said that the work was unsuitable for women. Mr. Wood , organised the meetings of a vigorous campaign, anti accompanied the deputation of colliery girls who went to London to lay their case before members of the Cabinet. The defence proved to be entirely successful, and the pit-brow girls are still at work in the Wigan district. Mr. Wood also started a fund for the Royal Infirmary and Dispensary, Wigan, by which a sum of £5,000 was raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>IN DEFENCE OF MODERN BATTING. C. B. FRY SAYS SPECTATORS HAVE LOST IN</p>
        <p>KNOWLEDGE.</p>
        <p>The proper way to bat is to put your feet (and your legs) wherever they ought to be in order to obtain command for the stroke you intend, and whether in front of the wicket or not makes no matter (the wicket does not exist for a good bat—till he is bowled) and to pay attention to hitting the ball hard whether in back play or in driving or in any other stroke chosen. I would add that the really good batsmen who use or used the forward stroke with such effect, Lionel Palairet, A. P. Lucas, Lord Harris, Wilaliam Gunn, R. H. Spooner, A. C. Maclaren, Torn Hayward (not to mention others), did not make the silly forward push; they drove the ball and they also looked at the ball when they drove it. W. G. never in his life played the stroke which is taught to most schoolboys as the chief requisite in batsmanship ; batsmen like Victor Trumper and J. T. Tyldesley simply do not know such a stroke.</p>
        <p>This is a digression—but not out of reason —for half the idiotic outcry against modern batsmen is really due to the passing away of the wrong kind of forward stroke from the repertoire of the numerous players in county cricket who are bowlers, etc., and not professed batsmen, and to the consequent substitution of some sort of back-play (often wrongly used but always more sensibly), with the result that people, who think they understand cricket but do not, have tumbled on the notion that " style " has departed from modern batting. The truth is that taken all round (and with no comparison made between those at the very top, modern batsmen are much superior to those of twenty years ago—and if spectators do not find them equally interest- ing it is because spectators have lost in knowledge what they have gained in numbers.—C. B. Fry in "Fry's Magazine.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>"THE PATRICIAN."</head>
        <p>"THE PATRICIAN."</p>
        <p>Gradually such illusions as we cherish dis- appear, and now Sir George Alexander tells us that clothes do not interest him, that his own dress suit is twelve years old, and other things in the August number of "The Patrician," just issued, which statements strike at the very foundations of belief. In the same issue begins a new story by Richard Dehan, which transports the reader to Siberia, a field new to the author of "The Dop Doctor." There is also an appreciation of Sir Ernest Cassel, friend and counsel of monarchs, financial giant and philanthropist, whose well-known distaste for publicity renders authentic information all the more valuable. Though the Royal Yacht Squadron is generally regarded as the most exclusive club in the world, it will come as a surprise to many who read the interesting and well illustrated article on the R.Y.S. that its membership does not exceed 250. A very topical note is struck by an instructive article on amateur caravanning from the pen of Mr. Harris Stone; and as usual the art and fashion illustrations are carefully chosen and from the best sources. Considerable space is devoted to travel, and the pictufe of a well- known author as one of the übiquitous boy scouts, drawn by a new artist, is a gem of caricature. "The Patrician's" theatrical criticisms, written and illustrated by Kate Carew, are again a striking feature of the magazine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>STRANGE CEREMONY,</head>
        <p>A STRANGE CEREMONY,</p>
        <p>A ceremony of almost extraordinary interest, owing to the sharp contrast in which it showed up the old and the new order of things, was the three-day National Memorial Service for the late Empress Dowager in the Southern Court of the Forbidden City itself from ten in the morning tilt five in the evening, writes the special correspon- dent of the Globe. The first two days were for the attendance of officials of all kinds, schools, guilds, foreigners, and all the people of position who wished to show their respect to the memory . of the late Empress, and the last day for the entire garrison of Pekin and representatives of the whole army. It was certainly fitting that a national memorial service should be</p>
        <p>OFFERED TO ITER MEMORY, but the manner in which the opportunity Wag Artill6o4l to admit all and sundry within the eacred precincts of the Forbidden City must have been an exquisite humiliation to the Court, and was certainly very distasteful to all the old school officials in Pekin. To these, however much they may be convinced of the necessity for change in the form of government, the Forbidden City, as the historical abode of the Son of Heaven, has always been the most sacred temple in China, and to see this trodden by a motley holiday-mak- ing crowd, under the pretence of &amp;tending a memorial service, appeared as an act of sacrilege.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>FUNERAL MUSIC.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL MUSIC.</p>
        <p>Persons provided with mourning badges from one of the Ministries passed Through the Gates of Heavenly Peace, the Gate of Exalted Dignity, and finally through the immense Gate of Noon- day, which with its lofty pavilion forme three sides of a square, the actual gate consisting of three parallel tunnels each 60ft. in length, lead- ing through the base of the pavilion, into the first courtyard of the Forbidden City itself. This courtyard is made up of a paved area some 200 yards square, with the pavilion of the Gate of Noonday forming the centre of the south, and the pavilion of the Gate of Concord forming the centre of the north side. On the east and west side of the court are minor gates, each with their pavilion, and these were turned into refreshment places, where tea and Chinese refreshments were served to "mourners." The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>ACTUAL MEMORIAL SERVICE</head>
        <p>ACTUAL MEMORIAL SERVICE</p>
        <p>took place in tho great pavilion on the north side. Mourners approached this pavilion from the east. The first hall of the pavilion was con- verted into a chapel where a choir of Buddhist priests from the Imperial Yellow Temple, dressed in their full dress of Imperial Yellow with the hat worn by Imperial retainers, in- toned the Buddhist scriptures all day long. Close against itho Buddhist priests in their ancient Chinese dress was stationed a band of the Im- perial Guards dressed in a crimson modern mili- tary uniform with a profusion of gold lace and tassels. This band alternately discoursed Chinese dirges, Chopin's Funeral March, and lively quickAeps. INCONGRUOUS TOUCHES.</p>
        <p>Instead of wearing their Chinese Court dress, the officials officiating as Masters of the Cere- motlies appeared, one in a khaki military uniform with aiguilettes all complete, the other in a most extraorditlary travesty of European frock-coat and top-hat. Passing to the west "mourners" next passed a Taoist choir intoning their liturgy in a similar chapel to that on the east occupied by the Buddhist priests, and so out into the open. As in the case of the Buddhist priests the military band stationed against them gave an in- congruous touch, so here against the chapel of the Taoist priests in their red robes ard dresses of the style worn under the Ming dynasty, was stationed a hideous</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>LITTLE WHEEZY HARMONIUM.</head>
        <p>LITTLE WHEEZY HARMONIUM.</p>
        <p>This supplied the accompaniment to hymns in Chinese sung by schoolchildren from the mission schools to the tune, save the mark! of "Way, down upon the Swanee River." Another most incongruous touch was supplied by the hideous paper decorations which had been perpetrated on the splendid surroundings amid which the service was held. Tho five-coloured flag of the Republic) displayed everywhere in the Forbidden City of the Ta Ching Dynasty was startling enough, but when the scheme of deco- ration was completed by strings of penny flags of all nations, rows of paper roses stuck all over the buildings, and even the superb ,bronze lions guarding the entrance to the funeral pavilion were treated to necklaces of paper roses' making them look for all the world like Dog Toby on the cover of Punch, one felt that bad taste could no further go</p>
