      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAEVII.NER ' SToRY.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAEVII.NER ' SToRY.</p>
        <p>['UT, RICIITS RESEIIV ED ..I</p>
        <p>heard the season will be one of the liveliest in Simla for many years." "I think so too," said Wells Fisher, with easy affability. "I've got e, .regular string of attractions to save you, your crowd, the fusty old civiliang, and the Viceroy's 'party from dying of tininess. I'm going to be a. public benefactor thlti 60116011.. 1 u,,n't think.l:l C.B. for it, but I'll desbrve one." "Who is this Mks Stewart of Inver—ln vertochty, wasn't it—that you were talkii4 about?'</p>
        <p>"Ah!" exclaimed Well 2 Flolior, turnini round, "so that's your game, is it?" Captain Kinross felt .uncomfortable. •He had not anticipated Wells Fisher was a thought- reader. The fact of the mattez is, however, Captain Kinresq had so often mentioned his determination to marry money, that it had become a standing joke :amongst all who knew him that, having failed in this plan, he was really anxious to marry anybody. The ,mere inquiry on his part as to who a lady was act chaffing tongues wagging respecting his matrimonial intentions.</p>
        <p>With an effort he broke into a laugh at the suggestion. "Conscience!" he said. "You are always suspectiry, everybody of ,some- thing. I'll be afraid eo i.ask how your grand- mother is next. But she's Scotch, isn't she?" "My grandmother Scotch!" exclaimed Wells Fisher, aghast. "Heaven .forbid, and Miss Stewart forgive." "1 mean Miss Stewart," adkled the Cap- tain.</p>
        <p>"Scotch," said the burly humorist, stop- ping in the middle of the narrow path, and with a twinkle in his eye looking at his com- panion, " Scotch ! Why, 'man, you can't get within fifty yards of her for her Scotch. I thought she was speaking Gaelic when I first met her. Then I thought it was German. And if I hadn't ben the deuce of a clever fellow, I'd never have known that it was a sort of English. My dear Kinrces, your posi- tion in Simla, this season will be positively enviable. You'll have to act as interpreter. I suppose you speak Scotch fluently?" "Well," said the Captain, "not exactly fluently. That is, you know, I can speak it to a Scotch person, and I can read it—that is, I have not had much practice." "But, Donald man," said Wells Fisher, hitting him mirthfully on the shoulder, "a chap with a name like yours should speak Scotch like Rob Roy MacGregor. And, look here, she's a good-looking sort- of a lass, and I daresay that, without much persuasion, she'd change her name from that of Mies Jessie Stewart to that of Mrs. Murray Kin- ross."</p>
        <p>The me m parted and went off to their re- spective ‘bungalows. Captain Murray Kinrceu felt an unusual elation of spirit. Yet he had a dread of what was before -him. He did not, as a matter of fact, -knoW one word of Scotch.</p>
        <p>. . Wells Fishc.,r chuckled the whble way home. When he dropped into a big, long armchair he broke into a,trirnado of laughter. "Well," he ,said to himself, "if I don't have a spree with old Kinross, may I never see Simla again!"</p>
        <p>It was the first function of the .season, ansi the heart of Mrs. Allen Barker was exceeding glad. Finding herself the widow of a *Commis- • sioner at the age of forty-five, she had wisely resolved that to come home and live in a Kensington flat would not be half so inter- esting as Calcutta, .in the winter months, and some fashionable hill station in the hot weather.</p>
        <p>She was a wainan in a. thousand. Her little dances were arranged with the skill of a general. At her house you met all the pretty girls, and generally a handful of eligible men. Still, she did not neglect the plain girls, nor overlook men who hardly came within the definition of eligibles. To-night she was radiant. Simla was hav- ing its first town warming, as' it were, in the way of festivities, and the event was taking place at Highlands, her bijou bungalow. The Viceroy and his lady were coming. , Ac- cordingly, she was a, proud woman.</p>
        <p>Of course,, there was a good deal of talk in regard to who Miss. Jessie Stewart really, was. She had accompanied Mrs. Allen Barker to afternoon tea at the Viceregal Lodge, and every •afternoon she had been seen out driv- ing with the lady who presided at High- lands. She was a • tall, transparent-skinned, black-eyed, beautiful woman. Kinross was not quite as happy at the din- ner-party- as he had Roped. The Viceroy and his lady were almest at the other end of the table. But this lie did not mind. What he did mind was that Mrs. Allen Barker had for- gotten to introduce him to the lady from In- vertochty, but had introduced him to a gar- rulous. but uninteresting little. woman, the wife of an Inspector-General of Police, down Madras way. Her he took in to dinner. It was Wells Fisher who had the honour of giv- ing his arm to Miss Stewart. Certainly, this tall and graceful Scotch girl looked charming. Laughter shone from her eyes, and there was colour in her cheeks. Her burly companion, Wells .Fisher, was playing the role of the prince of captivators. Kinross was never so miserable in his life. He felt indignant with Mrs. • Allen Barker. Further, he was sure Wells Fisher was steal- ing a march on him. Then he thought Miss Stewart might at least have done him the honour of glancing his way, if only for tli'2 sake of their common nationality. All through the dinner he was sullen and dissatisfied. When the ladies hag' left the table even after-dinner' stories from Talbot fell unheeded on his ears. It was only when the men had gork to join the ladies that a ray of hope came for an instant into his heart. "I ray, Kinross," shouted Graham, "you are wanted. Come and net as interpreter." "As what ? " said. the Captain. "As interpreter," repeated Graham. "Miss _Stewart has been telling us a story about an extraordinary creature called a gillie. There is no doubt it is exoefaing,ly amusing, but I'm hanzed if I can under- I stand it!"</p>
        <p>At the • far• end of the verandah was a little group surrounding the lass from Inver- tochty. Wells Fisher, however, was hang- ing lazly over the balcony, pretending to take a poetic interest in the moonbeams on the distant hills. Just then Mrs. Allen Barker came up. "My dear Captain Kinross, have you met everybody?" "Oh, yes, 'thank you," he answered, half laughing.</p>
        <p>"-But- not Miss Stewart," hinte4 Talbot</p>
        <p>. . "How silly of me," said the hostccs ; "you aro, the two people, who ought to meet. She is just as infatuated about Scotland as you are. Come along." And so the great moment that Donald Murray Kinross had looked forward to had arrived.</p>
        <p>In the Crush of guests—for now' a couple of hundred or so had come up to the High. lands to meet the Vicn.oy and his lady after 'dinner Mrs. Allen Barker slipped away, and in the mirth and laughter and chatter Kinross was not quite certain what were the words passing between himself and the stately, black-haired Scotch girl. One thing, however, he realised with awful consciousness. She .was talking to him in Scotch, and using words of which he had not the faintest knowledge.. The thought floated into his mind to be courageous an.d, laugh at his own ignorance. But just three feet away was that confounded Wells •Fisher gazing steadily at the hills, and as immov- able as a statue.</p>
        <p>"I'm gey behadden the Mistress Barker for gi'en Inc the honour o' mectin' a Scot. Are ye i' the Black Watch regiment? " Captain Kinross stamny_srld an apology. He was not in a Scotch regiment at all; he belonged to the Glamorganshire Rifles. "I'm swcer tae gong into the ballroom and wi' folks I canna talk tae. Yell be feelin' that ~yer::;c1? "</p>
        <p>Captain Kinro.,7.s war, , quite certain Well 3 Fieher was looking .at .11Im out of the corner of his Pye. -.. . _</p>
        <p>"Just let's speak in Scotch, for I can speak the English no owre wed. I am no that auld farrant as to ken exactly the meaning of the words." Kinross laughed, and still he was awk- ward. "Yes, Miss Stewart, I agree with you it is best. Don't you think the grounds look very beautif-ul with those coloured lamps?" "Mr. Wells Fisher has asked me jest tae dander doun the banks. I'm nae ta'en up wi' these bonnie wallies. Certie, but if we were in Perthshire we'd ha' had the pipes skirlin' a' the nicht."</p>
        <p>Kinross gave A nervous glance at the girl. Why on earth was she talking like this? Lie could not understand a word she was say- ing. Surely she knew English enough not to use such outlandish phrases? " }lse ye forgotten,,.yer mither tongue?" asked Miss Stewart, a little reprovingly.</p>
        <p>This remark Kinross understood. " Well, you see, that is—We Kinrosses of Inverness—at least, my people—have lived mostly in England, and I'm sorry to say I'm not well acquainted with the brogue." "Ye wanna be for ca'ing it a brogue?" exclaimed Miss Stewart, apparentl-7 aghast.</p>
        <p>"No, no, I mean dialect."" "An' it's no a decalect either, I'm think. in'," she answered, haughtily; " it's a, guid language of its ai n. An' ye dinna speak it?" „</p>
        <p>"-AhT" he said, "but -a man. niay be as good a Scot as any, although he can't talk the jargon—er—that is, I mean the language." Mini Rtewart certainly grew two inches " W4.,,e1," she threw back, "I'm thinkin' that a Scot micht speak a pickle o' his am n tongue and be none the waur. Do yo no think so, Maister Wells Fisher?"</p>
        <p>"Eh, I beg pardon," said Wells Fisher, turning round. I didn't catch what you were talking about." Kinross knew differently, and would like to have told him so.</p>
        <p>"I was jest tellin' the Captain that a guid Sootch Captain aye speaks hia amn tongue." Certainly," replied Well Fisher, with eagerness; a man that doesn't speak his own language is only half a man. Now I suppose you two are just delighted to meet one another?"</p>
        <p>"Well, yqu see, Fisher," interrupted Kin- ross, "I am not acquainted with Scotch." "Why, my. clear chap, I thought you spoke it like a kilted native." "He doesna speak it at a'," said Miss Stewart.</p>
        <p>Just then Graham, the assistant some- thing-or-otlaer to the Lieutenant-Governor of the Punjab, brought along his sister to be, introduced to Miss Stewart.</p>
        <p>Kinross, happy at the release, edged away .and mingled with the throng. He could aee• Mrs. Allen Barker fluttering hither and 'thither in an ecstasy of delight, _</p>
        <p>,Certainly her dinrier-party lad been a sue- bees. And the soirée was being enjoyed by :everyone. The Highlands was one of the Prettiest houses in Simla, and to-night, radiant with brightly-dressed women and the verandah picked out with gleaming lan- terns, and the mighty snow-clad mountains bathed in clear moonlight as a background, it was assuredly one of the most charming places in India. An invitation from. Talbot to join a little card - party was declined curtly by Captain Kinross. He did not want to play cards. In- deed, his present desire was to slip off to his own bungalow. To have done so, however, at that early hour might have appeared dis- respectful to the hostess. He went down the steps leading to the plantation to smoke and to think. By. the ornamental lake the band of the Goorkhas 'Was playing, and along the shaded paths he encountered couples enjoying the beauty of the night and each other's company. He was a little annoyed at their presence. He had an idea he had made a fool of himself, and he shrank from the chaff of Wells Fisher that he would undoubtedly be subject to up at the United Service Club. There was a little leafy arbour close to the ornamental water, and here he sat down to finish his cigarette. The strains of the music and the laughter of the guests reached his ear—but he cared little for them. Suddenly he heard the footsteps of two people coming down the path on the other side .of the arbour. When he recognised that the man's voice was that of Wells Fisher he angrily .threw away his cigarette, and it fell with a sizz into the lake.</p>
        <p>He , ought, of course, to have risen and gone_ -away. He was not the man to play eavesdropper. And yet, somehow, he felt constrained to sit where he was and keep quiet. Poor chap," Wells Fisher was saying, "I was sorry for him. No doubt he would have been infinitely thankful if the earth had swallowed him up when you were talk- ing to him." He's just a gowk," replied his com- panion; and a pang went through the heart of the listening Captain as he recognised the voice of Miss Stewart.. What struck him as more tha.n remarkable was that,. although Miss Stewart used a Scotch word, she was now speaking in the plainest of plain English. "He is just a gowk," she, repeated; "lie is no more a Scot than you are, Mr. Fisher. I thought I remembered him, although he has changed a good deal since I Was a girl. His name is Donald Webster. His father was a merchants I think, in London, and I remember him coming down to Perthshire to visit an old aunt, Mrs: I.l;irray Kinross." " ! " remarked Wells Fisher; " ana that's where he got his name?" "Yes," the girl replied, "and where he got his money also. You see, he was an only .child, and, indeed, an only nephew, and when the aunt died she left him her money on .condition that he took the name of Mur- ray Kinross." The listening man could hear Wells Fisher chuckle quietly. He had no remem- brance of this girl, and yet , what she said was true enough. A plague on himself for ever having maspieraded as a Scot! He bit his lip with _anger. He wanted to , hear more of . what was being said on the other side of the arbour. The voices were lowered, so that only 'at intervals he caught a snatch of a sentence. He had a dread there was love-making not far from him. Wells Fisher was .1, irtdently speaking very seriously. "You gave me half a promisP., you know, once," he said, softly, but distinctly. " Ah, but it woe only half a one," and the girl laughed quietly. "Well, give me a full promise now." "You will regret it, any way." "I will take the risk. May I?"</p>
        <p>Captain. Kinross was not anxious to hear more. Ile moved on his seat, and the lovers on the other side of ' the arbour rose and walked arm in arm along one of the moonlit paths. KinrOss watched them, and chagrin was in his heart.</p>
        <p>Nobody quite knows why it was, but the next day Captain Murray. Kinross suddenly left Simla. Indeed, I might say nobody cared very much. For a new matter of gossip was in the public mind, the engagement of Miss Jessie Stewart, of Invertochty, Perthshire, Scotland, to Mr. Wells Fisher, of Simla, and formerly of Cadogan-square, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>TIIE LASS FROM INVERTOCIITY.</head>
        <p>TIIE LASS FROM INVERTOCIITY.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN FOSTER FRASER Author of "A Woman of Fire," "Round the World on a Wheel," "Vagabond Papers," "When Mary and I were Wed," kct</p>
        <p>"We are six weeks too soon," said Gra- ham, who was Assistant-something-or-other to the Lieutenant-Governor of the Punjab, "for till the Viceroy comes up from Calcutta, Simla is really the most impossible pla-ce lii this much over-rated India." "Well," remarked Talbot, who was, a col- lector down Agra way, "why don't you go back to Lahore? The flowers are still bloom- ing in the Mall, and I darcsay there's a man or two yet to be found in the club." "I'm sick of Lahore," said Graham. "I'm sick of the confounded Mall and the eternal evening drive. The club bores me. I'm sick of the fellows one meets there, and I daresay they are sick of me. I'm sick of India; I'm sick of Simla; indeed, I'm sick of every- thing." "My dear chap," said Talbot, "now that you have reached / finality in sickishness, I would suggest you see the Civil doctor and get him to make up a pill for your liver." "Simla,'' added Graham, with a blase in- difference, "is hopeless. Last year, when the Viceroy was here, there was some junketing and lively times, and something to really keep a fellow awake. But now "—and he shrugged his shoulders "why, enjoying Simla is like enjoying one's dinner with no wine. There isn't a woman in the place." "It's liver, Graham; it's liver that is troubling you," said Talbot. in despair. It was evening as the two men sauntered towards the United Service Club. There was a crisp chill in the air, and though the sun had set, the distant tips of the Himalayas were flushed in beautiful crimson. • A few of the bungalows were just, awakening into theft summer activity—.true, a little disconsolate after their winter sleep—and the hotels were shaking themselves into life ready for the rush of visitors about to set in from the" plains. The disconsolate mourning of Mr. Law- rence Graham was not exactly justified. The whirl of the Simla season had not arrived; but there were already many folks in the town of the mountains. _ _</p>
        <p>The club, instead of presenting gloom, was bright with many lights, and there was the chatter of many men. "Half the delight of Simla," a mouse-haired man sitting in a cor- ner was saying, "is paying one's respects to the ladies.'</p>
        <p>Tctlbot turned towards him. "I say, Kin- ross," he said, "there's a countrywoman of yours 'just arrived, and staying with Mrs. Allen. Barker. I haven't seen her, but no doubt she is of the conventional Scotch type. I claresay she has red hair and is freckled, and says 'I dinna ken.' You'll be able to exercise your native Doric upon her." A little flush touched the features of Donald Murray Kinross. He was thin and angular. Across the room you might say he was clean-shaven. Within a couple of yards' distance, however, you, would recoggise he had a moustache. Nature had given him a sallow skin, a pair of watery blue eyes, and. a manner that suggested that he was hewn from ice. Captain Murray Kinross, of the Glamor- ganshire Rifles, was the ptyle .in which he was gazetted. "I wish," said he, "you would-find some other means of diversion than stale witticisms about Scotland." "No malice, my dear chap," Talbot shouted back. "You're a Scot—though I am hanged if you look like one--and she's a Scot, and you can be her knight errant to your heart's desire. But, -Kinross, I've been won- dering why on earth you didn't join a Scotch regiment. Here are You, a valiant Hee- lander "—and Talbot drawled out the word to make the assembly laugh—" and an officer to a confounded Taffy regiment. Man, you're not doing justice to the land of heather and bannoek cakes." Kinross made no retort. He immersed him- self in a copy of the Civil and Military Gazette. Donald Murray Kinross was exceedingly proud of his name, and invariably wrote it in full. He had delivered a lecture on Burns to the Camg Mutual Improvement Society at Umballa. He had been in India now for eleven years. With wealthy girls, English, American, and Australian, he had danced- at mess romps. Tie had taken them in to sup- per at the soirees of the Viceroy's wife. Occa- sionally his heart glowed. But no lady's heart had ever glowed in return. Wells Fisher, the big, cheery lump of a civilian who made India his home from choice, the man who managed all the Simla private theatricals, called ladies of nineteen by their Christian names, and was hail-fel- low-well-met with every official from Peshawar to Calcutta, came dawdling into the club. His mere presence brought mirth to that rather jaded assembly. "Evening!" he said, with a nod, intended to go round the twenty men already asseipbled. "Hullo!" exclaimed Graham. "I have been looking for you the whole afternoon." "Well. I am glad you didn't find me. I have been most agreeably and delightfully employed, engaging the star of our next dramatic season." _ -</p>
        <p>"Is the star of othcr ladies in the de- scendant?"</p>
        <p>"I am afraid EIO. This girl is divine; she's charming. She'll make our Dramatic Society a perfect success, and the ladies of Simla jealous." "You're enthusiastic," said Talbot. "Talbot," said Wells Fisher, with mock gravity, "enthusiastic is not the word. Ah, Kinross, my dear Scotehman, you'll be de- lighted to meet her. She comes from your land, from a place called Inver—lnver-- Oh! I can't say it, but it's a place that sticks in your throat when you try to pronounce it." "Who is she?" asked Graham. "Miss Stewart." "Beautiful?"</p>
        <p>" Exquisitely !" " Rich? "</p>
        <p>"Well, she's the only daughter of the richest whisky distiller in Scotland, so one may presume that she is not poor." Does she say, 'I dinno, ken'?" asked Graham, sarcastically. Wells Fisher just tumbled back into a long, comfortable' chair, and broke into laughter.</p>
        <p>" Qui hi!" he shouted. When the Hindu 12oy arrived, he commanded that he be served with a large whisky peg. He drank his whisky, as he did everything in the world, with gusto. He was big and florid, and had a smile that was renowned throughout India. Captain Kinross looked over the top of the Civil and Military Gazette at him. So. the new arrival was Scotch! Evidently she was not freckled. The probability was that her hair was some other tint than red. The only daughter of ' a Scotch distiller—that meant wealth! Staying with Mrs. Allen Barker indi- cated she was young! Being at Simla sug- gested she would probably be a fiancee before the end of the season. He was Scotch—she was Scotch. He was poor—she was wealthy. Ergo, he would marry her! Wells Fisher knew not, as 'he sprawled hack, puffing clouds from his cheroot, that the mouse-haired and sallow-cheeked Captain Kinross was waiting fori him to move home- wards.</p>
        <p>It Wag just on seven o'clock, however, when he threw down the paper he had been glanc- ing at, pitched the fag end of his cheroot to the furthermost corner of the room, lay back, stretched out his arms, and yawned.</p>
        <p>"Heigh ho!" he said, " I think I'll be tod- dling." "Heigh ko!" repeated Captain Kinross, simulating a similar yawn, "and I think I'll he doing the same. Are you going down the road?"</p>
        <p>"Yes." said Wells Fisher; "we'll go to- ether."</p>
      </div>
