      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>GATHERING_ THE ONTARIO HARVEST.</head>
        <p>GATHERING_ THE ONTARIO HARVEST.</p>
        <p>The annual problem of gathering the crops will soon *face the Ontario farmer, who will have to deal with the same difficulties ho has met in previous years. During the period of harvest a small army of.rnen are required, and they are always difficult to get. Gener- ally speaking men who have not had experionce at farm work should go out earlier in the year, but it is different with the experienced man, and the harvest reason offers him one of the best opportunities to start work in Ontario. Wages are increased for the two or three months' engagement. During the winter, it is true, ha wilt not earn the same high rate ' of wages, but he will be on the ground ready to corn- I mence operations in the spring. The reports received by the London office of the Ontario Government indicate that there will be an unusually heavy demand for men for the harvest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>VIGMEN'S QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>VIGMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>An English and a Russian Duchess: A Contrast. BONNET STRINGS AGAIN.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Connaught's return to Canada in the autumn, to resume his duties as Governor-General of the Dominion, will hasten forward the arrangements for the wedding of Prince Arthur and the Duchess of Fife. The betrothal of the young couple has given great satisfaction to the parents of the expectant bride and bridegroom, as well as to their Majesties the King and Queen and Queen Alexandra. There is no more popular member of the Royal Family than Prince Arthur, who is some years the senior of his fiancee,and seemed at one time in danger of becoming a confirmed bachelor. His unaffected and engaging manners have enabled him to undertake with conspicuous success miesions to foreign and distant Courts, and in recent years he has represented the Roy al Family at many important public engagements. The youthfulness of the Duchess of Fife, and the retirement in which she has lived, have prevented her from being so well- known, but she is a charming Princess, and her marriage will open up for her a wider sphere of life. Besides being a duchess in her own right, she inherits a considerable share of the very large fortune left by her father, the late Duke of Fife. A ROYAL DUCHESS SISTERHOOD.</p>
        <p>The history of the Grand Duchess Serge of Russia, who is now on a visit to this country, i 4 both romantic and tragic. After the death of her mother, Princess Alice, she spent most of her girlhood at , Queen Victoria's Court, and married in 1884 the Grand Duke Serge of Russia. Ten years later her younger sister married the present Emperor of Russia. The Grind Duke Serge was killed by revolutionists close to his palace in Moscow and his widow, the Duchess Elizabeth, afterwards resolved to renounce the pleasures of the world for the remainder of her life. She has no children of her own, and after giving up palace life in Moscow, she gathered around her a com- munity of high-born ladies, named after the sisters of Lazarus, because the members•allopted the character- istics of Martha and Mary. Whilst being deeply religious, they trouble themselves about many things that concern those to whose welfare they devote their lives. The Grand Duchess and her assistants have taken the vows of poverty, celibacy and obedience for life. The other members take the vows for a year only; then they may renew them for three years, and finally for life. The Sisterhood founded by the Duchess Serge is quite a new one in Russia, and is on the same lines as our Sisters of Charity, who work among the poor. ' The Duchess maintains a home for disabled soldiers and consumptives, a hospital for women, and a free dispenbary. AN ADVENTUROUS HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Two English ladies have accomplished a daring and extraordinary feat in riding across the roof of the world, or that wonderful plateau that separates India from Eastern Thibet. During the journey they crossed mountain passes that have never .before been trodden by the feet of women. They visited monas- teriEs, met and conversed with saints, and pet formed the astonishing journey with the smallest caravans. Four men servants, eight ponies, and two yaks, com- pleted the party. They reached a height of 18,000 feet, and for a month were beyond the pale of civilisa- tion. At Leh the King received them and gave them provisions for the most difficult part of the journey. fhis involved the climbing over glaciers, fording dangerous rivers, exploring gorges, and sealing pre- cipitous mountains. Except for severe headaches both ladies enjoyed excellent health throughout the record trip, which they regarded iu the light of a holiday.</p>
        <p>t ho the white velvet sash over a coloured dress, effect is good when other white trimmings employed. COLOUR COMBINATIONS. e °f White and black as a combination has lost on 4111 its popularity; on the contrary, the most sacces; of dresses at this season's various functions have bee f this popular contrast, with which trimmings etre bright colour are often used, or black and Whits tot combined with some brilliant tint, as for ieloto yellow and vivid green dresses may be taletge• with more sober magpie trimmings. This r meet of three colours promises to be favoured uciiieat the remainder of the summer. Yellow in its Pretdok shades is still much worn, more especially by• jo women, and a long vogue may be protected for it. boi combination with black it is very handsome, if the proportions of each colour are about equal. WATCHES FOR THE FEET. • w tch One of the present fads of fashion is the L' floe carried on the foot, either on the ankle or on tile the cap. In the latter instance it is inserted bies lowest strap of a three-bar shoe, and in form rest or an oblong4buckle, and is made of platinum, gr, or silver. 'I he ankle watch differs in shape from trilB' tO , the usual time-keeper, as it must conforlanov the curves of the instep, on which it rests. be in the case of the near-sighted would there the any difficulty in ascertaining the hour, °ewe numeral twelve on the dial faces the wearer. Per "hoe it is not a matter for surprise that so far there been no brisk demand for these novelties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>RETURN OF BONNET STRINGS.</head>
        <p>THE RETURN OF BONNET STRINGS.</p>
        <p>Old and even obsolete fashions are constantly being revived, for change is the keynote of modes. But before designers and fashionmongers launch the styles of former generations they adopt them to the present prevailing ideas. The bonnet string is a case in point. No woman of to-day would muffle her jaws in wide strings, tied in a big bow under the chin, as did the women of the early Victorian period. Hence the bonnet string that is revived every two or three years, and has quite caught on this season, is not a string at all (except when it consists of two lengths of ribbon velvet, and is tied at the side), but an attenuated strap of the same description, attached to the brim of the hat on either side and passed under the chin, which is left quite free and merely outlined. Quite a pretty effect is obtained when the strings are closed by means of a medium-sized rose, worn according to taste, on the right or left side, just below the ear. Tbis mode is particularly becoming when worn with a hat of white tulle or lace. THE' VOGUE FOR BLACK VELVET.</p>
        <p>It is useless to attempt to account for the vagaries and inconsistencies of Dame Fashion, or one would be tempted to seek a lesson for the extraordinary vogue for black vzlvet as a trimming for gowns, and its use in millinery during the hottest months of the year. Its association, too, with toilettes of the thinnest and flimsiest of white muslins sounds incongruous, yet it is in fact a very happy combination when the velvet is employed for the high broad waist belt, and the full Bishop muslin sleeves are tied at the wrist with narrow velvet ribbon. Braces of the same material are sometimes seen on 'the corsages of such dresses. For revers, sashes and millinery, no fabric is as Itniversally used as black velvet. Coloured silk sashes are increasingly used with white toilettes to which they act as a foil. Another seeming incongruity is</p>
        <p>2 Eggs,</p>
        <p>2 ozs. Butter. 3 ozs. Flour, 3 ozs. Sugar,</p>
        <p>"There! I knew you would ask where those little pink cakes came from. I'm so glad you like them. I made them myself with Bird's Blanc-mange Powder. Oh yes! with my recipe the daintiest afternoon tea cakes can be made with Bird's Blanc-mange Powder. These are called Duchess Buns.' A few Glace or Crystalised Cherries. 1 saltspoonful Salt, 2 tablespoonfuls Milk, . 1 teaspoonful Bird's Baking Powder. and 1 packet of BIRD'S BLANC-MANGE Powder (Raspberry Flavour).</p>
        <p>Put the butter and sugar in a basin and set on the range to melt. Next beat to a cream; add the eggs one at a time, beating up well after each, then the milk. -</p>
        <p>• " ' _ _ . inge, Mix thoroughly together the flour, Bird's Baking Powder, Bird's Blanc-lq,_ „ .f, e' Powder, and the cherries previously cut into small pieces: add this dry mixture degrees to the liquid ingredients, beating the whole to a perfectly smooth batt . Have ready sufficient small Bun tins which have been well buttered; half fill eacilry. of these with the batter, placing a piece of cherry on the top, and bake inunediate about 10 minutes in a hot quick oven.</p>
