      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>SECONDARY EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>SECONDARY EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Committee's Regulatiorl et Ensure Longer Period of Attendalle</p>
        <p>al • d With the object of securing a longer peter attendance at the county secondary schools OLIO; taut amendment of the secondary school reg.tll6' tio.; was on Monday adopted by the Lancashire Bo°,-fit Committee at their meeting at Preston. This fuldlaP:, for an undertaking to be given by parents or Oer °I; that pupils who are twelve years of age or joining a secondary school shall attend for lieVd at ' three years, and that pupils who are admitt „AO younger age shall not leave until they are IWO ts old. Sir Henry Hibbert, M.P., who presided. ;00, nuteber of average periods of attendance 11110,r;". secondary schools in the administrative aree;d 00:4 were four schools, he said, where boys remain gusi° e two years. In one school boys over twelve Oro, tO. two years, one month; in another two Ytt ' months; two schools, two years, 'five moot.t"o'yst; schools, two years, six months; two schoolo, eight months ,• three schools, two years, elevett:ool3o°J two schools, three years, two months ; and Olidslrla three years, nine months. The average for of slightly higher. Everyone with the education 0 tbee present generation at heart would agreed Ito bhort periods such' as he bad instsocAel not sufficient for the education rTss&amp; 11—ing benefit the children as it should. In a speecii_,,"iy o—of he made bold to remark that if children were ste to stay in the secondary schools up to about,:nold 014 fourteen it was infinitely preferable that they fo". cb°' continued their education in the elemeptarY diVe%Pevi The cost of secondary education was exceedlogwoste sive, and undoubtedly tbete was a very great 000ernou, public money so far as ,Lancashite WBoof unless children stayed longer. ardi"sbat A resolution was passed that parents or gAtifteko tr, pupils joiniA, secondary schools must tinueof joioAr:' the pupils shall,if twelve or over at the tioo3,,,ree y05,r,71; remain at the school a minimum period of in vitu" or, if under twelve until the end of the ter"' the pupil attains the age of 15.</p>
