      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 26, 1913.</p>
        <p>BOWLING. WITH THE WOODS.</p>
        <p>Successful Start of Local Liberal</p>
        <p>Handicap.</p>
        <p>CLUB SEMI-FINALS.</p>
        <p>The great event last Saturday in local bowling circles was the start of the gigantic handicap promoted by the Warrington Liberal Association. The Bank Quay Green was in fine condition and over 160 interesting games were played before a large attend- ance. The third round will be entered upon next Saturday, and we publish the draw in another column. The class of bowling witnessed last Saturday was much above the average.</p>
        <p>The semi-final games in the Warrington and District .Llssociation' club championship competition resulted in the Cemetery Hotel and Senior Conserva- tives qualifying for the final. The semi-final in the Warrington Works Association competition was reached, the games in the fourth round played on Saturday b6ing full of exciting incidents.</p>
        <p>Cemetery Hotel had a big handful to deal with in Bull's Head, for they only won by six points on the home and home games. At the Cemetery Hotel the homesters gained an advantage of 72 points, winning on all but two games. The only losing man on the Bull's Head side who put up anything like a score was A. Gleave, who was only cue point behind G. Ball, sen. After peeling at eight Gleave lost the jack and was 9-13 in arrears at the 17th end. Play- ing steadily, he ran to 15-13, only to be caught up again at the 23rd end. The game was 19 each at the 2Sth end, and after another run, Gleave only wanted one poiut to win. Ball secured a double and was therefore victorious.</p>
        <p>J. Melbourne (Cemetery) had hard lines in his game with A. Regan, for, after leading 16-13 at the 21st end, he was beaten by two chalks. Regan led 13-5 at the 11th end before he could record another point. Melbourne then lead 17-13, Regan having had eight blank ends. The players peeled at 18 and again at 19. Amidst great excitement Regan landed his winning double.</p>
        <p>On their own green the Bull's Head men won by sixty-six points, thus reducing the majority of the Cemetery team to one of six points. Only three of the Cemetery men were successful in winning, the others putting up a fairly good fight to keep the majority on their side. One ~f the best games in the match was that between T. Quarrie and A. Gleave Head). The scores were pretty even all through the game, and at the 26th end the players peeled with 17. At the 29th end the score was 19 each. Gleave sent in a good wood and lauded a single, only to be caught up on the next length by Quarrie, who handled the jack and his woods in capital fashion and scored the winning single on the 32nd end.</p>
        <p>Two of the Cemetery men put up a very poor show, W. Bridge and W. J. Woods only recording eight and four points respectively against J. Cooper and J. W. Jolley. It was only on the last few that J. Isherwood (Bull's Read) triumphed over W. Barton (Cemetery). At the 20th end the players had peeled with 14 and again at the 26th end the score was 17 each. Barton, who had played exceedingly well, only recorded another single to Isherwood's winning three.</p>
        <p>At Hood-lane the Conservatives were having a very hard time of it at the hands of Crcsfield's No. 2. The Sankey-street team finally won by eight points, a total win of 114 on the home and home matches. The games were of a highly interesting character and Crosfield's came very near to victory. The Conservatives gained their winning points on the last four men, Crosfield's having been leading prior to this. R. F. Anderton (Conservatives) had no easy task in beating E. Warburton by 21-19. At the 25th end the score was 16 each. Warburton could not find his land and at the 20th length Anderton led 20-17. Warburton played a plucky game to the finish and was only two points behind when Anderton recorded his final single.</p>
        <p>F. Heesom (Conservatives) ought to have won against W. Howard, for at the 24th end he led 20-14. Howard, however, got on a good length and playing the jack in a skilful manner had a good run of seven to game.</p>
        <p>The final, which should take place on August 23rd, will be a most exciting one, as the teams appear to be fairly well matched.</p>
        <p>Some good matches will be witnessed to-day on the Horse and Jockey Green,when the following teams will meet in the semi-final of the Warrington Works Association competition at 2-30 p.m. : County Asylum v. St. Helens Cable No. 1. livlands' No. 1 v.ll. A. Naylors. Referee, Mr. J. Willett.</p>
        <p>The entries for the annual main close to-day, and the final of the competition will take place on August 9th.</p>
        <p>The games last Saturday were witnessed by a large number of spectators. The green was in excellent condition and the bowling was of a very high standard. The tightest game was that in which County Asylum beat Orcefield's No. 1 by three points. The Asylum team had to concede five points to taeir opponents, and it took them all their time to win by a trio of points.</p>
        <p>The Cable team bad little difficulty in disposing of the Howley Tannery team by 28 points; and Rylands had by far the best 9f matters against Richmond's, Academy-street, to win by 13 points, handicapped 39 poihts behind. Naylor's gave the P. and K. Wheel Stlop men a severe trounciug, entering the semi-final with a win of 40 points.</p>
        <p>A good attendance is expected to-day, for the teams competing are about the best in the competition. It would be hazardous for anyone to prophesy as to the ultimate finalists, as some startling surprises will probably accrue.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling handicap for the " Steel" Challenge Cup in connection with the " Constitution " Lodge of the 1.0.0. F. produced a fine array of entries, and a series of keen games. Mr. T. Watson is to be congratulated on securing the much-coveted trophy, although in the final he had a very hard task in din- posing of J. Lawton, who ran him to a close finish.</p>
        <p>The redoubtable Joe Gleave came very near winning the Individual Merit Competition in the Manchester section of the Lancashire County Amateur Associa- tion. In the seventh round he accounted for F. Gardner (Roebuck) by 21-12, but in the semi-final be met more than his match in C. P. Hough, who scraped home by a couple of chalks. W. Gaskell (Man and Scythe) was the winner of the competition.</p>
        <p>THUMB BIAS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>BRITISH CROWN GREEN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>BRITISH CROWN GREEN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Inter-County Championship.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Lancashire met Staffordshire and on the two games one at Birkdale and the other at New Hampton, Staffordshire, won by nine points. Details:—</p>
