      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Friendly Matches.</head>
        <p>Friendly Matches.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS v. RYLANDS BROS. A bowling match, between teams representing Messrs. Rylands Bros. and the Warrington branch of the Com- mercial Travellers' Association, was played at the Sankey Chapel House Hotel, on Saturday, and after a keen struggle resulted in a win for Rylands Bros. Scores :</p>
        <p>---CO-n-aniercial Travellers. Ryland s Bros. F Plumpton 6l 7 S Payne , 111,7 J Holliday T Critcbley W L Walker IIJ W Barker G Box E Higson C Hibbert ll 1 12 L Bate J Smith HO Williams J Harvey F Webster lO 1 W Webb J Marshall lOl 11 G Bowmen f13 R Lowe ll i 0 Richardson 5 ( 7 W G Hayes</p>
        <p>125 67 Majority for Rylands Bro. 7.</p>
        <p>119 81</p>
        <p>SEE ALSO ON PAGE 5.</p>
        <p>COMPULSORY ISOLATION OF CONSUMPTIVES. Mr. John Burns, the president of the Local Govern- ment Board, replies in Tuesday's Parliamentary papers to a question whether any local authorities have taken steps to secure the compulsory isolation of tuberculosis cases in a stare of high infectivity. Mr Burns says :—" In the session of 1911 the Corporation of St. Helens obtained by means of a local Act certain special powers in regard to the compulsory isolation of persons suffering from pulmonary tuberculosis. Similar powers were refused in the case of Sheffield in the session of 1912, and in the cases of Derby and Southport in the present session. Similar powers have been obtained in the present session by Liverpool, East Ham, and Bradford. The medical officer of health of St. Helens considers that the powers have proved useful, but he reports that during 1912 in only one cane was it necessary to make an application to the court for an order authorising compulsory removal."</p>
      </div>
