        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME. An exceent well-varied programme has been sub mitted at the Hippodrome this week, and, judging by the large crowds which assemble twice nightly the turns are popular with everyone. A Savage Encounter" is a laughable prehistoric absurdity pre- sented by Fred Edwards and Miss May Yates. Edwards is a very clever comedian, and as a savage causes roars of laughter. Miss Yates plays her part well and captivates the audierce by her vivaciousness. Wood, Wells and Wilkins are a vocal comedy trio of great ability. In addition to provoking much mirth, they also sing exceedingly well, their voices blending most harmoniously. Orpheus has proved himself capable of manipulating almost any kind of wind instrument. His coach horn specialities are particu- larly entertaining. J. H. Scotland is a basso vocalist, who is most vociferously received fur a refined "turn." His " Wee little housie by the sea " number was most expressively rendered and he was equally successful in his dialect items. " The ragtime village" is a laughable burlesque by J. P. Lieg, an original and up-to-date entertainer. Smart parodies on rag- time songs are rendered in a manner which cannot fail to please. Crawford and Johnson are up-to-date vocalists and dancers and play the saxophone with taste and skill. Leolin is a novelty juggler with an abundance of really smart tricks, lie also swings the clubs in a remarkable way. Walter Saxby, eccentric comedian, also contributes to a good and all- round programme. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. The dramatic comedy •` 1,000 to one against," an exciting adventure in the life of Dick Rutherford, cracksman, will be presented by H. J. Corner and Leo Ware's powerful company of sketch artistes. Other turns will be Dan Whiteley, the irresponsible, eccentric funniosity ; Berg Bros., trick cyclists ; Mertz and Whittle, comedians; LawieY and East, in musical comedietta, Lilian Carter, soporano ; Mike Gray, eccentric juggler; and the original Three Leonas. THEATRE ROYAL. The pictures this week are of a very Ligh standard, and for to-night the principal films will be " The:shop girl" and "The bath chair," t‘vo excellent subjects. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next week "The opium smoker," showing the evils of opium smoking,will be the chief picture and for the entire week "1812," the dramatic story of the" beginning of the end" of the dic'atorship of Napoleon will be shown. The great military drama "Under the red cross" will be projected in two parts on Thursday, Friday and Saturday. THE PICTURE HOUSE. Good business is the rule at the new Picture House in Winmarleigh-street, under the able direction of Mr. C. L. .Ashton, and everything possible is done to ensure the comfort of patrons. A fine series of films which include travel, comedy and drama ,1 are being shown to-day. Special mention should be made of the star picture "Dublin Dan," a detective story, teeming with excitement. Next week two fine feature films will be shown :—Monday, "Theresa the Adventuress " ; Thursday, "'rho Lion Hunters." The programme will also include "His Mother's Son," " Professor's Daughter," "Genoa," "Max and th e Pankake " and "Just Gold."</p>
