        <p>DRINK AND CRIME. Ex-Sergeant's Striking Address at Warrington Temperance Meeting. The Warrington Citizens' Temperence League is con tinuing its campaign in favour of temperance reform and on Wednesday evening another successful open-air meeting was held at the corner of Thelwall-lane, Latchford. Mr. J. E. Pardoe presided. He said the subject they were there to discuss was very much neglected in that quarter of the town and he thought every opportunity should he taken of bringing the temperance question before the people. (Applause.) Ex-Police Sergeant Quinton, (of Salford) gave a very interesting address which embodied many of the experiences the speaker had had whilst he was a member of the force. Deal- ing with the effect strong drink had on the child life of the country, he said that steps had been taken to Prevent children of tender years from going into public- houses. After referring to the noble work done by the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, the speaker said that statistics went to prove that while the parents should have spent the money on the little ones, they bad spent it on something re h'ch did not satisfy but createu a desire for more. Mr. Parr in his report in connection with the N.S.P.C.C. for last year stated that the number of children affected through the neglect of the parents was 159,407 and cut of those 1,143 died. Mr. Parr in his report stated that it Was no exaggeration to say that at least nine- tenths of thoes cases were ca ised through the excessive drinking of the parents. (Hear bear.) The ex-sergeant said he had seen many instances of crime and wrong doing which had been perpetrated through excessive drinking. He had known a that class inspector in the police force who had come to be an ordinary policeman. He had continued his drinking habits and was dismissed from the force without a character and died on the streets of Manchester. Drink was the father of all crimes, for a man would commit an offence in drink that he would not otherwise. The so-called mote ate drinkers were the worst enemies stpractu'le, of the temperance cause A person who had the will to take a drink and leave it, was the most liable to cause others to follow his example and they, per Laps rot being of strong will, fell to the temptation aed ruint aerdths.er ldfirtertiorunse, neoyf becamedrink m w ecroe laid o pdtn on would find work for all people in the countr h tint - y o were del irons of working. (Applause). ,</p>
